  {
    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_344",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAtCoder Inc. sells T-shirts with its logo.\nYou are given Takahashi's schedule for N days as a string S of length N consisting of 0, 1, and 2.\nSpecifically, for an integer i satisfying 1\\leq i\\leq N,\n\n- if the i-th character of S is 0, he has no plan scheduled for the i-th day;\n- if the i-th character of S is 1, he plans to go out for a meal on the i-th day;\n- if the i-th character of S is 2, he plans to attend a competitive programming event on the i-th day.\n\nTakahashi has M plain T-shirts, all washed and ready to wear just before the first day.\nIn addition, to be able to satisfy the following conditions, he will buy several AtCoder logo T-shirts.\n\n- On days he goes out for a meal, he will wear a plain or logo T-shirt.\n- On days he attends a competitive programming event, he will wear a logo T-shirt.\n- On days with no plans, he will not wear any T-shirts. Also, he will wash all T-shirts worn at that point. He can wear them again from the next day onwards.\n- Once he wears a T-shirt, he cannot wear it again until he washes it.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of T-shirts he needs to buy to be able to wear appropriate T-shirts on all scheduled days during the N days. If he does not need to buy new T-shirts, print 0.\nAssume that the purchased T-shirts are also washed and ready to use just before the first day.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nS\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the minimum number of T-shirts Takahashi needs to buy to be able to satisfy the conditions in the problem statement.\nIf he does not need to buy new T-shirts, print 0.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq M\\leq N\\leq 1000\n- S is a string of length N consisting of 0, 1, and 2.\n- N and M are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 1\n112022\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n\nIf Takahashi buys two logo T-shirts, he can wear T-shirts as follows:\n\n- On the first day, he wears a logo T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the second day, he wears a plain T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the third day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the fourth day, he has no plans, so he washes all the worn T-shirts. This allows him to reuse the T-shirts worn on the first, second, and third days.\n- On the fifth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the sixth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n\nIf he buys one or fewer logo T-shirts, he cannot use T-shirts to meet the conditions no matter what. Hence, print 2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 1\n222\n\nSample Output 2\n\n3\n\nSample Input 3\n\n2 1\n01\n\nSample Output 3\n\n0\n\nHe does not need to buy new T-shirts.",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every day Takahashi either\n\n* wears **no** T‑shirt (`S[i] = '0'`);\n* wears a **plain** or a **logo** T‑shirt (`S[i] = '1'`);\n* must wear a **logo** T‑shirt (`S[i] = '2'`).\n\nAll T‑shirts (plain and bought logo ones) are clean before day 1.\nThe only moment when a worn T‑shirt becomes clean again is a day with `0`.\nTherefore the whole period is split by the `0`‑days into *segments* of\nconsecutive non‑zero days.  \nInside one segment a T‑shirt can be used **at most once**, but after the\nsegment finishes (the next `0`) every T‑shirt is clean again and can be used\nin the following segments.\n\nConsequences  \n\n* The same set of T‑shirts must be sufficient for **every** segment.\n* For a fixed segment we only have to know  \n  `p` – number of `'1'` days (meal)  \n  `c` – number of `'2'` days (competition).\n\nPlain T‑shirts can be used only on `'1'` days, at most `M` of them in a\nsegment (because we own exactly `M` plains).  \nAll `c` competition days need a logo T‑shirt.\nIf `p > M`, the remaining `p‑M` meal days also need a logo T‑shirt.\n\nHence the minimal number of logo T‑shirts that makes this segment feasible is  \n\n```\nneed = c + max(0, p - M)\n```\n\nThe whole schedule is feasible iff we own at least the **maximum** `need`\namong all segments.\n\nThe answer is that maximum, or `0` if it is `0`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread N, M, S\nmax_need = 0\np = c = 0                 # counters for the current segment\n\nfor each character ch in S:\n    if ch == '0':\n        max_need = max(max_need, c + max(0, p - M))\n        p = c = 0         # start a new segment\n    else if ch == '1':\n        p += 1\n    else:                 # ch == '2'\n        c += 1\n\n# last segment (if S does not end with '0')\nmax_need = max(max_need, c + max(0, p - M))\n\nprint(max_need)           # 0 is printed automatically when max_need == 0\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the minimal number of logo T‑shirts\nrequired.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor a fixed segment with `p` meal days and `c` competition days,\nany feasible assignment needs at least `c + max(0, p‑M)` logo T‑shirts.\n\n**Proof.**  \n* All `c` competition days must use a logo T‑shirt → at least `c` logos.  \n* At most `M` plain T‑shirts are available in the segment, therefore at most\n  `M` of the `p` meal days can use a plain shirt.\n  The remaining `p‑M` (if positive) meal days also need a logo.\n  Hence at least `max(0, p‑M)` additional logos are necessary.\n\nSumming both parts gives the claimed lower bound. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nIf Takahashi owns `L` logo T‑shirts with  \n\n```\nL ≥ c + max(0, p - M)\n```\n\nfor a segment, then the segment can be scheduled.\n\n**Proof.**  \n* Use a plain shirt on any `min(p, M)` meal days (possible because `M` plain\n  shirts exist and each is used at most once).  \n* The remaining `p - min(p, M) = max(0, p‑M)` meal days and all `c` competition\n  days (total `c + max(0, p‑M) ≤ L`) are assigned distinct logo shirts.\nAll assignments respect the “once per segment” rule, therefore the segment\nis feasible. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nLet  \n\n```\nL* = max over all segments ( c + max(0, p - M) )\n```\n\nThen `L*` logo T‑shirts are sufficient for the whole schedule.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy definition, for each segment its own required number of logos does not\nexceed `L*`. Applying Lemma&nbsp;2 segment by segment shows that every segment\ncan be scheduled using the same pool of `L*` logo shirts (they are washed\nbetween segments). Consequently the whole period is feasible. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nNo number `< L*` of logo T‑shirts can make the schedule feasible.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider a segment that attains the maximum, i.e. for which  \n\n```\nc + max(0, p - M) = L*\n```\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 this segment alone needs at least `L*` logo shirts.\nThus any smaller amount is insufficient. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm outputs the minimal possible number of logo T‑shirts Takahashi\nhas to buy.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe algorithm computes `max_need = L*` as defined above.\n*By Lemma&nbsp;3* `L*` shirts are enough, so the algorithm never\noverestimates the necessary amount.\n*By Lemma&nbsp;4* any smaller number fails, so the algorithm never\nunderestimates.\nTherefore `max_need` is exactly the minimal required number, which the\nalgorithm prints. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\n`N ≤ 1000`.  \nThe algorithm scans the string once, performing O(1) work per character.\n\n*Time*: `O(N)`  \n*Memory*: `O(1)` (only a few integer variables)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    N = int(data[0])\n    M = int(data[1])\n    S = data[2]\n\n    max_need = 0          # maximal logos required among all segments\n    p = c = 0             # counters for the current segment\n\n    for ch in S:\n        if ch == '0':\n            # finish current segment\n            need = c + max(0, p - M)\n            if need > max_need:\n                max_need = need\n            p = c = 0\n        elif ch == '1':\n            p += 1\n        else:            # ch == '2'\n            c += 1\n\n    # last segment (if any)\n    need = c + max(0, p - M)\n    if need > max_need:\n        max_need = need\n\n    print(max_need)      # 0 is printed automatically when no logo is needed\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms\nto the required input‑output format.",
