 1\n\n2\n\nIf Takahashi buys two logo T-shirts, he can wear T-shirts as follows:\n\n- On the first day, he wears a logo T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the second day, he wears a plain T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the third day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the fourth day, he has no plans, so he washes all the worn T-shirts. This allows him to reuse the T-shirts worn on the first, second, and third days.\n- On the fifth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the sixth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n\nIf he buys one or fewer logo T-shirts, he cannot use T-shirts to meet the conditions no matter what. Hence, print 2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 1\n222\n\nSample Output 2\n\n3\n\nSample Input 3\n\n2 1\n01\n\nSample Output 3\n\n0\n\nHe does not need to buy new T-shirts.",
    "prediction": "xiaomi/mimo-v2.5",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    M = int(data[1])\n    S = data[2].strip()\n    \n    # We simulate the days in blocks separated by wash days (0s)\n    blocks = []\n    current_block = []\n    for ch in S:\n        if ch == '0':\n            if current_block:\n                blocks.append(current_block)\n                current_block = []\n            # Wash day: reset, no need to record anything\n        else:\n            current_block.append(ch)\n    if current_block:\n        blocks.append(current_block)\n    \n    # If no days requiring shirts, answer is 0\n    if not blocks:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    \n    # For each block, we need to see how many logo T-shirts are needed in that block,\n    # given that we have M plain shirts available.\n    # Plain shirts can be reused within the block (since no wash until block end),\n    # but logo shirts are only washed at wash days (end of block).\n    \n    total_logo_needed = 0\n    for block in blocks:\n        # Count '1's and '2's in this block\n        ones = block.count('1')\n        twos = block.count('2')\n        \n        # Plain shirts can cover up to M of the '1's.\n        # Remaining '1's need logo shirts if any logo shirts are available,\n        # otherwise we need more logo shirts.\n        # But note: logo shirts can be used for both '1' and '2'.\n        # We want to minimize total logo shirts bought overall (across all blocks)\n        # but since logo shirts can be reused after wash days, we need to consider\n        # the maximum requirement in any single block? Actually, careful:\n        # The logo shirts bought are available from day 1 and washed on 0 days.\n        # So they can be reused across blocks.\n        # Therefore, we need to ensure that in each block, we have enough\n        # logo shirts to cover the days that cannot be covered by plain shirts.\n        # Let x = number of logo shirts we buy.\n        # In each block, we can use up to x logo shirts (since they are all available\n        # at the start of the block, assuming we washed them all at the previous 0 day).\n        # But note: plain shirts are also washed on 0 days, so they are available\n        # at the start of each block too.\n        # So in each block, we have M plain and x logo shirts available.\n        # We need to cover ones and twos.\n        # Twos must be covered by logo.\n        # Ones can be covered by plain or logo.\n        # So we need: x >= twos  (for this block)\n        # and also, if ones > M, then we need additional logo shirts for the extra ones:\n        # x >= twos + max(0, ones - M)\n        # Therefore, for this block, minimum x required is max(twos, twos + max(0, ones - M))\n        # = twos + max(0, ones - M)\n        # So total logo shirts needed is max over blocks of (twos + max(0, ones - M))\n        \n        required_in_block = twos + max(0, ones - M)\n        total_logo_needed = max(total_logo_needed, required_in_block)\n    \n    print(total_logo_needed)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
