    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given an undirected graph G with N vertices and M edges.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M, the i-th edge is an undirected edge connecting vertices u_i and v_i.\nA graph with N vertices is called good if the following condition holds for all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K:\n\n- there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i in G.\n\nThe given graph G is good.\nYou are given Q independent questions. Answer all of them.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th question is as follows.\n\n- Is the graph G^{{(i)}} obtained by adding an undirected edge connecting vertices p_i and q_i to the given graph G good?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nu_1 v_1\r\nu_2 v_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nu_M v_M\r\nK\r\nx_1 y_1\r\nx_2 y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nx_K y_K\r\nQ\r\np_1 q_1\r\np_2 q_2\r\n\\vdots\r\np_Q q_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th line should contain the answer to the i-th question: Yes if the graph G^{{(i)}} is good, and No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 0 \\leq M \\leq 2 \\times10^5\n- 1 \\leq u_i, v_i \\leq N\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq x_i, y_i \\leq N\n- x_i \\neq y_i\n- i \\neq j \\implies \\lbrace x_i, y_i \\rbrace \\neq \\lbrace x_j, y_j \\rbrace\n- For all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K, there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i.\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i, q_i \\leq N\n- p_i \\neq q_i\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 3\r\n3 1\r\n5 4\r\n5 5\r\n3\r\n1 5\r\n2 6\r\n4 3\r\n4\r\n2 5\r\n2 6\r\n5 6\r\n5 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\nNo\r\nNo\r\nYes\r\nYes\r\n\n\n- For the first question, the graph G^{{(1)}} is not good because it has a path 1 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 5 connecting vertices x_1 = 1 and y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor the original graph `G`\n\n* `G` is *good* – for every forbidden pair `(x_i , y_i)` the two vertices are **not**\n  connected by a path.\n* Therefore `x_i` and `y_i` belong to **different connected components** of `G`.\n\nWhen we add one new edge `(p , q)`\n\n* only the two components that contain `p` and `q` can become one component.\n* All other components stay unchanged.\n\nConsequently a forbidden pair becomes connected **iff**\nits two vertices lie exactly in those two components that are merged by the new edge.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Pre‑processing  \n\n*Build the components of the original graph.*\n\n```\nuse a Disjoint Set Union (DSU)\nfor every original edge (u , v):   union(u , v)\n```\n\nAfter all unions `root(v)` is the id of the component of `v`.\n\n*Map every forbidden pair to the unordered pair of component ids.*\n\n```\nfor each (x , y):\n        a = root(x)          # component of x\n        b = root(y)          # component of y      (a != b, because G is good)\n        key = (min(a,b), max(a,b))\n        forbidden_set.add(key)\n```\n\n`forbidden_set` contains at most `K ≤ 2·10^5` elements, look‑ups are `O(1)`.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Answer a query `(p , q)`\n\n```\nc1 = root(p)\nc2 = root(q)\n\nif c1 == c2:                # edge stays inside one component\n        answer = \"Yes\"\nelse:\n        key = (min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2))\n        if key in forbidden_set:\n                answer = \"No\"          # merges a forbidden pair\n        else:\n                answer = \"Yes\"\n```\n\nEach query is answered in `O(α(N))` time for the two `find` operations\n(`α` – inverse Ackermann, practically constant).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints *Yes* exactly for those queries that keep the\ngraph good.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nIn the original graph `G` each forbidden pair `(x_i , y_i)` belongs to two\ndifferent connected components.\n\n**Proof.** The statement of the problem says that `G` is good, i.e. there is **no**\npath between `x_i` and `y_i`. In an undirected graph vertices are in the same\nconnected component **iff** a path exists between them. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nAfter adding a single edge `(p , q)` the only vertices that become newly\nconnected are those that lie in the component of `p` together with those that\nlie in the component of `q`.\n\n**Proof.** Adding one undirected edge can only join the two previously\ndisconnected components that contain its endpoints. No other component obtains a\nnew edge, therefore any path that uses the new edge must start in the first\ncomponent and end in the second one. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nA forbidden pair `(x , y)` becomes connected after adding edge `(p , q)`\n*iff* `{root(x), root(y)} = {root(p), root(q)}`.\n\n**Proof.**  \n*If* the unordered component sets are equal, by Lemma&nbsp;2 the two components\nmerge, therefore `x` and `y` are now in the same component – they are connected.\n\n*Only‑if*: assume the pair becomes connected.\nBefore the addition they were in different components (Lemma&nbsp;1).  \nThe only way two previously different components can become one is the merge\ndescribed in Lemma&nbsp;2, thus the two components containing `x` and `y`\nmust be exactly the two components of `p` and `q`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nFor a query `(p , q)` the algorithm outputs **No**  ⇔  there exists a forbidden\npair that becomes connected after adding the edge.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe algorithm outputs **No** exactly when `c1 ≠ c2` and the unordered pair\n`(min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2))` is stored in `forbidden_set`.  \n`forbidden_set` contains precisely all unordered component pairs of the\noriginal forbidden pairs (construction step).  \nHence **No** is printed precisely when there is a forbidden pair whose component\npair equals `{c1,c2}`, i.e. when the condition of Lemma&nbsp;3 holds. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nFor every query the algorithm prints **Yes** if and only if the graph obtained\nby adding the queried edge remains good.\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*If* the algorithm prints **Yes**.  \nTwo cases:\n\n* `c1 == c2` – the edge is inside one component, no new connections appear,\n  therefore all forbidden pairs stay disconnected → graph stays good.\n* `c1 != c2` and the unordered pair is **not** in `forbidden_set`.  \n  By Lemma&nbsp;4 no forbidden pair becomes connected, so the graph stays good.\n\n*Only‑if* the resulting graph is good.  \nIf the graph stays good, no forbidden pair becomes connected.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 there is no forbidden pair with component set `{c1,c2}`.\nConsequently the unordered pair `{c1,c2}` is absent from `forbidden_set`,\nso the algorithm does not output **No**; it outputs **Yes**. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Building DSU* : `O(N + M α(N))`  \n*Processing forbidden pairs* : `O(K α(N))` (only `find`)  \n*Answering Q queries* : `O(Q α(N))`\n\nOverall `O((N+M+K+Q) α(N))`, well below the limits.  \nMemory usage:\n\n* DSU parent & size arrays – `O(N)`\n* Set of forbidden component pairs – `O(K)`\n* Input storage – `O(N+M+K+Q)` (or streamed)\n\nAll fit into the allowed memory.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nsys.setrecursionlimit(1 << 25)\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n\n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n\n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    size = [1] * (N + 1)\n\n    def find(x: int) -> int:\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n\n    def union(a: int, b: int) -> None:\n        a = find(a)\n        b = find(b)\n        if a == b:\n            return\n        if size[a] < size[b]:\n            a, b = b, a\n        parent[b] = a\n        size[a] += size[b]\n\n    # original edges\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        union(u, v)\n\n    K = int(next(it))\n    forbidden_set = set()\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        cx = find(x)\n        cy = find(y)\n        if cx == cy:\n            # shouldn't happen because input guarantees G is good,\n            # but we ignore just in case\n            continue\n        if cx > cy:\n            cx, cy = cy, cx\n        forbidden_set.add((cx, cy))\n\n    Q = int(next(it))\n    out_lines = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q = int(next(it))\n        cp = find(p)\n        cq = find(q)\n        if cp == cq:\n            out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            if cp > cq:\n                cp, cq = cq, cp\n            out_lines.append(\"No\" if (cp, cq) in forbidden_set else \"Yes\")\n\n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms\nto the required input‑output format.",
