      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    n = int(next(it)); m = int(next(it))\n    \n    graph = defaultdict(set)\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        if u == v:  # self-loop, ignore for connectivity\n            continue\n        graph[u].add(v)\n        graph[v].add(u)\n    \n    k = int(next(it))\n    bad_pairs = set()\n    for _ in range(k):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        bad_pairs.add((min(x, y), max(x, y)))\n    \n    # Find connected components using BFS/DFS\n    visited = [False] * (n + 1)\n    comp_id = [0] * (n + 1)\n    comp_count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if not visited[i]:\n            comp_count += 1\n            queue = deque([i])\n            visited[i] = True\n            comp_id[i] = comp_count\n            while queue:\n                node = queue.popleft()\n                for neighbor in graph[node]:\n                    if not visited[neighbor]:\n                        visited[neighbor] = True\n                        comp_id[neighbor] = comp_count\n                        queue.append(neighbor)\n    \n    q = int(next(it))\n    for _ in range(q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q_val = int(next(it))\n        p, q_val = min(p, q_val), max(p, q_val)\n        if comp_id[p] == comp_id[q_val]:\n            # Adding edge between two nodes in same component doesn't create new path between previously disconnected pairs?\n            # But we must check if this new edge connects two components that together bridge a bad pair.\n            # Actually: if p and q_val are in same component, then the edge doesn't change connectivity.\n            # But if they are in different components, then ALL pairs (x,y) where x in comp1 and y in comp2 become connected.\n            # So we need to check: does adding edge (p,q_val) connect two components that together contain any bad pair?\n            # However, note: the graph was good initially, meaning no bad pair was connected.\n            # After adding edge (p,q_val), the two components become one. So any bad pair (x,y) where x was in comp(p) and y in comp(q_val) becomes connected -> bad.\n            # So we must check: for any bad pair (x,y), if x and y are in the two components that we are merging, then it becomes bad.\n            # But we can't iterate over all K bad pairs per query (K,Q up to 200K -> 40e9 worst case).\n            # Instead: precompute for each component, which bad pairs are \"split\" across it?\n            # Actually: we can precompute a set of bad pairs that are split between components.\n            # But note: a bad pair (x,y) is split if comp_id[x] != comp_id[y]. Since the original graph is good, all bad pairs are in different components.\n            # So for each bad pair (x,y), we know comp_id[x] and comp_id[y] (and they are different).\n            # Now, when we add edge (p,q_val), we merge comp_id[p] and comp_id[q_val]. \n            # The new graph is NOT good if there exists ANY bad pair (x,y) such that {comp_id[x], comp_id[y]} == {comp_id[p], comp_id[q_val]}.\n            # So we can precompute: for each unordered pair of components (c1, c2) (with c1 < c2), whether there is a bad pair with one endpoint in c1 and the other in c2.\n            # Then for each query: if the two components (c_p, c_q) have at least one bad pair between them -> then adding the edge makes it bad -> \"No\"\n            # Otherwise, \"Yes\"\n            pass  # we'll do the precomputation below\n    \n    # Precomputation: for each pair of components, record if there is a bad pair between them.\n    comp_bad = defaultdict(set)  # key: (min_comp, max_comp), value: True if exists at least one bad pair between them\n    for x, y in bad_pairs:\n        cx = comp_id[x]\n        cy = comp_id[y]\n        if cx == cy:\n            # This should not happen because the original graph is good\n            continue\n        c1, c2 = min(cx, cy), max(cx, cy)\n        comp_bad[(c1, c2)] = True\n    \n    # Now process queries\n    for _ in range(q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q_val = int(next(it))\n        p, q_val = min(p, q_val), max(p, q_val)\n        c1 = comp_id[p]\n        c2 = comp_id[q_val]\n        if c1 == c2:\n            # The edge is within the same component: connectivity doesn't change -> still good\n            print(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            c1, c2 = min(c1, c2), max(c1, c2)\n            if (c1, c2) in comp_bad:\n                print(\"No\")\n            else:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n\nmain()\n```",
