      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every operation `i`\n\n```\nS_i = { A_i,1 , … , A_i,Ki }          (Ki ≥ 2)\nC_i = weight\n```\n\nall `Ki·(Ki‑1)/2` edges of the complete graph on `S_i` are added and **all\nthese edges have the same weight `C_i`**.\n\nThe whole graph can contain up to `N·(N‑1)/2` edges – far too many to store.\nOnly the weight of a *minimum spanning tree* (MST) is required.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.  Observations  \n\n* In Kruskal’s algorithm edges are processed in non‑decreasing order of\n  weight and an edge is taken **iff** it connects two different connected\n  components (disjoint‑set / union‑find structure).\n\n* Inside one operation all edges have the **same weight**.  \n  When Kruskal reaches this weight it may choose any subset that connects\n  the still separated components inside `S_i`.  \n  If among the vertices of `S_i` there are `r` different components,\n  exactly `r‑1` edges of weight `C_i` are necessary and sufficient\n  (any spanning tree of those `r` components).\n\n* Therefore we never have to look at the individual edges, only at the\n  set of different DSU roots that appear in the current `S_i`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Algorithm  \n\n```\nread all operations\nsort them by C_i (ascending)\n\nmake a DSU for the N vertices          # initially N components\nanswer = 0\n\nfor each operation (C, vertices) in sorted order:\n        R = set of distinct DSU roots of the vertices\n        if |R| <= 1:               # already inside one component\n                continue\n        answer += (|R|-1) * C       # we need exactly this many edges\n        union all roots in R        # after that they become one component\n\nif DSU still has more than one component:\n        print -1\nelse:\n        print answer\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints  \n\n*`-1` iff the final graph is disconnected*, otherwise the weight of a\nminimum spanning tree.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nWhen the algorithm processes an operation with weight `C`,\nlet `R` be the set of DSU roots of its vertices *before* any unions of this\noperation.  \nAny spanning tree of the whole graph that respects Kruskal’s order must\ncontain at least `|R|-1` edges of weight `C` whose endpoints are inside the\noperation’s vertex set.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAll edges of the operation have weight `C`; all edges processed earlier\nhave smaller weight and therefore cannot connect two vertices whose\ncurrent DSU roots are different (otherwise those vertices would already be\nin the same component).  \nConsequently, before handling weight `C` the vertices of the operation are\nsplit into exactly the `|R|` DSU components represented by `R`.  \nA spanning tree has to connect those components, which needs at least\n`|R|-1` edges, and every such edge must belong to the current operation,\nbecause only this operation provides edges between the vertices of `R`\nwith weight `C`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nThe algorithm adds exactly `|R|-1` edges of weight `C`\n(and pays `( |R|-1 )·C`) while processing an operation.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe algorithm unions the `|R|` components into one component.\nEach union merges two different components, i.e. corresponds to one edge of\nweight `C`.  \nTo merge `|R|` components we need exactly `|R|-1` unions, therefore the\nalgorithm adds exactly that many edges and the stated cost. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nAfter processing all operations the DSU components are exactly the\nconnected components of the final graph.\n\n**Proof.**  \n*If two vertices become connected* in the real graph, there exists a path\nwhose edges appear in some operations.  \nConsider the heaviest edge on that path; when its operation is processed,\nits endpoints are in different DSU components (otherwise the whole path\nwould already have been connected). The algorithm unions those components,\nso the two end vertices become united. Repeating this argument along the\npath finally unites the two original vertices.  \n\n*Conversely*, the algorithm only unites vertices when it processes an\noperation that really contains an edge between the two DSU components,\nhence every union corresponds to a real edge. Therefore vertices that are\nin the same DSU set are connected by a series of real edges. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nIf the algorithm finishes with a single DSU component, the total paid\nweight equals the weight of a minimum spanning tree of the whole graph.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider Kruskal’s algorithm on the (implicit) edge list.\nKruskal processes edges by non‑decreasing weight; when it reaches weight\n`C` it sees exactly the edges added by the operations with that weight.\nBecause all those edges are equivalent, any choice that connects the still\nseparate components is optimal.\n\nOur algorithm, for each weight `C`, connects the same components and pays\nthe minimum possible amount `( |R|-1 )·C` (Lemma&nbsp;2).  \nThus the set of edges implicitly taken by our algorithm is a possible\noutput of Kruskal, i.e. it is a minimum spanning tree. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 5  \nIf the algorithm finishes with more than one DSU component, the final\ngraph is disconnected.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 DSU components equal the graph’s connected components.\nHence more than one DSU component means more than one connected component,\ni.e. the graph is disconnected. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm prints  \n\n* `-1` iff the graph after all operations is disconnected;  \n\n* otherwise the total weight of a minimum spanning tree of the graph.\n\n**Proof.**  \nIf the algorithm prints `-1`, by Lemma&nbsp;5 the graph is disconnected.\nIf the graph is disconnected, Lemma&nbsp;5 tells us the algorithm will end\nwith several DSU components, therefore it prints `-1`.  \n\nIf the algorithm does **not** print `-1`, there is only one DSU component,\nhence the graph is connected (Lemma&nbsp;3) and, by Lemma&nbsp;4,\nthe accumulated weight is exactly the MST weight. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Reading input* – `O( Σ Ki )` ≤ `4·10⁵`.  \n\n*Sorting operations* – `O( M log M )` ≤ `2·10⁵ log 2·10⁵`.  \n\n*Processing* – each vertex appears in exactly one `find` per operation,\nso total `O( Σ Ki · α(N) )`, where `α` is the inverse Ackermann function,\npractically constant.\n\nOverall  \n\n```\nTime   : O( M log M + Σ Ki )\nMemory : O( N + Σ Ki )   (DSU + stored operations)\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits (`N, M ≤ 2·10⁵`, `Σ Ki ≤ 4·10⁵`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Disjoint Set Union (Union‑Find) with component counter\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\nclass DSU:\n    __slots__ = (\"parent\", \"size\", \"components\")\n    def __init__(self, n: int):\n        self.parent = list(range(n))\n        self.size = [1] * n\n        self.components = n - 1          # we will use 1‑based vertices, ignore index 0\n\n    def find(self, x: int) -> int:\n        # path compression\n        while self.parent[x] != x:\n            self.parent[x] = self.parent[self.parent[x]]\n            x = self.parent[x]\n        return x\n\n    def union(self, a: int, b: int) -> bool:\n        ra = self.find(a)\n        rb = self.find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return False\n        if self.size[ra] < self.size[rb]:\n            ra, rb = rb, ra\n        self.parent[rb] = ra\n        self.size[ra] += self.size[rb]\n        self.components -= 1\n        return True\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = list(map(int, sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()))\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = next(it)\n    M = next(it)\n\n    operations = []\n    for _ in range(M):\n        K = next(it)\n        C = next(it)\n        verts = [next(it) for _ in range(K)]\n        operations.append((C, verts))\n\n    # sort by weight (ascending)\n    operations.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n\n    dsu = DSU(N + 1)          # vertices are 1..N, index 0 unused\n    total = 0\n\n    for C, verts in operations:\n        roots = set()\n        for v in verts:\n            roots.add(dsu.find(v))\n        if len(roots) <= 1:\n            continue\n        total += (len(roots) - 1) * C\n        # union all roots together\n        roots = list(roots)\n        base = roots[0]\n        for r in roots[1:]:\n            dsu.union(base, r)\n\n    if dsu.components == 1:\n        print(total)\n    else:\n        print(-1)\n\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms\nto the required input / output format.",
