    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_355",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThis problem has a similar setting to Problem G. Differences in the problem statement are indicated in red.\nThere is a grid with H rows and W columns, where each cell is painted red or green.\nLet (i,j) denote the cell in the i-th row from the top and the j-th column from the left.\nThe color of cell (i,j) is represented by the character S_{{i,j}}, where S_{{i,j}} = . means cell (i,j) is red, and S_{{i,j}} = # means cell (i,j) is green.\nThe number of green connected components in the grid is the number of connected components in the graph with the vertex set being the green cells and the edge set being the edges connecting two adjacent green cells. Here, two cells (x,y) and (x',y') are considered adjacent when |x-x'| + |y-y'| = 1.\nConsider choosing one red cell uniformly at random and repainting it green. Print the expected value of the number of green connected components in the grid after repainting, modulo 998244353.\n\nWhat does \"print the expected value modulo 998244353\" mean? \r\nIt can be proved that the sought expected value is always rational.\r\nFurthermore, the constraints of this problem guarantee that if that value is expressed as \\frac{{P}}{{Q}} using two coprime integers P and Q, there is exactly one integer R such that R \\times Q \\equiv P \\pmod{{998244353}} and 0 \\leq R < 998244353. Print this R.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W\r\nS_{{1,1}}S_{{1,2}}\\ldotsS_{{1,W}}\r\nS_{{2,1}}S_{{2,2}}\\ldotsS_{{2,W}}\r\n\\vdots\r\nS_{{H,1}}S_{{H,2}}\\ldotsS_{{H,W}}\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq H,W \\leq 1000\n- S_{{i,j}} = . or S_{{i,j}} = #.\n- There is at least one (i,j) such that S_{{i,j}} = ..\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 3\r\n##.\r\n#.#\r\n#..\n\nSample Output 1\n\n499122178\r\n\nIf cell (1,3) is repainted green, the number of green connected components becomes 1.\nIf cell (2,2) is repainted green, the number of green connected components becomes 1.\nIf cell (3,2) is repainted green, the number of green connected components becomes 2.\nIf cell (3,3) is repainted green, the number of green connected components becomes 2.\nTherefore, the expected value of the number of green connected components after choosing one red cell uniformly at random and repainting it green is (1+1+2+2)/4 = 3/2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4 5\r\n..#..\r\n.###.\r\n#####\r\n..#..\n\nSample Output 2\n\n598946613\n\nSample Input 3\n\n3 4\r\n#...\r\n.#.#\r\n..##\n\nSample Output 3\n\n285212675",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-20b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor the given grid\n\n* `.`  – red cell  \n* `#` – green cell  \n\nlet\n\n* `C` – number of green connected components in the original grid  \n* `R` – number of red cells (the choice set)  \n\nWhen a red cell is turned green it is adjacent to at most four green cells.\nSuppose a chosen red cell `x` touches\n\n```\nk = |{different components that have a neighbor of x}|\n```\n\ncomponents.  \nAfter recoloring\n\n```\nnew components = C – k + 1\n```\n\nTherefore the expected number of green components is\n\n```\nE = ( Σ_x (C – k_x + 1) ) / R\n  = C + 1  –  ( Σ_x k_x ) / R\n```\n\nOnly `Σ k_x` has to be computed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.  Label all green components\n\nA BFS/DFS over the whole grid gives every green cell a component label\n`comp[i][j]`.  \nDuring the search we also count the number of distinct labels – this is `C`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  For every red cell count the number of *different*\nadjacent component labels\n\n```\nfor each red cell (i,j)\n    neighbours = []\n    for 4 directions\n        if neighbour is inside the grid and green\n            neighbours append comp[ni][nj]\n    k  = number of distinct values in neighbours\n    sum_k += k\n```\n\n`k` is at most 4, so using a small fixed set or `set()` is fine.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Final formula\n\n```\nP = (C + 1) * R  –  sum_k\nQ = R\nresult = P / Q  (mod MOD)\n```\n\n`MOD = 998244353` is prime, the modular inverse of `Q` exists:\n\n```\ninvQ = pow(Q, MOD-2, MOD)\nanswer = (P % MOD) * invQ % MOD\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the required value.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nAfter recoloring a red cell `x`, the number of green connected components equals  \n`C – k_x + 1`, where `k_x` is the number of distinct green components adjacent to `x`.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n`x` is added to the graph of green cells.  \n*If `x` touches no green component (`k_x = 0`):*  \n`x` becomes a new component – components increase by 1.  \n*If `x` touches `k_x ≥ 1` distinct components:*  \nAll those components are merged into a single component together with `x`.  \nThe change of component count is `+1 – k_x`.  ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \n`Σ_{x∈red} k_x` computed by the algorithm equals the sum of `k_x` for all red cells.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nFor each red cell the algorithm inspects its 4 neighbours.  \nA neighbour that is green contributes its component label to the local list.\nThe algorithm then counts distinct labels, which is exactly `k_x`.  \nSummation over all red cells gives the required sum. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nLet `S = Σ_{x∈red} k_x`.  \nThe value  \n\n```\nE = ( (C + 1) * R – S ) / R\n```\n\nis the expected number of green components after recoloring one uniformly\nrandom red cell.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 total components after recoloring cell `x` is `C – k_x + 1`.  \nSumming over all red cells and dividing by `R`:\n\n```\nE = ( Σ_x (C – k_x + 1) ) / R\n  = ( R·C + R – S ) / R\n  = ( (C + 1)·R – S ) / R\n```\n\nwhich is exactly the expression above. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm outputs the expected number of green components after one\nrandom red cell is recolored, expressed modulo `998244353` as required.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n* By Lemma&nbsp;1 the component change formula is correct.  \n* By Lemma&nbsp;2 the algorithm obtains the correct sum `S`.  \n* By Lemma&nbsp;3 the rational value `E` is computed.  \n* The algorithm then applies modular arithmetic to\n`P = (C + 1)·R – S` and `Q = R` and outputs  \n`P * Q^{-1} (mod MOD)`.  \nBecause `MOD` is prime and `Q` is a positive integer less than `MOD`,\n`Q^{-1}` exists and the output is exactly the unique integer `R`\nsuch that `R · Q ≡ P (mod MOD)`.  \nTherefore the algorithm prints the demanded answer. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Complexity Analysis\n\n*Labeling components:* `O(H·W)`  \n*Counting `k_x` for all red cells:* each cell checks at most 4 neighbours → `O(H·W)`  \nTotal time   : `O(H·W)`  \nMemory usage : `O(H·W)` for the grid and component labels, fitting the limits\n(`≤ 10⁶` integers).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().strip().splitlines())\n    h, w = map(int, next(it).split())\n    grid = [list(next(it).rstrip()) for _ in range(h)]\n\n    comp = [[-1]*w for _ in range(h)]\n    comp_id = 0\n    dir4 = [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]\n\n    # BFS to label green components\n    for i in range(h):\n        for j in range(w):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#' and comp[i][j] == -1:\n                q = deque()\n                q.append((i,j))\n                comp[i][j] = comp_id\n                while q:\n                    x,y = q.popleft()\n                    for dx,dy in dir4:\n                        nx,ny = x+dx, y+dy\n                        if 0 <= nx < h and 0 <= ny < w and grid[nx][ny] == '#' and comp[nx][ny]==-1:\n                            comp[nx][ny] = comp_id\n                            q.append((nx,ny))\n                comp_id += 1\n\n    C = comp_id                # number of green components\n    R = 0                      # number of red cells\n    sum_k = 0\n\n    for i in range(h):\n        for j in range(w):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                R += 1\n                seen = set()\n                for dx,dy in dir4:\n                    ni, nj = i+dx, j+dy\n                    if 0 <= ni < h and 0 <= nj < w and grid[ni][nj] == '#':\n                        seen.add(comp[ni][nj])\n                sum_k += len(seen)\n\n    P = (C + 1) * R - sum_k\n    invR = pow(R, MOD-2, MOD)\n    ans = (P % MOD) * invR % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above.",
