    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every cell  \n\n* `#` – green  \n* `.` – red  \n\nTwo cells are adjacent when they share a side (Manhattan distance `1`).\n\nFor a fixed grid let  \n\n* `C₀` – number of green connected components  \n* `R` – set of red cells, `|R| = N (>0)`  \n\nWe choose one cell `v ∈ R` uniformly at random,\npaint it green and look at the new number of green components.\nThe required answer is the expected value of this number,\nwritten modulo  \n\n```\nMOD = 998244353                (prime)\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Effect of painting one red cell  \n\nNeighbouring green components of a red cell `v`\n\n```\nk(v) = number of *different* green components among the up to 4 neighbours\n```\n\n```\nk(v) = 0   →   v becomes an isolated component\nk(v) = 1   →   v joins that component, nothing else changes\nk(v) ≥ 2   →   all those k(v) components are merged into one together with v\n```\n\nHence after painting `v`\n\n```\nnew component count = C₀ + 1 – k(v)                (1)\n```\n\n(the `+1` is the new cell itself, `k(v)` is subtracted because the `k(v)`\ndifferent components turn into a single one).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Expected value  \n\nFrom (1)\n\n```\nE = C₀ + 1 – ( Σ_{v∈R} k(v) ) / N               (2)\n```\n\nDefine  \n\n```\nS = Σ_{v∈R} k(v)\n```\n\nThen  \n\n```\nE = (C₀ + 1) – S / N.\n```\n\nAll numbers are integers except the fraction `S / N`.  \nBecause `MOD` is prime, division by `N` is performed by multiplying with the\nmodular inverse `N⁻¹ = N^{MOD-2} (mod MOD)`.\n\nFinally  \n\n```\nanswer = (C₀ + 1) – S * N⁻¹   (mod MOD)          (3)\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   What has to be computed ?\n\n* `C₀` – number of green components  \n* `S` – sum of `k(v)` over all red cells  \n* `N` – number of red cells  \n\nAll can be obtained in **O(H·W)** time.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Computing the green components  \n\nBreadth‑first search (or DFS) on the grid, visiting only `#` cells.\nEach visited green cell receives the id of its component (`0,1,2,…`).\nThe number of performed BFS runs is exactly `C₀`.\n\nThe component id of a cell `(i,j)` is stored in a 2‑D array `comp`,\n`-1` means “not green”.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Computing `S`\n\nFor every red cell (`.`) we look at its four neighbours,\ncollect the (different) component ids that are **not** `-1`,\nthe size of this set is `k(v)`.  \n`k(v) ≤ 4`, therefore a tiny Python `set` (or a fixed‑size loop with a\nsmall manual deduplication) is fast enough.\n\nAdd `k(v)` to the running total `S`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.   Modulo arithmetic  \n\n```\ninvN = pow(N, MOD-2, MOD)          # modular inverse\nans  = (C0 + 1) % MOD\nans  = (ans - S % MOD * invN) % MOD\n```\n\nThe final `ans` is already in the required interval `[0, MOD)`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 7.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the value described in the statement.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor a red cell `v` let `k(v)` be the number of distinct green components\nadjacent to `v`. After repainting `v` green the total number of green\ncomponents equals `C₀ + 1 – k(v)`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBefore repainting there are `C₀` components.\n`v` itself creates a new component (`+1`).  \nIf `k(v)=0` this component stays isolated – total `C₀+1`.  \n\nIf `k(v)≥1` the `k(v)` neighbouring components become connected through `v`,\nthey turn into a single component, i.e. the number of components is reduced\nby `k(v)-1`.  \nOverall change: `+1 - (k(v)-1) = 1 - k(v)`. Adding to `C₀` gives\n`C₀ + 1 - k(v)`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nLet `S = Σ_{v∈R} k(v)` and `N = |R|`.  \nThe expected number `E` of green components after the random repainting is  \n\n```\nE = (C₀ + 1) – S / N .\n```\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1, for each red cell `v` the resulting number equals\n`C₀ + 1 – k(v)`.  \nThe expectation over the uniform distribution on `R` is the average:\n\n```\nE = (1/N) Σ_{v∈R} (C₀ + 1 – k(v))\n  = C₀ + 1 – (1/N) Σ_{v∈R} k(v)\n  = C₀ + 1 – S/N .\n```\n∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nThe algorithm computes the exact values `C₀`, `S` and `N`.\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n* `N` – simply the number of `.` cells while scanning the input.  \n\n* `C₀` – BFS explores each green cell exactly once, each BFS run visits a\nmaximal 4‑connected set of green cells, i.e. one component.\nThe number of runs therefore equals the number of green components.  \n\n* `S` – for a red cell the algorithm inspects its four neighbours,\ncollects the ids of green neighbours, discards duplicates,\ncounts them (`k(v)`) and adds to `S`.  \nBy definition of `k(v)` this contributes exactly the required term.\nSummed over all red cells we obtain `S`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nThe value printed by the algorithm equals  \n`(C₀ + 1) – S·N⁻¹ (mod MOD)`, i.e. the modular representation of the rational\n`(C₀ + 1) – S/N`.\n\n**Proof.**  \n`invN = N^{MOD-2} (mod MOD)` is the modular inverse of `N` because `MOD`\nis prime and `N` is not divisible by `MOD`.  \nThus `S·invN ≡ S/N (mod MOD)`.  \nThe program computes `ans = (C₀+1) – S·invN (mod MOD)`,\nexactly the expression of the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe program outputs the required expected value of the number of green\nconnected components after repainting one uniformly random red cell,\nrepresented as the unique integer `R (0 ≤ R < MOD)` satisfying  \n`R·Q ≡ P (mod MOD)` for the reduced fraction `P/Q`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the algorithm obtains the true integers `C₀, S, N`.  \nLemma&nbsp;2 tells that the exact expected value is the rational number\n`(C₀ + 1) – S/N`.  \nLemma&nbsp;4 shows that the printed number is exactly the residue of this\nrational number modulo `MOD`, i.e. the unique integer `R` described in the\nstatement. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 8.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n* BFS labeling: each cell examined at most once → `O(H·W)` time, `O(H·W)` memory\n  for the component id array.\n* Summation of `k(v)`: again each cell examined once, constant work per cell →\n  `O(H·W)` time, `O(1)` extra memory.\n* Final arithmetic: `O(1)`.\n\nOverall  \n\n```\nTime   :  O(H·W)   ≤ 10⁶ operations\nMemory :  O(H·W)   ≤ 10⁶ integers  (≈ 8 MB)\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 9.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().splitlines())\n    H, W = map(int, next(it).split())\n    grid = [list(next(it).strip()) for _ in range(H)]\n\n    # component id for green cells, -1 otherwise\n    comp = [[-1] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    dirs = [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]\n\n    C0 = 0                     # number of green components\n    # BFS over green cells\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#' and comp[i][j] == -1:\n                # start new component\n                dq = deque()\n                dq.append((i, j))\n                comp[i][j] = C0\n                while dq:\n                    x, y = dq.popleft()\n                    for dx, dy in dirs:\n                        nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                        if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W:\n                            if grid[nx][ny] == '#' and comp[nx][ny] == -1:\n                                comp[nx][ny] = C0\n                                dq.append((nx, ny))\n                C0 += 1\n\n    N = 0                      # number of red cells\n    S = 0                      # sum of k(v)\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                N += 1\n                neigh = set()\n                for dx, dy in dirs:\n                    nx, ny = i + dx, j + dy\n                    if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W:\n                        cid = comp[nx][ny]\n                        if cid != -1:\n                            neigh.add(cid)\n                S += len(neigh)\n\n    invN = pow(N, MOD - 2, MOD)          # modular inverse of N\n    ans = (C0 + 1) % MOD\n    ans = (ans - (S % MOD) * invN) % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input‑output format.",
