      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(1 << 25)\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.readline\n    \n    H, W = map(int, input().split())\n    grid = [list(input().strip()) for _ in range(H)]\n    \n    MOD = 998244353\n    \n    # Count red cells\n    red_count = 0\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                red_count += 1\n    \n    if red_count == 0:\n        # No red cells, but problem guarantees at least one.\n        # However, if it were zero, expected components would be 0/0? \n        # But constraints say at least one red.\n        print(0)\n        return\n    \n    # Precompute modular inverse of red_count\n    inv_red = pow(red_count, MOD - 2, MOD)\n    \n    # First, we need to compute:\n    # E = (1/red_count) * sum_{red cell r} (components after painting r green)\n    # = (1/red_count) * [ sum_{r} (initial_components + delta_r) ]\n    # where delta_r = change in number of components when painting r green.\n    # Since painting r green can only merge components or stay same,\n    # delta_r = 1 - (number of distinct green components adjacent to r)\n    # Because painting a red cell reduces the component count by (adjacent_components - 1)\n    # Actually, new_components = initial_components + 1 - (adjacent_components)\n    # So delta_r = 1 - adjacent_components.\n    \n    # Therefore, E = (1/red_count) * sum_r (initial_components + 1 - adj_comp(r))\n    # = initial_components + (1/red_count) * sum_r (1 - adj_comp(r))\n    # = initial_components + 1 - (1/red_count) * sum_r adj_comp(r)\n    \n    # So we need:\n    # 1. initial number of green connected components\n    # 2. For each red cell, number of distinct green components adjacent to it (0-4)\n    \n    # We'll label each green cell with its component id using BFS/DFS.\n    comp_id = [[-1] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    component_count = 0\n    \n    dirs = [(1,0), (-1,0), (0,1), (0,-1)]\n    \n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#' and comp_id[i][j] == -1:\n                # BFS\n                queue = [(i, j)]\n                comp_id[i][j] = component_count\n                for x, y in queue:\n                    for dx, dy in dirs:\n                        nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                        if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W and grid[nx][ny] == '#' and comp_id[nx][ny] == -1:\n                            comp_id[nx][ny] = component_count\n                            queue.append((nx, ny))\n                component_count += 1\n    \n    # Now compute for each red cell, number of adjacent distinct green components.\n    sum_adj = 0\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                adjacent_comps = set()\n                for dx, dy in dirs:\n                    ni, nj = i + dx, j + dy\n                    if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '#':\n                        adjacent_comps.add(comp_id[ni][nj])\n                adj_count = len(adjacent_comps)\n                sum_adj += adj_count\n    \n    # Expected value = component_count + 1 - sum_adj / red_count\n    # modulo MOD.\n    numerator = (component_count + 1) * red_count - sum_adj\n    numerator %= MOD\n    ans = numerator * inv_red % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
