      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every divisor `k` of the number of boxes `n`\n\n* the first truck receives boxes `1 … k`,  \n* the second truck receives boxes `k+1 … 2k`, and so on.\n\nAll trucks contain exactly `k` boxes, therefore the sequence is split into  \n\n```\nm = n / k          groups (trucks)\n```\n\nFor one fixed `k` we need\n\n```\nmaxSum(k) = maximum total weight of a truck\nminSum(k) = minimum total weight of a truck\ndiff(k)   = maxSum(k) – minSum(k)\n```\n\nThe required answer for the test case is\n\n```\nmax over all divisors k of n   diff(k)\n```\n\nIf `n` is prime, only `k = 1` and `k = n` are possible, but the\nformula works for every divisor.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Prefix sums\n\nLet  \n\n```\npref[0] = 0\npref[i] = a1 + a2 + … + ai          (1 ≤ i ≤ n)\n```\n\nSum of boxes `l … r` is `pref[r] – pref[l‑1]`.  \nFor group `j (0‑based)` and size `k`\n\n```\nstart = j * k\nend   = (j+1) * k\nsum_j = pref[end] – pref[start]\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\nFor each test case\n\n```\nread n and array a[1…n]\nbuild pref[0…n]\nfind all divisors of n   (usual O(√n) enumeration)\nanswer = 0\nfor every divisor k\n        m = n // k\n        curMin = +∞ , curMax = -∞\n        for j = 0 … m-1\n                s = pref[(j+1)*k] – pref[j*k]\n                curMin = min(curMin, s)\n                curMax = max(curMax, s)\n        answer = max(answer, curMax - curMin)\nprint answer\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the required maximum absolute\ndifference.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor a fixed divisor `k` of `n`, the algorithm computes\n`maxSum(k)` and `minSum(k)` correctly.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n- The `j`‑th truck (0‑based) receives boxes numbered  \n  `j*k + 1 … (j+1)*k`.  \n- Using the prefix sums the algorithm computes\n  `s_j = pref[(j+1)*k] – pref[j*k]`, which equals the sum of exactly those\n  boxes.\n- The inner loop iterates over all `m = n/k` trucks, comparing each sum to\n  `curMin` and `curMax`.  \n  After the loop finishes, `curMin = min_j s_j` and `curMax = max_j s_j`,\n  i.e. `minSum(k)` and `maxSum(k)`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nFor every divisor `k`, the value `diff(k)` produced by the algorithm\nequals the maximum absolute difference between the totals of any two trucks\nwhen each truck holds exactly `k` boxes.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nAll trucks for this `k` contain exactly `k` boxes, so their totals are\nprecisely the `s_j` computed above.  \nThe largest difference of two numbers is obtained by the maximum minus the\nminimum, i.e. `maxSum(k) – minSum(k)`.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the algorithm calculates exactly this difference. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nThe algorithm’s final answer equals  \n\\(\\displaystyle \\max_{k\\mid n} diff(k)\\).\n\n**Proof.**\n\nThe outer loop iterates over every divisor `k` of `n`.  \nFor each such `k` it computes `diff(k)` (Lemma&nbsp;2) and updates\n`answer` with the maximum of the current `answer` and that value.\nTherefore after the loop finishes, `answer` equals the maximum `diff(k)`\nover all divisors. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nFor each test case the algorithm outputs the maximum possible absolute\ndifference between the total weights of two trucks over all allowed\ntruck sizes `k`.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the algorithm outputs  \n\\(\\displaystyle \\max_{k\\mid n} diff(k)\\).\nThis is exactly the definition of the required value. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\nFor a test case with length `n`\n\n* Enumerating divisors: `O(√n)`.\n* For each divisor `k` the inner loop visits `n/k` trucks,\n  and the sum over all divisors of `n/k` is at most `n`  \n  (each index of the array is counted at most once per divisor).\n  Therefore total work per test case: `O(n + √n)`.\n\nAcross all test cases the total `n` does not exceed `150 000`,\nso the program easily fits in time limits.\n\nMemory usage: `O(n)` for the array and its prefix sums.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport math\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().strip().split())\n    t = int(next(it))\n    out_lines = []\n\n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(next(it))\n        a = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(n)]\n        pref = [0] * (n + 1)\n        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n            pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + a[i - 1]\n\n        # find all divisors of n\n        divs = []\n        for d in range(1, int(math.isqrt(n)) + 1):\n            if n % d == 0:\n                divs.append(d)\n                if d != n // d:\n                    divs.append(n // d)\n\n        best = 0\n        for k in divs:\n            m = n // k\n            cur_min = 10**20\n            cur_max = -1\n            for j in range(m):\n                s = pref[(j + 1) * k] - pref[j * k]\n                if s < cur_min:\n                    cur_min = s\n                if s > cur_max:\n                    cur_max = s\n            diff = cur_max - cur_min\n            if diff > best:\n                best = diff\n\n        out_lines.append(str(best))\n\n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe code follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above.",
