    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_317",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given an integer B.\nIf there exists a positive integer A such that A^A = B, print its value; otherwise, output -1.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nB\n\nOutput\n\nIf there exists a positive integer A such that A^A = B, print its value; otherwise, print -1.\nIf there are multiple positive integers A such that A^A = B, any of them will be accepted.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq B \\leq 10^{{18}}\n- B is an integer.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n27\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\n\n3^3 = 27, so print 3.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n100\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\n\nThere is no A such that A^A = B.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n10000000000\n\nSample Output 3\n\n10",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-20b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor a given integer `B` we have to determine whether there is a positive\ninteger `A` such that\n\n```\nA^A = B\n```\n\nIf such an `A` exists, we output it; otherwise we output `-1`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Observations\n\n* `B ≤ 10^18`.\n* For `A = 15`, `15^15 = 4.378 … × 10^17 < 10^18`.\n* For `A = 16`, `16^16 = 1.844 × 10^19 > 10^18`.\n\nSo the only possible values of `A` are in the interval `[1, 15]`.  \nThat means a simple brute force over at most 15 candidates is sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nfor A from 1 to 15 (inclusive)\n        val = A^A          // integer power\n        if val == B\n                print A\n                exit\n        if val > B\n                break        // further A will only increase A^A\nprint -1\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the required value.\n\n*Lemma 1*  \nIf the algorithm prints some integer `A`, then `A^A = B`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe algorithm prints `A` only when `val == B`, where `val` was computed\nas `A^A`. Hence `A^A = B`. ∎\n\n*Lemma 2*  \nIf a positive integer `A₀` satisfies `A₀^A₀ = B`, the algorithm will print\nsome integer (not necessarily `A₀`).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe loop iterates over all integers from `1` to `15`.  \nSince `A₀` satisfies the equation and `A₀ ≤ 15` (proved by the observation\nabove), the iteration reaches `A = A₀`.  \nAt that point `val == B` and the algorithm prints `A₀`. ∎\n\n*Theorem*  \nThe algorithm prints a valid `A` if one exists; otherwise it prints `-1`.\n\n*Proof.*  \n- If a valid `A` exists, by Lemma&nbsp;2 the algorithm prints it, and by\n  Lemma&nbsp;1 the printed value satisfies the equation.\n- If no valid `A` exists, the loop never triggers the `print A` statement.\n  After exhausting all candidates the algorithm prints `-1`.\n\nThus the algorithm behaves exactly as required. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis\n\nThe loop runs at most 15 times, each time computing an integer power\n(`O(log A)` multiplications, but `A ≤ 15`).  \nTherefore\n\n```\nTime   : O(1)  (constant, ≤ 15 iterations)\nMemory : O(1)\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    line = sys.stdin.readline()\n    if not line:\n        return\n    B = int(line.strip())\n\n    # We only need to check A from 1 to 15 because 16^16 already exceeds 1e18\n    for A in range(1, 16):\n        val = pow(A, A)\n        if val == B:\n            print(A)\n            return\n        if val > B:\n            break\n    print(-1)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe code follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above.",
