      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor the given integer `B` we have to decide whether there is a positive integer `A`\nwith  \n\n```\nA ^ A = B               ( ^ means exponentiation )\n```\n\nIf such an `A` exists we output it, otherwise we output `-1`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Observations  \n\n* `A` can only be small.\n  `A = 16` already gives `16^16 = 2^64 = 1.84·10¹⁹ > 10¹⁸`,\n  the maximal possible `B`.  \n  Therefore any possible `A` satisfies `A ≤ 15`.\n\n* The range `1 … 15` is tiny, we can simply try every value, compute `A**A`\n  (Python’s integer exponentiation has arbitrary precision) and compare with `B`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread B\nfor A from 1 upwards:\n        X = A ** A\n        if X == B:   output A and stop\n        if X > B:    break   # further A will only increase X\noutput -1\n```\nThe loop will stop after at most 15 iterations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints a correct answer.\n\n*Lemma 1*  \nIf the algorithm outputs an integer `A`, then `A^A = B`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe algorithm outputs `A` only when the condition `X == B` is satisfied,\nwhere `X` was set to `A ** A`. Hence `A^A = B`. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2*  \nIf there exists a positive integer `A₀` with `A₀^A₀ = B`,\nthe algorithm will output some integer (not `-1`).\n\n*Proof.*  \n`A₀ ≤ 15` (argument above). The loop iterates over every integer `A`\nstarting from `1` and increases by `1` each step, therefore it reaches `A₀`.\nWhen it reaches `A₀` we have `X = A₀ ** A₀ = B`, so the algorithm\noutputs `A₀` and terminates. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3*  \nIf the algorithm outputs `-1`, then no positive integer `A` satisfies `A^A = B`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe algorithm only outputs `-1` after the loop finishes without finding\n`X == B`. The loop examined **all** integers `A ≥ 1` up to the first `A`\nwith `A^A > B`. For any larger `A` the value `A^A` is strictly larger\n(because the function `A ↦ A^A` is strictly increasing for `A ≥ 1`),\nso it can never become equal to `B`. Hence no solution exists. ∎\n\n\n\n*Theorem*  \nThe algorithm prints `A` iff a positive integer `A` with `A^A = B` exists;\notherwise it prints `-1`.\n\n*Proof.*  \n- If the algorithm prints an integer `A`, by Lemma&nbsp;1 it satisfies the\n  required equation, so a solution exists.\n- If a solution exists, Lemma&nbsp;2 guarantees the algorithm prints a\n  (possibly different) integer, never `-1`.\n- If the algorithm prints `-1`, Lemma&nbsp;3 shows that no solution exists.\n\nThus the output is correct in all cases. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\nThe loop runs at most 15 times, each iteration does one exponentiation of\nsmall integers.  \nTime complexity: `O(1)` (constant).  \nMemory consumption: `O(1)`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    B = int(data)\n\n    A = 1\n    while True:\n        power = A ** A\n        if power == B:\n            print(A)\n            return\n        if power > B:          # further A will only increase the value\n            break\n        A += 1\n\n    print(-1)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above.",
