tput 1\n\n3\r\n1 1 1 0 0 1 0 1\r\n\nThe sample output corresponds to the following actions:\n\n- Find potions of types 2,3,1 in this order. Pick up all of them.\n- Find potions of types 3,2 in this order. Do not pick up any of them.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Do not pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-2 monster. Use one type-2 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-1 monster. Use one type-1 potion to defeat it.\n\nIn this sequence of actions, the value of K is 3.\nThere is no way to avoid defeat with K\\leq 2, so the sought value of K _ {{\\min}} is 3.\r\nThere are multiple sequences of actions that satisfy K=3 and allow him to avoid defeat; you may print any of them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4\r\n2 3\r\n1 4\r\n2 1\r\n1 2\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe will inevitably be defeated by the first monster he encounters.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30\r\n1 25\r\n1 2\r\n1 10\r\n1 18\r\n2 18\r\n1 11\r\n2 11\r\n1 21\r\n1 6\r\n2 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 11\r\n1 24\r\n1 11\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n1 18\r\n2 25\r\n1 8\r\n1 10\r\n1 11\r\n2 18\r\n2 10\r\n1 10\r\n2 2\r\n1 24\r\n1 10\r\n2 10\r\n1 25\r\n2 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\r\n1 1 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-20b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every event let\n\n* `t = 1` – a potion of type `x` is found (pick it or skip it)\n* `t = 2` – a monster of type `x` appears  \n  it must be defeated by a potion of the same type that has already been picked\n\nWhile the adventure is running we can keep at most `K` potions in the bag.\nWe have to\n\n* decide whether it is possible to defeat all monsters\n* if it is possible – find the minimum `K` (`Kmin`)\n  that makes this possible\n* output one strategy that uses only `Kmin` potions at any moment\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Observations\n\n* A potion that we pick is stored forever – we can **never** discard it later.\n* The only reason to pick a potion earlier than the moment we need it\n  is that at that moment we would otherwise be doomed.\n  Picking later would only help, never hurt, because the bag is smaller.\n* Therefore, for a fixed `K` the following greedy strategy is optimal\n\n```\nfor each event from first to last\n    if it is a potion (t = 1)\n        we need a potion of this type before the next monster of the same type,\n        i.e. if inventory[type] == 0 and more monsters of this type are still\n           pending.  Then we *must* pick it.\n        otherwise we skip it (picking would only increase the bag size).\n    if it is a monster (t = 2)\n        we must have one potion of this type in the bag.\n```\n\nIf during the simulation we violate the bag size `K` or we encounter a monster\nwithout a potion, the current `K` is impossible.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Checking a fixed `K`\n\nDuring the simulation we need to know\n\n```\nremaining[type]   – how many monsters of this type are still to come\ninventory[type]   – how many potions of this type we have in the bag\ntotal             – current number of potions stored (sum over all types)\n```\n\n`remaining[type]` is obtained once in the beginning – it is simply the total\nnumber of monsters of each type.  \nWhile iterating, after defeating a monster we decrease the corresponding\n`remaining[type]`.\n\nAlgorithm `possible(K)`  \n```\ninput: K\ninitialise remaining[1..N] with monster counts\ninventory[1..N] = 0\ntotal = 0\nanswer list for all potion events (kept in input order)\n\nfor each event (t, x)\n    if t == 1      # potion\n        if inventory[x] == 0 and remaining[x] > 0:\n            if total + 1 > K:  return (False, [])\n            inventory[x] += 1\n            total += 1\n            record '1' for this potion\n        else\n            record '0'\n    else           # monster\n        if inventory[x] == 0:  return (False, [])\n        inventory[x] -= 1\n        total -= 1\n        remaining[x] -= 1\n\nreturn (True, list_of_0/1)\n```\n\nThe proof of optimality follows from the observation in section&nbsp;1:\nany strategy that keeps the bag size ≤ K can only postpone picking potions,\ntherefore the greedy one never fails if a successful strategy exists.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Searching the minimal `K`\n\n`K` is an integer in `[0, N]`.  \nWe binary search this interval:\n\n```\nlo = 0, hi = N\nwhile lo < hi:\n    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n    if possible(mid) succeeds:\n        hi = mid\n    else:\n        lo = mid + 1\nKmin = lo\n```\n\nAfter the search we run `possible(Kmin)` once more to obtain the concrete\nsequence of 0/1 decisions.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the required result.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nDuring the simulation of `possible(K)` the content of the bag never\nexceeds `K`.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nThe only place where the bag size increases is when a potion is taken.\nThe algorithm checks `total + 1 > K` and rejects the whole attempt if it\nwould exceed `K`.  Therefore the bag size is always ≤ `K`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nIf `possible(K)` returns *True*, the returned strategy defeats all monsters\nand never keeps more than `K` potions.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n*All monsters are defeated*:  \nwhen a monster is processed we check that `inventory[x] > 0`.  \nIf not, the function returns *False*.  \nOtherwise a potion is consumed, so the monster is defeated.\n\n*Bag size never exceeds K*: from Lemma&nbsp;1. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nIf there exists any strategy that defeats all monsters with bag size ≤ `K`,\nthen `possible(K)` returns *True*.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nConsider an optimal strategy `S` with bag size ≤ `K`.  \nWhile the greedy simulation (`possible(K)`) runs, whenever it chooses not\nto pick a potion, strategy `S` also can postpone this pick – it never needs\na potion of this type before the next monster of the same type.\nWhen the greedy simulation is forced to pick a potion, every strategy\nmust pick one at that moment, otherwise it would fail at the upcoming\nmonster.  \nThus the greedy simulation never picks a potion that some legal strategy\nskips, and never skips a potion that any legal strategy must pick.\nConsequently it never fails where a legal strategy succeeds and\nconsequently it returns *True*. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nLet `K_min` be the smallest `K` for which a strategy exists.\nThe binary search ends with `lo = K_min`.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nThe predicate “`possible(K)` succeeds” is monotone:\nif it succeeds for `K`, it also succeeds for all larger values\n(because we only increase the allowance).  \nBinary search on a monotone predicate always yields the minimal true\nvalue. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe program prints `-1` iff it is impossible to defeat all monsters.  \nOtherwise it prints the minimum possible maximum number of potions\n`K_min` and a valid sequence of actions achieving this bound.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n*Impossible case:*  \nIf no strategy exists, predicate “`possible(K)` succeeds” is false for all\n`K` in `[0,N]`.  \nBinary search will end with `lo = N+1` (after the loop we check\n`possible(hi)`).  The program detects this and prints `-1`.\n\n*Possible case:*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;4 the binary search returns `K_min`.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the call `possible(K_min)` succeeds,\nand by Lemma&nbsp;2 the returned strategy is valid and uses at most\n`K_min` potions.  \nNo strategy can use a smaller bound, again by monotonicity.\nHence the printed `K_min` and the actions are correct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Complexity Analysis\n\nLet `N` be the number of events (≤ 200 000).\n\n*Checking a fixed `K`* is linear: `O(N)` time, `O(N)` memory for the\narrays `remaining`, `inventory`, and the answer list.\n\n*Binary search* needs `⌈log₂(N+1)⌉ ≤ 19` iterations.\n\n```\nTotal time   :  O(N log N)   ≤ 4·10⁶ operations\nTotal memory :  O(N)\n```\n\nBoth fit easily into the limits.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().strip().split())\n    try:\n        N = int(next(it))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    events = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        t = int(next(it))\n        x = int(next(it)) - 1          # 0‑based type\n        events.append((t, x))\n\n    # pre‑compute total monsters per type\n    total_monsters = [0] * N\n    for t, x in events:\n        if t == 2:\n            total_monsters[x] += 1\n\n    # binary search for minimal K\n    def possible(K: int):\n        remaining = total_monsters[:]     # monsters still to come\n        inventory = [0] * N\n        total = 0\n        actions = []                      # only for potion events\n        for t, x in events:\n            if t == 1:                    # potion\n                if inventory[x] == 0 and remaining[x] > 0:\n                    if total + 1 > K:\n                        return False, []\n                    inventory[x] += 1\n                    total += 1\n                    actions.append('1')\n                else:\n                    actions.append('0')\n            else:                         # monster\n                if inventory[x] == 0:\n                    return False, []\n                inventory[x] -= 1\n                total -= 1\n                remaining[x] -= 1\n        return True, actions\n\n    lo, hi = 0, N\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        ok, _ = possible(mid)\n        if ok:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    Kmin = lo\n    ok, actions = possible(Kmin)\n    if not ok:\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        print(Kmin)\n        print(' '.join(actions))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input/output format.",
