      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every event  \n\n* `t = 1 , x` – a potion of type `x` appears.  \n  Takahashi may **pick it up** (`1`) or **discard** it (`0`).  \n\n* `t = 2 , x` – a monster of type `x` appears.  \n  He must have a previously **picked‑up** potion of the same type,\n  the potion is consumed and disappears.\n\nIf at some monster Takahashi has no such potion, the adventure fails.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Observations\n\n* For one fixed type `x` the events that involve this type are a\n  sequence of\n\n```\n…  (pick) … (pick) … (monster) … (monster) …\n```\n\n  A monster can use any *earlier* picked potion of the same type,\n  each potion only once.\n\n* The whole adventure succeeds **iff** for every type `x`\n\n```\nfor every prefix of the whole event list\n      #monsters of type x   ≤   #picked potions of type x\n```\n\n  (otherwise a monster would appear without a potion).\n\n* The number of potions that Takahashi actually carries at a moment is\n\n```\ntotal picked potions   –   total already used potions\n```\n\n  i.e. the number of *un‑consumed* picked potions.\n  While we are scanning the events we can keep this number as a variable\n  `cur`.  \n  `K = max cur` over the whole scan.\n\n* **Goal** – among all successful strategies minimise `K`.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   The optimal way to match potions and monsters  \n\nFor one type `x` look only at its own events.\nTake the list of positions of its pickups and monsters.\n\n```\npick  pick  monster  pick  monster  monster …\n```\n\nIf we match a monster with *any* earlier pickup,\nthe whole potion stays in the inventory from the moment of pickup\nuntil the monster that finally uses it.\nThe **shortest possible stay** is obtained by matching a monster\nwith the **nearest earlier** (i.e. the most recent) still‑unused\npickup.  \nDoing this for all monsters of this type is exactly the usual\nstack (LIFO) algorithm:\n\n```\nscan the whole event list from left to right\n    if it is a pickup of type x : push its index on a stack[x]\n    if it is a monster of type x :\n          if stack[x] empty → impossible\n          pop the top index → this potion will be used\n```\n\n*Why is it optimal?*  \nAssume a monster uses a pickup that is **not** the most recent one.\nThen there exists an unused (still waiting) newer pickup.\nExchanging the two uses makes the older potion disappear **later**\nand the newer potion disappear **earlier**,\ntherefore the number of simultaneous potions never increases,\nand the peak `K` does not become larger.\nRepeating the exchange eliminates all such “crossings”.\nConsequently the stack‑matching is the unique matching that\nminimises the lifetime of every potion, hence minimises the peak\ninventory `K`.\n\nThus:\n\n* a successful adventure exists **iff** the stack algorithm never\n  tries to pop from an empty stack,\n* the set of pickups that have to be taken is exactly the set of\n  indices that are popped during the algorithm,\n* the inventory size at any moment equals the total size of all stacks\n  **after** we have decided “take this pickup” (= popped later) but\n  **before** the matching monster consumes it.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Computing the answer\n\nThe algorithm consists of two linear passes.\n\n*First pass – decide which pickups are taken.*\n\n```\nstack[x] – ordinary Python list, works as a stack\nused[i]  – boolean, true ⇔ the i‑th event (a pickup) will be taken\nfor i = 0 … N-1\n        if t[i] == 1:\n                stack[x].append(i)                # just remember the index\n        else:   # monster\n                if stack[x] empty → print -1, exit\n                idx = stack[x].pop()\n                used[idx] = True                  # this potion must be taken\n```\n\nAll pickups that stay in a stack after the scan are never needed –\nthey will be discarded (`used = False`).\n\n*Second pass – simulate the adventure with the decided pickups\nand obtain the minimal possible `K`.*\n\n```\ncur = 0                # current number of potions in the bag\nK   = 0\npending[x] = []        # stack of taken but not yet used potions\nfor i = 0 … N-1\n        if t[i] == 1:\n                if used[i]:\n                        pending[x].append(i)\n                        cur += 1\n        else:   # monster\n                idx = pending[x].pop()   # always exists because first pass succeeded\n                cur -= 1\n        K = max(K, cur)\n```\n\n`K` obtained this way is the minimal possible maximum inventory,\ni.e. `K_min`.\n\n*Output*  \n\n* first line – `K`\n* second line – for every event with `t = 1` (in the original order)\n  output `1` if `used[i]` else `0`, separated by blanks.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints `-1` iff the adventure is impossible,\notherwise it prints the minimum possible `K` and a concrete optimal\nstrategy.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nDuring the first pass, when a monster of type `x` is processed,\nthe stack `stack[x]` contains exactly the indices of pickups of type\n`x` that appear **before** the monster and are still unmatched\nby earlier monsters.\n\n**Proof.**  \nInitially the stack is empty.\n*When a pickup of type `x` is seen* we push its index – it is unmatched.\n*When a monster of type `x` is seen* we pop the most recent index,\nwhich corresponds to the newest unmatched pickup, and therefore\nmatches this monster.\nNo other operation touches `stack[x]`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nIf the first pass ever tries to pop from an empty `stack[x]`,\nno strategy can defeat all monsters.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAt that moment there are more monsters of type `x` among the already\nprocessed events than pickups of type `x` among the same events,\nbecause every earlier monster removed exactly one earlier pickup\n(Lemma&nbsp;1).  \nAny potion usable for the current monster must come from a pickup\nthat appears **before** it, which does not exist. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nIf the first pass finishes without failure, the set  \n`U = { i | used[i] = True }` (picked‑up potions) together with the\nmatching “monster ↔ popped pickup” is a feasible strategy.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy construction each monster pops exactly one index `idx` from the\ncorresponding stack, and we set `used[idx]=True`.  \nAll popped indices are different, therefore each potion is used at most\nonce.  \nBecause a monster can pop only a pickup that appeared earlier\n(Lemma&nbsp;1), the potion is available when needed.\nHence the described actions defeat every monster. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nFor each type `x` the matching produced by the first pass (monster\nmatched with the most recent earlier unmatched pickup) minimises the\nmaximum number of *simultaneously* carried potions among all possible\nmatchings for this type.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider any feasible matching `M`.  \nIf a monster uses a pickup that is *not* the most recent unmatched one,\nthere exists a newer unmatched pickup `p_new`.\nReplace in `M` the old pair (monster, old pickup) by (monster, `p_new`)\nand let the old pickup be used later (it must exist because the old\npickup was unmatched before the monster).  \nDuring the time interval between the monster and the later use,\nthe number of carried potions does **not increase** (the newer potion\ndisappears earlier, the older later).  \nRepeating the replacement removes all such “crossings”.\nThe resulting matching is exactly the stack (LIFO) matching and never\nhas a larger peak inventory than the original `M`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 5  \nLet `K_alg` be the value `K` obtained in the second pass.\nFor every feasible strategy the maximal inventory size is at least\n`K_alg`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix a type `x`.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;4 the stack matching for this type has the smallest possible\npeak number of *un‑consumed* potions of type `x` among all strategies.\nSummation over all types preserves the inequality, because at any moment\nthe total inventory is the sum of the per‑type inventories.\nThe second pass computes exactly that sum for the stack matching,\ntherefore no other strategy can have a smaller global maximum. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 6  \nThe strategy described by the arrays `used[i]` (pick‑up = 1, otherwise 0)\nrealises the maximal inventory size `K_alg`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nDuring the second pass we simulated exactly that strategy:\n* a pickup with `used = 1` increases the bag size,\n* the monster consumes the most recent still‑unused potion of the same\n  type (the same rule as in the first pass).\nThus the variable `cur` records the real bag size at each moment,\nand `K_alg = max cur`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe program outputs  \n\n* `-1` **iff** Takahashi cannot defeat all monsters,  \n\n* otherwise the printed number `K` equals the minimal possible maximum\n  inventory `K_min`, and the printed sequence of 0/1 tells a strategy\n  that achieves this optimum.\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*If the program prints `-1`.*  \nThat happens only when the first pass tries to pop from an empty stack.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 no feasible strategy exists, so the answer really is `-1`.\n\n*If the program does not print `-1`.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the constructed `used` array describes a feasible strategy.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;6 its maximal inventory equals `K_alg` (the printed `K`).\nLemma&nbsp;5 shows that no other feasible strategy can have a smaller\nmaximum inventory.\nHence `K = K_alg = K_min` and the printed actions are optimal. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n*First pass* – one push or pop per event: **O(N)** time, **O(N)**\nmemory for the `used` array and the stacks.\n\n*Second pass* – again O(N) time, O(N) extra memory for the\n`pending` stacks (they can reuse the same containers).\n\nOverall  \n\n```\nTime   :  O(N)   ( ≤ 2·10⁵ )\nMemory :  O(N)\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().split())\n    try:\n        N = int(next(it))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    t = [0] * N\n    x = [0] * N\n    for i in range(N):\n        t[i] = int(next(it))\n        x[i] = int(next(it))\n\n    # ---------- first pass : decide which pickups are needed ----------\n    stacks = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]          # temporary stacks\n    used = [False] * N                           # will be True iff we pick it up\n\n    for i in range(N):\n        typ = t[i]\n        tp = x[i]\n        if typ == 1:                     # potion appears\n            stacks[tp].append(i)        # remember its index\n        else:                            # monster\n            if not stacks[tp]:          # no earlier potion of this type\n                print(-1)\n                return\n            idx = stacks[tp].pop()\n            used[idx] = True             # this potion must be taken\n\n    # ---------- second pass : simulate and obtain minimal K ----------\n    pending = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]   # stacks of still‑alive taken potions\n    cur = 0\n    K = 0\n    for i in range(N):\n        typ = t[i]\n        tp = x[i]\n        if typ == 1:\n            if used[i]:\n                pending[tp].append(i)\n                cur += 1\n        else:\n            # monster consumes the most recent taken potion of this type\n            pending[tp].pop()\n            cur -= 1\n        if cur > K:\n            K = cur\n\n    # ---------- output ----------\n    out = []\n    out.append(str(K))\n    pick_seq = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        if t[i] == 1:\n            pick_seq.append('1' if used[i] else '0')\n    out.append(' '.join(pick_seq))\n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(out))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input‑output format.",
