      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    events = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        t = int(data[index])\n        x = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        events.append((t, x))\n    \n    # Track potions by type\n    potion_count = defaultdict(int)\n    # Track monster encounters and their order\n    monster_events = []\n    potion_events = []\n    \n    # First pass: check if all monsters can be defeated\n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 2:\n            monster_events.append((x, i))\n        else:\n            potion_events.append((x, i))\n    \n    # For each monster, we need a potion of same type available at that time\n    # We'll simulate greedily: when we see a monster, if we have the potion, use it.\n    # If not, we fail.\n    # But we also need to decide which potions to pick up to minimize max inventory.\n    \n    # We'll use a greedy strategy: for each monster, we must have a potion of type x available.\n    # We can postpone picking up potions until we see a monster that needs it.\n    # However, we must pick up potions before the monster that requires them.\n    \n    # We'll do: for each monster event, we need to have at least one potion of type x picked up before it.\n    # We can simulate backwards: for each monster, we require one potion of type x from the potions that occur before it.\n    # We want to minimize the maximum number of potions held at any time.\n    \n    # Strategy:\n    # For each potion type, collect all potion events and monster events (by time).\n    # For each monster event of type x, we need to assign one potion event of type x that occurs before it.\n    # We want to use the \"latest possible\" potion event for each monster to leave earlier potions for other monsters.\n    # This minimizes the number of potions we hold at once.\n    \n    # Step 1: For each type, collect potion indices and monster indices.\n    type_potions = defaultdict(list)\n    type_monsters = defaultdict(list)\n    \n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            type_potions[x].append(i)\n        else:\n            type_monsters[x].append(i)\n    \n    # Check: for each monster type, we must have at least as many potions as monsters before it.\n    for x in type_monsters:\n        if len(type_potions[x]) < len(type_monsters[x]):\n            print(-1)\n            return\n    \n    # Now, we simulate: for each monster event, we assign the latest available potion that hasn't been used yet and occurs before the monster.\n    # This is optimal to minimize inventory: we delay picking up potions as much as possible.\n    \n    # We'll create an array to record which potions are picked (1) or not (0)\n    pick = [0] * len(potion_events)\n    potion_event_index = 0\n    potion_to_event_map = {}  # maps potion event index to position in potion_events list\n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            potion_to_event_map[i] = potion_event_index\n            potion_event_index += 1\n    \n    # For each monster event, we need to assign a potion event of same type that occurs before it.\n    # We'll use a greedy matching: for each monster event (in order), assign the latest potion event of that type that is not yet assigned and occurs before it.\n    # But we need to assign in increasing time order of monsters.\n    \n    # We'll maintain for each type a list of available potion events (by index) that occur before the current monster.\n    # We'll process monsters in chronological order.\n    used_potions = set()\n    potion_assignment = {}  # monster_index -> potion_index (the potion event used)\n    \n    # For each type, we'll have a list of potion indices (sorted) and we'll use a pointer for the latest available.\n    # Actually, we can do: for each monster event in order, we choose the latest potion event of that type that is not used and before the monster.\n    # We'll precompute for each type the list of potion events.\n    \n    for x in type_monsters:\n        type_potions[x].sort()  # already sorted by index, but ensure\n    \n    # We'll create a list of monster events sorted by time\n    all_monsters = []\n    for x in type_monsters:\n        for mon_time in type_monsters[x]:\n            all_monsters.append((mon_time, x))\n    all_monsters.sort()  # by time\n    \n    # For each type, we maintain a pointer to the next available potion (from the end backwards)\n    # We'll use a greedy: for each monster in order, we pick the latest potion event of that type that is not used and before the monster.\n    # We'll use a stack-like approach: for each type, we have a list of potion events (in increasing time), and we want to assign the rightmost one that is not used.\n    # Actually, we can use a pointer from the end of the potion list for each type.\n    # We'll create a copy of potion lists and use a pointer for each type that moves from right to left.\n    potion_pointers = {}\n    for x in type_potions:\n        potion_pointers[x] = len(type_potions[x]) - 1  # start from last\n    \n    # We'll go through monsters in chronological order\n    for mon_time, x in all_monsters:\n        # Find the latest potion event of type x that is before mon_time and not used\n        while potion_pointers[x] >= 0 and type_potions[x][potion_pointers[x]] >= mon_time:\n            potion_pointers[x] -= 1\n        if potion_pointers[x] < 0:\n            print(-1)\n            return\n        # Assign this potion to the monster\n        assigned_potion_index = type_potions[x][potion_pointers[x]]\n        potion_assignment[mon_time] = assigned_potion_index\n        used_potions.add(assigned_potion_index)\n        potion_pointers[x] -= 1  # use this potion and move to next available (earlier)\n    \n    # Now, we know which potion events to pick: the ones that are assigned to monsters\n    # But note: we might have extra potions that we can pick to help with future monsters? \n    # However, we want to minimize the maximum inventory. The minimal strategy is to pick only the potions that are necessary.\n    # But we must pick the ones that are assigned. However, we might pick more? But we want minimal max inventory.\n    # The above assignment uses exactly the minimal set of potions needed: each monster gets one potion, and we use the latest possible to avoid holding extra ones.\n    # However, we might have potions that are not assigned but we can pick them to help with future monsters? \n    # But we already assigned the latest available potion for each monster. So any extra potion we pick would only increase the inventory.\n    # So we only pick the ones that are assigned to monsters.\n    \n    # But wait: what if a potion is not assigned to any monster? Then we don't need to pick it. We can discard it.\n    # So we set pick[i] = 1 only if the potion event is assigned to a monster.\n    \n    for i in range(len(potion_events)):\n        event_time = potion_events[i][0]  # the actual time index in events\n        if event_time in used_potions:\n            pick[i] = 1\n        else:\n            pick[i] = 0\n    \n    # Now, we need to compute K_min: the maximum number of potions held at any time.\n    # We simulate the events in order and count the inventory.\n    inventory = 0\n    max_inventory = 0\n    # We need to know which potion events are picked (we have the pick array for potion_events)\n    # But we also need to know the time order of all events.\n    # We'll create an array for the entire sequence of events: for each event, what is the action?\n    # We'll simulate from time 0 to n-1.\n    potion_used = set()  # the potion event indices that are picked and assigned (used_potions)\n    # We'll create a list of events with their type and whether we pick the potion\n    # We have