over time, we need to know when potions are picked and when used.\n    # We can precompute for each monster which selected potion is used for it.\n    # Then, during simulation, when we see a potion event that is selected, count++.\n    # When we see a monster event, count-- (because we use a potion).\n    # The maximum count is K.\n    \n    # Precompute assignment: for each type, assign potions to monsters.\n    assigned_potion_for_monster = {}  # map monster index -> selected potion index used for it\n    for x in range(1, N+1):\n        if x not in monsters:\n            continue\n        m_list = sorted(monsters[x])\n        p_list = sorted(potions[x])\n        # Selected potions for this type: the ones we selected earlier.\n        # We need to know which potions are selected for this type.\n        # We can collect selected potions for this type from selected_potion_indices.\n        selected_for_x = [p for p in sorted(p_list) if p in selected_potion_indices]\n        # Now, assign: i-th monster (in order) gets the i-th selected potion.\n        for i, m in enumerate(m_list):\n            assigned_potion_for_monster[m] = selected_for_x[i]\n    \n    # Now simulate.\n    count = 0\n    max_count = 0\n    # We'll also need to output for each potion event whether we pick it.\n    # We have potion_event_indices in order. We can create an array of zeros of length equal\n    # to number of potion events, then set to 1 if the index is in selected_potion_indices.\n    potion_actions = [0] * len(potion_event_indices)\n    # Map from event index to position in potion_actions.\n    event_to_potion_action_pos = {}\n    for pos, idx in enumerate(potion_event_indices):\n        event_to_potion_action_pos[idx] = pos\n    \n    # Also, we need to track for each monster, which potion we use, but for count, we just need\n    # to know that at monster event, we use a potion.\n    # However, we must ensure that the potion we use is indeed picked before the monster.\n    # Since we assigned based on indices, it should be.\n    # But during simulation, when we encounter a potion event, if it is selected, we pick it.\n    # When we encounter a monster event, we use a potion, but we don't need to specify which one\n    # for the count. But we should check that we have a potion of that type available.\n    # We can maintain for each type a count of available selected potions.\n    available = defaultdict(int)\n    for idx, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            if idx in selected_potion_indices:\n                # pick it\n                available[x] += 1\n                count += 1\n                potion_actions[event_to_potion_action_pos[idx]] = 1\n            # else, discard\n        else:  # t == 2, monster\n            if available[x] == 0:\n                # This should not happen if selection is correct.\n                print(-1)\n                return\n            available[x] -= 1\n            count -= 1\n        if count > max_count:\n            max_count = count\n    \n    # Output\n    print(max_count)\n    print(' '.join(map(str, potion_actions)))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
