      "generated_answer": "**Python function**\n\n```python\ndef count_submultisets(nums, l, r):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the number of sub‑multisets of `nums` whose element sum is in [l, r].\n    The result is given modulo 10**9 + 7.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    nums : List[int]\n        The input array (0‑indexed, non‑negative integers).\n    l, r : int\n        Inclusive lower and upper bounds for the required sums.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Count of sub‑multisets with sum in the given range, modulo 1_000_000_007.\n    \"\"\"\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    # we never need sums larger than r\n    MAX = r\n\n    from collections import Counter\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n\n    # dp[s] = number of ways to obtain sum s using processed values\n    dp = [0] * (MAX + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1                         # empty multiset\n\n    for v, cnt in freq.items():\n        if v == 0:\n            # adding any number of zeros (0..cnt) multiplies every count by (cnt+1)\n            mult = (cnt + 1) % MOD\n            for i in range(MAX + 1):\n                dp[i] = dp[i] * mult % MOD\n            continue\n\n        # bounded knapsack using sliding‑window DP (O(MAX) per distinct value)\n        new_dp = [0] * (MAX + 1)\n        for rem in range(v):\n            window = 0\n            # iterate over indices i = rem + k*v\n            for k, i in enumerate(range(rem, MAX + 1, v)):\n                window = (window + dp[i]) % MOD\n                # keep only the last (cnt+1) elements in the window\n                if k > cnt:\n                    sub_idx = i - (cnt + 1) * v\n                    window = (window - dp[sub_idx]) % MOD\n                new_dp[i] = window\n        dp = new_dp\n\n    # sum the answers for all sums inside [l, r]\n    ans = sum(dp[l:r + 1]) % MOD\n    return ans\n```",
