      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor a fixed window width `W` we can check how many lines are necessary\nif we always put the next word on the current line whenever it still fits,\notherwise we start a new line.\nThe greedy rule is optimal because a line never becomes *shorter* by moving a\nword to the next line – it can only increase the number of lines.\n\nThe predicate  \n\n```\ncan(W) = “the sentence fits in ≤ M lines with width W”\n```\n\nis monotone:\n\n*if `can(W)` is true, it is also true for any larger width*,\nbecause a larger window never makes a previously feasible layout infeasible.\n\nTherefore the minimal width can be found by binary searching the smallest `W`\nwith `can(W) = true`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Formalisation  \n\n* `L[i]` – length of the *i*-th word (`1‑based`), `1 ≤ i ≤ N`.\n* `space = 1` – width of the gap between two consecutive words on the same line.\n\nFor a given `W`\n\n```\nlines = 1\ncur = 0                         # width already used on current line\nfor each word length x in L:\n        if cur == 0:            # first word on a line\n                cur = x\n        else if cur + space + x ≤ W:\n                cur += space + x\n        else:\n                lines += 1\n                cur = x\nreturn lines\n```\n\n`can(W)` is true iff the returned `lines ≤ M`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Bounds for the binary search  \n\n*Lower bound* : a single word must fit → `low = max(L[i])`.\n\n*Upper bound* : put everything on one line →  \n`high = sum(L[i]) + (N-1)` (the total length of all spaces).\n\nBoth bounds fit easily into 64‑bit (`≤ 2·10⁵·10⁹ + 2·10⁵`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread N, M and the list L\nlow  = max(L)\nhigh = sum(L) + (N-1)\n\nwhile low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if lines_needed(mid) <= M:     # can(mid) is true\n                high = mid\n        else:\n                low = mid + 1\n\nprint(low)        # the minimal feasible width\n```\n\n`lines_needed` implements the greedy simulation described above.\nThe loop runs `O(log(high-low))` ≤ about 60 iterations\n(`high` ≤ 2·10¹⁴) and each iteration scans the `N` words once.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the minimum window width that allows the\nsentence to be displayed in at most `M` lines.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor a fixed width `W` the greedy procedure described in `lines_needed`\nproduces the minimum possible number of lines among all layouts respecting `W`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider any layout that respects `W`.  \nTraverse the words from left to right; whenever the layout starts a new line,\nthe greedy algorithm also starts a new line (it cannot place the word on the\ncurrent line because the current line is already full: otherwise the layout\ncould have placed it there as well, contradicting the definition of a new line).\nThus the greedy algorithm never uses *more* lines than the arbitrary layout.\n∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \n`can(W)` (i.e. `lines_needed(W) ≤ M`) is monotone:\nif `can(W₀)` holds and `W₁ ≥ W₀` then `can(W₁)` also holds.\n\n**Proof.**  \nIncreasing the allowed line width cannot invalidate a layout that was legal\nfor the smaller width, because all line widths stay ≤ `W₀ ≤ W₁`.  \nHence any feasible layout for `W₀` is also feasible for `W₁`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nWhen the binary search terminates, `low` ( = `high`) is feasible\n(`can(low)` is true) and every `W < low` is infeasible.\n\n**Proof.**  \nInvariant of the loop:\n\n```\nall widths < low are infeasible,\nall widths ≥ high are feasible.\n```\n\n*Initialization* : `low = max(L)` is obviously infeasible for any smaller\nvalue, and `high` (all words on one line) is feasible, so the invariant holds.\n\n*Maintenance* :  \nLet `mid = (low+high)//2`.\n\n* If `can(mid)` is true, we set `high = mid`.  \n  All widths `≥ mid` stay feasible, those `< low` stay infeasible;\n  the invariant still holds.\n\n* If `can(mid)` is false, we set `low = mid+1`.  \n  Now every width `< low` (i.e. `≤ mid`) is infeasible, and widths `≥ high`\n  are unchanged feasible. Invariant preserved.\n\n*Termination* : loop stops when `low == high`. By the invariant,\n  `low` is feasible and any smaller width is infeasible. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nThe value printed by the algorithm equals the minimum feasible width.\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;3 the printed value `W* = low` is feasible, and no smaller width\nis feasible. Hence `W*` is exactly the minimum feasible width. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm outputs the minimum possible window width such that the sentence\ncan be displayed in at most `M` lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the procedure `lines_needed` computes the optimal (minimal) line\ncount for any width.  \nUsing this procedure inside the binary search together with Lemma&nbsp;2 yields a\nmonotone predicate, enabling correct binary search.  \nLemmas&nbsp;3 and&nbsp;4 prove that the binary search returns precisely the minimal\nfeasible width. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\n*`lines_needed`* scans `N` words → `O(N)` time, `O(1)` extra memory.  \nBinary search performs at most `⌈log₂(high−low+1)⌉` iterations  \n(`high−low ≤ 2·10¹⁴`) → ≤ 48–49 iterations (actually ≤ 60 with safe bound).\n\nTotal time: `O(N log ΣL)` ≤ `2·10⁵ · 60` ≈ `1.2·10⁷` operations.  \nMemory usage: `O(N)` for the input list (the algorithm itself is `O(1)`).\n\nBoth satisfy the limits easily.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n    L = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(N)]\n\n    # lower and upper bounds for the answer\n    low = max(L)                       # each word must fit alone\n    high = sum(L) + (N - 1)            # all words on one line\n\n    # helper: number of lines needed for a given width\n    def lines_needed(W: int) -> int:\n        lines = 1\n        cur = 0\n        for x in L:\n            if cur == 0:               # start of a line\n                cur = x\n            elif cur + 1 + x <= W:    # fits with a preceding space\n                cur += 1 + x\n            else:                      # start new line\n                lines += 1\n                cur = x\n        return lines\n\n    # binary search for minimal feasible width\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if lines_needed(mid) <= M:\n            high = mid\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1\n\n    print(low)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms\nto the required input‑output format.",
