    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_169",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an array of positive integers nums of length n.\nA polygon is a closed plane figure that has at least 3 sides. The longest side of a polygon is smaller than the sum of its other sides.\nConversely, if you have k (k >= 3) positive real numbers a_1, a_2, a_3, ..., a_k where a_1 <= a_2 <= a_3 <= ... <= a_k and a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + ... + a_k-1 > a_k, then there always exists a polygon with k sides whose lengths are a_1, a_2, a_3, ..., a_k.\nThe perimeter of a polygon is the sum of lengths of its sides.\nReturn the largest possible perimeter of a polygon whose sides can be formed from nums, or -1 if it is not possible to create a polygon.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [5,5,5]\nOutput: 15\nExplanation: The only possible polygon that can be made from nums has 3 sides: 5, 5, and 5. The perimeter is 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,12,1,2,5,50,3]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: The polygon with the largest perimeter which can be made from nums has 5 sides: 1, 1, 2, 3, and 5. The perimeter is 1 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 5 = 12.\nWe cannot have a polygon with either 12 or 50 as the longest side because it is not possible to include 2 or more smaller sides that have a greater sum than either of them.\nIt can be shown that the largest possible perimeter is 12.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [5,5,50]\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: There is no possible way to form a polygon from nums, as a polygon has at least 3 sides and 50 > 5 + 5.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n3 <= n <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor a set of side lengths `a1 ≤ a2 ≤ … ≤ ak` ( `k ≥ 3` ) a polygon exists **iff**\n\n```\na1 + a2 + … + a(k‑1)  >  ak                (1)\n```\n\nThe left side is the sum of all sides *except* the longest one.\nIf we call `S = a1 + … + ak` the total perimeter, condition (1) is equivalent to  \n\n```\nS  >  2·ak                                   (2)\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.  Observations  \n\n* To obtain the **largest** possible perimeter we want to keep as many\n  large numbers as we can.\n* After sorting the original array in **descending** order  \n\n  `b0 ≥ b1 ≥ … ≥ b(n‑1)`  \n\n  the longest side of any subset that starts at index `i`\n  is exactly `bi`.\n* If we keep the whole suffix `bi , bi+1 , … , b(n‑1)` its perimeter is  \n\n  `suffixSum(i) = totalSum – prefixSum(i‑1)`  \n\n  and condition (2) becomes  \n\n  `suffixSum(i) > 2·bi`.                       (3)\n\n* If condition (3) is **false** for the whole suffix,\n  removing any other element (which only reduces the sum of the\n  remaining sides) can never make it true.\n  Therefore a *feasible* subset with longest side `bi` must contain the\n  whole suffix.\n\nConsequences  \n\n* The optimal polygon, if it exists, is the **first** suffix (starting at\n  the smallest possible index) that satisfies (3) and has at least three\n  elements.\n* Because we scan the suffixes from left to right, the first feasible one\n  already yields the maximum perimeter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Algorithm\n```\nlargestPolygonPerimeter(nums):\n    sort nums descending → arr\n    total = sum(arr)\n    prefix = 0\n    for i from 0 to len(arr)-3:          # at least 3 elements must stay\n        # suffix = total - prefix\n        suffix = total - prefix\n        if suffix > 2 * arr[i]:          # condition (3)\n            return suffix                # maximal perimeter found\n        prefix += arr[i]                 # drop the current largest side\n    return -1\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns the required answer.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor a fixed index `i` (largest side `arr[i]`), if the whole suffix\n`arr[i … n‑1]` does **not** satisfy `suffix > 2·arr[i]`,\nthen **no** subset that contains `arr[i]` can satisfy the polygon\ncondition.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAny such subset is `arr[i]` together with a (possibly empty) set `T`\ntaken from `{arr[i+1], … , arr[n‑1]}`.\nIts perimeter is `arr[i] + sum(T)`.  \nThe sum of the other sides is `sum(T)`.\nIf the whole suffix fails, we have  \n\n```\narr[i] + sum(all later elements) ≤ 2·arr[i]   →   sum(all later) ≤ arr[i]\n```\n\nSince `sum(T) ≤ sum(all later)`, we get `sum(T) ≤ arr[i]`,\nhence `sum(T) ≤ arr[i]` which contradicts the required strict inequality\n`sum(T) > arr[i]`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nIf the whole suffix `arr[i … n‑1]` satisfies `suffix > 2·arr[i]`,\nthen the polygon formed by **exactly that suffix** is feasible.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe longest side of the suffix is `arr[i]`.  \nAll other sides sum to `suffix – arr[i]`.  \nFrom `suffix > 2·arr[i]` we get `suffix – arr[i] > arr[i]`.  \nThus the polygon inequality holds, and the suffix is a valid polygon. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nLet `i*` be the smallest index for which the suffix satisfies\n`suffix > 2·arr[i]`.  \nThe perimeter `suffix(i*)` is **no smaller** than the perimeter of any\nfeasible polygon that can be built from `nums`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider any feasible polygon `P`.  \nLet `j` be the index of its longest side in the sorted descending array.\nBecause `P` is feasible, by Lemma&nbsp;2 the whole suffix starting at `j`\nmust also be feasible, therefore `suffix(j) > 2·arr[j]`.  \nSince `i*` is the *smallest* index with that property, we have `i* ≤ j`.  \nSuffixes are nested: `suffix(i*)` contains `suffix(j)` and possibly more\nelements, therefore  \n\n```\nsuffix(i*)  ≥  suffix(j)  ≥  perimeter(P)\n```\n∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nThe algorithm returns `suffix(i*)` if such an index exists, otherwise `-1`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe loop examines indices `i = 0,1,…,n‑3` in increasing order.\n`prefix` holds the sum of elements before `i`, therefore `suffix = total‑prefix`\nequals `suffix(i)`.  \nWhen the condition `suffix > 2·arr[i]` becomes true,\nthe algorithm returns `suffix`.  \nBecause the indices are processed in order, the first returned suffix\ncorresponds exactly to `i*`.  \nIf the loop finishes without returning, no index fulfills the condition,\nhence no feasible polygon exists and the algorithm returns `-1`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \n`largestPolygonPerimeter` returns  \n\n* the maximum possible perimeter of a polygon whose side lengths are taken\n  from `nums`, or  \n* `-1` if no polygon can be formed.\n\n**Proof.**  \nIf the algorithm returns a positive value, by Lemma&nbsp;4 it equals\n`suffix(i*)`. By Lemma&nbsp;2 this suffix forms a valid polygon,\nso the returned value is attainable.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 no other feasible polygon has a larger perimeter,\ntherefore the returned value is optimal.\n\nIf the algorithm returns `-1`, Lemma&nbsp;4 tells us that no index\nsatisfies `suffix > 2·arr[i]`. By Lemma&nbsp;1 this means no subset with\nat least three sides can satisfy the polygon inequality, i.e. no polygon\ncan be built. Hence `-1` is correct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Sorting* : `O(n log n)`  \n*Single linear scan* : `O(n)`  \n\nOverall time `O(n log n)`, memory `O(1)` besides the input array\n(and the sorted copy, which can be done in‑place).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Reference Implementation  \n\n```python\ndef largestPolygonPerimeter(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the largest possible perimeter of a polygon that can be formed\n    from a subset of `nums`. If impossible, returns -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. sort descending\n    nums.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    total = sum(nums)          # total sum of all elements\n    prefix = 0                 # sum of elements that have been \"dropped\"\n\n    n = len(nums)\n    # need at least 3 sides left -> i <= n-3\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        suffix = total - prefix          # sum of nums[i .. n-1]\n        if suffix > 2 * nums[i]:         # polygon inequality satisfied\n            return suffix                # maximal perimeter found\n        prefix += nums[i]                # drop current largest side\n\n    return -1\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input‑output format.",
