    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef medianOfUniquenessArray(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    To find the median of the uniqueness array, we note that the uniqueness array\n    contains the number of distinct elements for every subarray. There are n*(n+1)/2 \n    subarrays. The median is the k-th smallest element, where k = (total_subarrays + 1) // 2.\n    \n    We can binary search for the smallest value X such that the number of subarrays \n    with at most X distinct elements is at least k.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    k = (n * (n + 1) // 2 + 1) // 2\n    \n    def count_subarrays_with_at_most(x: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Counts the number of subarrays with at most x distinct elements\n        using a sliding window approach in O(n) time.\n        \"\"\"\n        count = 0\n        left = 0\n        distinct_elements = 0\n        freq = {}\n        \n        for right in range(n):\n            # Add element at right pointer\n            val_r = nums[right]\n            freq[val_r] = freq.get(val_r, 0) + 1\n            if freq[val_r] == 1:\n                distinct_elements += 1\n            \n            # Shrink window from left if distinct count exceeds x\n            while distinct_elements > x:\n                val_l = nums[left]\n                freq[val_l] -= 1\n                if freq[val_l] == 0:\n                    distinct_elements -= 1\n                left += 1\n            \n            # Subarrays ending at right with at most x distinct elements\n            count += (right - left + 1)\n            \n        return count\n    \n    # Binary search for the smallest X\n    low = 1\n    high = len(set(nums))\n    ans = high\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if count_subarrays_with_at_most(mid) >= k:\n            ans = mid\n            high = mid - 1\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1\n            \n    return ans\n\n```",
