      "generated_answer": "I need to solve this step by step.\n\n**Problem Understanding:**\n- I have an array of chocolate prices\n- I have a certain amount of money\n- I must buy exactly 2 chocolates\n- I want to minimize the sum of the two chocolates I buy\n- I need to have non-negative leftover money\n- If I can't buy 2 chocolates without going into debt, return the original money amount\n\n**Approach:**\n1. To minimize the sum of two chocolates, I should pick the two cheapest chocolates\n2. Find the two smallest prices in the array\n3. Calculate the total cost of these two cheapest chocolates\n4. If this total cost is less than or equal to my money, return money - total_cost\n5. Otherwise, return the original money (can't afford two chocolates)\n\n**Steps to find two smallest prices:**\n- I can sort the array and take the first two elements\n- Or I can iterate through the array once to find the two smallest values\n\nSince the array size is small (≤ 50), sorting is fine.\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. Sort the prices array\n2. Take the sum of the first two elements (smallest prices)\n3. If sum ≤ money, return money - sum\n4. Otherwise, return money\n\n**Let me trace through examples:**\n\nExample 1: prices = [1,2,2], money = 3\n- Sorted: [1,2,2]\n- Two smallest: 1 + 2 = 3\n- 3 ≤ 3, so return 3 - 3 = 0 ✓\n\nExample 2: prices = [3,2,3], money = 3\n- Sorted: [2,3,3]\n- Two smallest: 2 + 3 = 5\n- 5 > 3, so return 3 ✓\n\n```python\ndef buyChoco(prices, money):\n    prices.sort()\n    min_cost = prices[0] + prices[1]\n    if min_cost <= money:\n        return money - min_cost\n    else:\n        return money\n```",
