    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    The key insight is that the bitwise operations (AND and OR) preserve the \n    total number of set bits at each bit position across the entire array. \n    To maximize the sum of squares (sum_{i=1}^k x_i^2), we want to make \n    the numbers as large as possible by concentrating the set bits.\n    \n    If there are C_p numbers with the p-th bit set in the original array, \n    we can arrange the bits such that the first C_p numbers (in any order) \n    in our final array have the p-th bit set. To maximize the sum of \n    squares, we distribute these bits among the k numbers such that the \n    first number receives as many set bits as possible, the second number \n    the next most, and so on.\n    \n    Specifically, we can create k numbers where the p-th bit is set for \n    the i-th number (1 <= i <= k) if i <= C_p.\n    \"\"\"\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Count the occurrences of each bit across all numbers.\n    # Constraints: 1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9, so 30 bits are sufficient.\n    bit_counts = [0] * 31\n    for x in nums:\n        for p in range(31):\n            if (x >> p) & 1:\n                bit_counts[p] += 1\n                \n    # Create the k resulting numbers.\n    # result_nums[i] will store the i-th number formed.\n    result_nums = [0] * k\n    for p in range(31):\n        # We can set the p-th bit for up to min(bit_counts[p], k) numbers.\n        # To maximize the sum of squares, we set the p-th bit for the first \n        # min(bit_counts[p], k) numbers.\n        count = bit_counts[p]\n        for i in range(min(count, k)):\n            result_nums[i] |= (1 << p)\n            \n    # Calculate the sum of squares modulo 10^9 + 7.\n    ans = 0\n    for x in result_nums:\n        ans = (ans + x * x) % MOD\n        \n    return ans\n\n```",
