    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_271",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere are N rectangular sheets spread out on a coordinate plane.\nEach side of the rectangular region covered by each sheet is parallel to the x- or y-axis.\r\nSpecifically, the i-th sheet covers exactly the region satisfying A_i \\leq x\\leq B_i and C_i \\leq y\\leq D_i.\nLet S be the area of the region covered by one or more sheets. It can be proved that S is an integer under the constraints.\r\nPrint S as an integer.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nA_1 B_1 C_1 D_1\r\nA_2 B_2 C_2 D_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nA_N B_N C_N D_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the area S of the region covered by one or more sheets as an integer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2\\leq N\\leq 100\n- 0\\leq A_i<B_i\\leq 100\n- 0\\leq C_i<D_i\\leq 100\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\r\n0 5 1 3\r\n1 4 0 5\r\n2 5 2 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n20\r\n\nThe three sheets cover the following regions. \r\nHere, red, yellow, and blue represent the regions covered by the first, second, and third sheets, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the area of the region covered by one or more sheets is S=20.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n2\r\n0 100 0 100\r\n0 100 0 100\n\nSample Output 2\n\n10000\r\n\nNote that different sheets may cover the same region.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n3\r\n0 1 0 1\r\n0 3 0 5\r\n5 10 0 10\n\nSample Output 3\n\n65",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-20b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every sheet  \n`A_i ≤ x ≤ B_i` and `C_i ≤ y ≤ D_i`.\n\nIf we split the whole plane into unit squares\n\n```\n[0,1]×[0,1] , [0,1]×[1,2] , … , [0,1]×[99,100]\n[1,2]×[0,1] , …                             \n```\n\na sheet covers a unit square **iff** its left‑bottom corner\n`(x , y)` satisfies\n\n```\nA_i ≤ x < B_i   and   C_i ≤ y < D_i\n```\n\nBecause all coordinates are integers, the total covered area equals the\nnumber of covered unit squares.\n\nThe plane is limited by `0 ≤ x , y ≤ 100`,\nso we only need a 100×100 boolean grid.\n\n**Algorithm**\n\n```\ncreate 100×100 grid G, all False\nfor each rectangle (A,B,C,D):\n    for x from A to B-1:\n        for y from C to D-1:\n            G[x][y] = True\nanswer = number of True entries in G\nprint answer\n```\n\n`N ≤ 100`, the loops run at most `100 * 100 * 100 = 1,000,000`\noperations – easily fast enough.\n\n**Correctness Proof**\n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the area of the union of all sheets.\n\n*Lemma 1*  \nFor any unit square S with left‑bottom corner `(x,y)` the algorithm\nmarks `G[x][y]` as True **iff** S lies inside at least one sheet.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a rectangle `(A,B,C,D)` contains S, then `A ≤ x < B` and `C ≤ y < D`.\nThe algorithm iterates over exactly those `x,y` ranges and sets\n`G[x][y] = True`. Conversely, if no rectangle contains S, none of the\nloops will visit `(x,y)` and `G[x][y]` remains False. ∎\n\n*Lemma 2*  \nThe number of True entries in G equals the total area of the union of\nthe sheets.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1, a unit square is counted (True) exactly when it is\ncovered by at least one sheet. Each unit square has area 1, so the\ncount equals the union’s area. ∎\n\n*Theorem*  \nThe algorithm outputs the correct area `S`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 the number of True entries computed by the algorithm\nequals the union area. The algorithm prints this number, hence it prints\n`S`. ∎\n\n**Complexity Analysis**\n\nThe grid has 10 000 cells. For each of the at most 100 rectangles we\nvisit at most 10 000 cells.  \nTime complexity: `O(N * 100 * 100) ≤ 1 000 000`.  \nMemory usage: `O(100 * 100)` booleans ≈ 10 kB.\n\n**Reference Implementation (Python 3)**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    it = iter(data)\n    n = int(next(it))\n    # 100x100 grid, False means uncovered\n    covered = [[False] * 100 for _ in range(100)]\n    for _ in range(n):\n        A = int(next(it)); B = int(next(it))\n        C = int(next(it)); D = int(next(it))\n        # mark all unit squares whose left‑bottom corner lies inside [A,B)×[C,D)\n        for x in range(A, B):\n            row = covered[x]\n            for y in range(C, D):\n                row[y] = True\n    # count True cells\n    area = sum(sum(row) for row in covered)\n    print(area)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above.",
