      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every sheet we are given an axis‑parallel rectangle  \n\n```\nA ≤ x ≤ B ,   C ≤ y ≤ D          ( A , B , C , D are integers )\n```\n\nThe wanted value `S` is the area of the union of all rectangles.\nBecause all rectangle borders lie on integer coordinates, the whole union\nconsists of whole unit squares whose lower‑left corners have integer\ncoordinates.  \nIf a unit square with corner `(i , j)` is covered by at least one sheet,\nits contribution to the area is exactly `1`.\n\nThe coordinate limits are tiny (`0 … 100`), therefore we can enumerate\nall unit squares directly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread N\nmake a 2‑D boolean array covered[101][101] initialised to False\nfor each rectangle (A,B,C,D):\n        for x from A to B-1:\n                for y from C to D-1:\n                        covered[x][y] = True\nanswer = number of cells with value True\nprint answer\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the area `S` of the union of the given\nrectangles.\n\n*Lemma 1*  \nA unit square `[x,x+1] × [y,y+1]` (with integer `x,y`) is completely inside\na rectangle `A ≤ x' ≤ B , C ≤ y' ≤ D` **iff** `A ≤ x < B` and `C ≤ y < D`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the inequalities hold, the whole square’s `x` coordinates lie in\n`[A,B]` and its `y` coordinates lie in `[C,D]`, therefore the square is a\nsubset of the rectangle.  \nConversely, if the square is a subset of the rectangle, its left side\n`x` must satisfy `A ≤ x` and its right side `x+1` must satisfy `x+1 ≤ B`,\nwhich is equivalent to `x < B`. The same argument holds for `y`. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2*  \nDuring the processing of a rectangle the algorithm marks exactly the\nunit squares that lie inside this rectangle.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe nested loops iterate over all integer pairs `(x,y)` with\n`A ≤ x ≤ B-1` and `C ≤ y ≤ D-1`. By Lemma&nbsp;1 this set coincides with the\nunit squares that are fully contained in the rectangle, and each of them\nis set to `True`. No other squares are touched. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3*  \nAfter all rectangles have been processed, a cell `covered[x][y]` is `True`\niff the corresponding unit square belongs to the union of the rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \n*If* the square belongs to the union, it belongs to at least one rectangle.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 that rectangle’s loops set `covered[x][y]` to `True`.  \n*Only‑if* `covered[x][y]` is `True`, it was set by some rectangle’s loops,\nhence the square lies inside that rectangle and therefore inside the union. ∎\n\n\n\n*Theorem*  \nThe algorithm prints the exact area `S` of the region covered by\none or more sheets.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the set of cells counted in `answer` is exactly the set of\nunit squares that belong to the union. Each such square has area `1`,\nso the total counted number equals the geometric area `S`. The algorithm\noutputs this number, therefore it is correct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\nThe grid size is at most `100 × 100 = 10 000`.  \nFor each rectangle we may touch at most this many cells, and there are at\nmost `N = 100` rectangles.\n\n```\nTime   : O(N · 100 · 100) ≤ 1·10⁶ operations   (well below limits)\nMemory : O(100·100) booleans ≈ 10 kB\n```\n\nBoth are easily acceptable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    # grid[x][y] corresponds to unit square [x,x+1] × [y,y+1]\n    MAX = 101          # coordinates go up to 100, we need indices 0..99\n    covered = [[False] * MAX for _ in range(MAX)]\n\n    for _ in range(N):\n        A = int(next(it))\n        B = int(next(it))\n        C = int(next(it))\n        D = int(next(it))\n        for x in range(A, B):          # A ≤ x < B\n            row = covered[x]\n            for y in range(C, D):      # C ≤ y < D\n                row[y] = True\n\n    # count covered unit squares\n    ans = sum(row[y] for row in covered for y in range(MAX) if row[y])\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\nconforms to the required input‑output format.",
