      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every person we know his parent, therefore the family is a rooted tree  \n(root = person 1).  \nFor a query `(x , y)` all descendants of `x` whose distance from `x`\nis at most `y` are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Reformulation\n\n```\ndepth[v] = distance from the root (person 1)\n```\n\n`v` is covered by the query `(x , y)` **iff**\n\n```\nx is an ancestor of v          (including v itself)      and\ndepth[v] ≤ depth[x] + y\n```\n\nFor a fixed node `v` look at all queries whose starting point `x`\nlies on the path `root → v`.  \nEach such query gives a number  \n\n```\nlimit = depth[x] + y                (the deepest depth it can cover)\n```\n\n`v` is covered  **iff** the maximum of all those limits is at least\n`depth[v]`.\n\nSo during a walk from the root to a leaf we only have to know\n\n```\ncurrent_max_limit = max { depth[x] + y |  query (x , y) is on the path\n                                           from the root to the current node }\n```\n\nIf `current_max_limit ≥ depth[current]` the current person is covered.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Data structures\n\n* `children[u]` – adjacency list of the tree.\n* `depth[u]` – depth of every node (computed once with an iterative DFS).\n* `queries_at[u]` – list of limits `L = depth[u] + y` belonging to queries\n  that start at node `u`.\n* **max‑heap** `heap` (implemented with Python’s `heapq` as a *min*‑heap of\n  the negative values) containing all limits of queries that are\n  currently active, i.e. whose starting node is an ancestor of the node\n  we are visiting.\n* dictionary `cnt` – how many active copies a concrete limit has.\n  It is needed because the heap does not support deletion,\n  we delete lazily: when the top element of the heap has `cnt == 0`\n  we pop it.\n\nAll operations are `O(log (N+M))`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Algorithm\n```\n1. read the tree, build children[]                 – O(N)\n2. compute depth[] by an iterative DFS               – O(N)\n\n3. for every insurance (x , y)\n        L = depth[x] + y\n        queries_at[x].append(L)                      – O(M)\n\n4. second DFS (iterative, own stack)                – O((N+M) log)\n        stack holds pairs (node, state) :\n                state = 0 → entering the node\n                state = 1 → leaving the node\n\n        when entering node u\n                for each L in queries_at[u]\n                        push -L into heap\n                        cnt[L] += 1\n                while heap not empty and cnt[ -heap[0] ] == 0\n                        pop heap\n                max_limit = -heap[0]   (or -∞ if heap empty)\n                if max_limit ≥ depth[u]   answer += 1\n\n                push (u,1) onto stack                // will be processed later\n                push all children of u (state 0)     // any order\n\n        when leaving node u\n                for each L in queries_at[u]\n                        cnt[L] -= 1\n                // lazy deletions – the next iteration will clean the heap\n```\n\nThe answer is the number of nodes for which the condition was true.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints the number of people covered by at\nleast one insurance.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nDuring the second DFS, just before processing a node `v`\n(the moment we evaluate the condition for `v`),\nthe heap contains exactly the limits `L = depth[x] + y` of all queries\nwhose starting node `x` is an ancestor of `v` (including `v` itself).\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe DFS is a classic depth‑first traversal.\n\n*When we first enter a node `u`* we push all limits of queries that start\nat `u`. All ancestors of `u` have already been entered and their limits\nare still in the heap because we have not left those ancestors yet.\nHence after the insertion the heap contains limits of **all** queries\nstarting at ancestors of `u` (including `u`).\n\n*When we leave a node `u`* we decrement `cnt[L]` for every limit belonging\nto a query that started at `u`. The value stays in the heap, but it is\nmarked “deleted”. The cleaning step performed before the next query\nremoves it from the heap. Therefore after leaving `u` the heap no longer\ncontains limits of queries whose start node is `u` nor of any node in the\nsubtree of `u` that has already been completely processed.\n\nConsequently, right before a node `v` is processed, the heap contains\nexactly the limits of queries whose start node lies on the current\nroot‑to‑`v` path. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nLet `max_limit(v)` be the maximum value stored in the heap when node `v`\nis processed (or `-∞` if the heap is empty). Then\n\n```\nmax_limit(v) = max { depth[x] + y | (x , y) is a query and x is an ancestor of v } .\n```\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the heap contains *all* limits belonging to queries whose\nstarting node is an ancestor of `v`, and **only** those. The maximum of a\nset is independent of representation, therefore the maximum value in the\nheap equals the right‑hand side. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nNode `v` is covered by at least one insurance **iff**\n`max_limit(v) ≥ depth[v]`.\n\n**Proof.**  \n*If part* – assume `max_limit(v) ≥ depth[v]`.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 there exists an ancestor `x` of `v` and a query `(x , y)`\nwith `depth[x] + y = max_limit(v)`. The inequality gives  \n`depth[v] ≤ depth[x] + y`, i.e. the distance from `x` to `v` is at most `y`,\nhence this query covers `v`.\n\n*Only‑if part* – assume `v` is covered. Then there exists a query `(x , y)`\nwith `x` ancestor of `v` and `depth[v] ≤ depth[x] + y`.  \nThe value `depth[x] + y` appears in the heap when `v` is processed,\ntherefore `max_limit(v)` is at least this value, consequently\n`max_limit(v) ≥ depth[v]`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nThe algorithm increments the answer exactly for the nodes that are\ncovered.\n\n**Proof.**  \nWhen a node `v` is processed the algorithm computes `max_limit(v)` as the\nnegative of the heap top (or `-∞` if empty) and checks the condition\n`max_limit(v) ≥ depth[v]`. By Lemma&nbsp;3 this condition holds precisely\nfor covered nodes, therefore the answer is increased exactly for those\nnodes. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm outputs the number of people that are covered by at least\none insurance.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;4 each covered person contributes `1` to the variable\n`answer` and each uncovered person contributes `0`. After the whole DFS\nall `N` persons have been processed, thus `answer` equals the total number\nof covered persons, which is printed. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n*building the tree & depth* : `O(N)` time, `O(N)` memory  \n*storing queries*            : `O(M)` time, `O(M)` memory  \n*second DFS* :\n\n* each query limit is inserted once and later deleted once →  \n  `O((N+M) log(N+M))` heap operations,\n* each node is visited a constant number of times.\n\nTotal  \n\n```\nTime   :  O((N + M) log (N + M))      ≤  O(3·10^5 log 3·10^5)\nMemory :  O(N + M)\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = list(map(int, sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()))\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = next(it)\n    M = next(it)\n\n    # ---------- tree ----------\n    children = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    parent = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for v in range(2, N + 1):\n        p = next(it)\n        parent[v] = p\n        children[p].append(v)\n\n    # ---------- depths ----------\n    depth = [0] * (N + 1)\n    stack = [1]\n    order = []                 # for later DFS we also need preorder order\n    while stack:\n        u = stack.pop()\n        order.append(u)\n        for w in children[u]:\n            depth[w] = depth[u] + 1\n            stack.append(w)\n\n    # ---------- queries ----------\n    queries_at = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    for _ in range(M):\n        x = next(it)\n        y = next(it)\n        L = depth[x] + y\n        queries_at[x].append(L)\n\n    # ---------- second DFS with heap ----------\n    heap = []                  # store negative limits for max‑heap\n    cnt = {}                   # limit -> active count\n    ans = 0\n\n    # iterative DFS with explicit entry / exit states\n    stack = [(1, 0)]           # (node, state) 0=enter, 1=exit\n    NEG_INF = -10**18\n\n    while stack:\n        u, state = stack.pop()\n        if state == 0:                     # entering u\n            # add all queries that start at u\n            for L in queries_at[u]:\n                heapq.heappush(heap, -L)\n                cnt[L] = cnt.get(L, 0) + 1\n\n            # clean heap top from already removed elements\n            while heap and cnt.get(-heap[0], 0) == 0:\n                heapq.heappop(heap)\n\n            cur_max = -heap[0] if heap else NEG_INF\n            if cur_max >= depth[u]:\n                ans += 1\n\n            # schedule exit processing\n            stack.append((u, 1))\n            # push children (any order)\n            for v in children[u][::-1]:\n                stack.append((v, 0))\n\n        else:                              # leaving u\n            for L in queries_at[u]:\n                cnt[L] -= 1\n            # lazy removal – clean now to keep heap small\n            while heap and cnt.get(-heap[0], 0) == 0:\n                heapq.heappop(heap)\n\n    print(ans)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input‑output format.",
