      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxFrequency(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    To maximize the frequency of an element, we want to choose a target value \n    that minimizes the sum of absolute differences between that target and \n    a window of elements in the sorted array. The optimal target for a \n    window [left, right] is the median element (nums[(left + right) // 2]).\n    \n    1. Sort the array.\n    2. Use a sliding window [left, right] to consider all subarrays of length >= 1.\n    3. Calculate the cost to equalize all elements in the current window to the median.\n    4. If the cost exceeds k, shrink the window from the left.\n    \"\"\"\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Calculate prefix sums to compute range sums in O(1)\n    prefix_sum = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        prefix_sum[i + 1] = prefix_sum[i] + nums[i]\n        \n    def get_cost(left, right):\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the minimum cost to make all elements in nums[left:right+1] \n        equal to the median element.\n        \"\"\"\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        median = nums[mid]\n        \n        # Elements to the left of the median:\n        # Cost is (median * count - sum of elements)\n        left_side_cost = (median * (mid - left)) - (prefix_sum[mid] - prefix_sum[left])\n        \n        # Elements to the right of the median:\n        # Cost is (sum of elements - median * count)\n        right_side_cost = (prefix_sum[right + 1] - prefix_sum[mid + 1]) - (median * (right - mid))\n        \n        return left_side_cost + right_side_cost\n        \n    left = 0\n    max_freq = 0\n    for right in range(n):\n        # Shrink the window if the cost to equalize exceeds k\n        while get_cost(left, right) > k:\n            left += 1\n        \n        # Update the maximum frequency found so far\n        max_freq = max(max_freq, right - left + 1)\n        \n    return max_freq\n```",
