n    \n    return max_freq\n```\n\nWait, the `while True` loop may be infinite if `l > r`. But we break if `l > r`.\n\nBut better to use a `while` condition.\n\nAlternatively, we can do:\n\n```python\ndef maxFrequencyScore(nums, k):\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    prefix = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        prefix[i+1] = prefix[i] + nums[i]\n    \n    l = 0\n    max_freq = 0\n    \n    for r in range(n):\n        mid = (l + r) // 2\n        left_count = mid - l\n        left_sum = prefix[mid] - prefix[l]\n        left_cost = nums[mid] * left_count - left_sum\n        \n        right_count = r - mid\n        right_sum = prefix[r+1] - prefix[mid+1]\n        right_cost = right_sum - nums[mid] * right_count\n        \n        total_cost = left_cost + right_cost\n        \n        while total_cost > k:\n            l += 1\n            if l > r:\n                break\n            mid = (l + r) // 2\n            left_count = mid - l\n            left_sum = prefix[mid] - prefix[l]\n            left_cost = nums[mid] * left_count - left_sum\n            \n            right_count = r - mid\n            right_sum = prefix[r+1] - prefix[mid+1]\n            right_cost = right_sum - nums[mid] * right_count\n            total_cost = left_cost + right_cost\n        \n        if l <= r:\n            max_freq = max(max_freq, r - l + 1)\n    \n    return max_freq\n```\n\nThis is clearer.\n\nBut note: recalculating `mid` and cost after `l += 1` is necessary.\n\nThis should work.\n\nLet me test with previous example.\n\nBut we can optimize by putting cost computation in a function, but not needed.\n\nFinal answer:\n\nWe return the function as requested.\n\nNote: The problem says \"Return the function body without invoking it\".\n\nSo here is the final function:",
