      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nWe have a grid `H × W` (up to `10 × 10`) and at most `7` rectangular tiles.\nEach tile may be used at most once (some tiles may stay unused),\nmay be rotated by `90°` and may be flipped – i.e. the two dimensions\n`(A,B)` and `(B,A)` are allowed.\n\nWe have to decide whether the tiles can cover the whole grid exactly,\nno overlap and no empty cells.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.  Backtracking\n\nBecause `N ≤ 7` the number of different subsets of tiles is tiny\n(`2⁷ = 128`).  \nThe grid itself is small enough (`≤ 100` cells).\n\nWe perform a depth‑first search:\n\n```\nsearch():\n    find the first empty cell (row, col)\n    if none exists → all cells are filled → success\n    for every unused tile t:\n        for each orientation (w,w') in { (A,B), (B,A) }:\n            if the tile fits with its upper-left corner at (row,col):\n                place it (mark cells as filled)\n                recurse\n                if recursion succeeded → return success\n                undo placement\n    return failure\n```\n\n*Finding the first empty cell*: iterate rows then columns;  \nthe first unfilled cell is used as the anchor for the next tile.\nThis deterministic order keeps the search small.\n\n*Pruning*:  \nIf a tile does not fit on the board or would overlap already filled cells,\nthe orientation is skipped instantly.\n\nBecause a tile can be placed in at most two positions (the two orientations),\nthe worst number of recursive calls is bounded by  \n`(8)⁰ + 2·8¹ + 2²·8² + … + 2⁷·8⁷` – well below one million\nand easily fast enough.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Implementation details\n\n* **Grid representation**: a 2‑D list `grid[H][W]` of booleans.\n* **Tile usage**: a bitmask `used` of length `N`.\n* **Orientation**: simply a pair `(w,w')`.\n* **Placement / unplacement**: changing the grid entries to `True/False`.\n* The recursion stops immediately when the whole board is filled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints `\"Yes\"` iff the grid can be tiled.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nDuring the search, the set of used tiles is always a subset of the original\ntile list, each at most once.\n\n**Proof.**  \nTiles are only marked as used when they are placed, and are unmarked only\nwhen the placement is undone during backtracking.\nThus no tile is ever used twice simultaneously. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nWhenever the recursive call `search()` reaches a state where the grid has\nno empty cells, the tiles placed so far form a perfect tiling.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe invariant of the algorithm is that placed tiles never overlap and\nnever leave the board.  \nWhen no empty cell remains, every cell is covered by exactly one of those\ntiles – hence a perfect tiling. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nIf there exists a perfect tiling using any subset of the given tiles,\nthe algorithm will eventually reach a state with no empty cells.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider one fixed perfect tiling.  Scan the board in the order used by the\nsearch (first empty cell).  When the algorithm processes the first empty\ncell, the tiling contains some tile that covers it in one of its two\norientations; that option will be tried.\nBy induction, at every recursive step the algorithm can choose the tile\nexactly as in that perfect tiling, staying consistent and eventually\nreaching the fully covered state. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nIf the algorithm terminates with success (no empty cells) it outputs\n`\"Yes\"`; if it explores all possibilities and cannot fill the grid,\nit outputs `\"No\"`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nDirectly from Lemma&nbsp;2 (success state) and the exhaustive enumeration\nof all possibilities (Lemma&nbsp;3). ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm prints `\"Yes\"` iff the grid can be covered exactly by a\nsubset of the given tiles.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n*If part*: Assume the algorithm prints `\"Yes\"`.  \nThen it reached a success state, hence by Lemma&nbsp;2 the tiles placed\nform a perfect tiling – existence is proved.\n\n*Only‑if part*: Assume a perfect tiling exists.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the search will eventually reach a success state,\nso the algorithm prints `\"Yes\"`.\n\nThus the two statements are equivalent. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Complexity Analysis\n\nLet `C = H×W ≤ 100`, `N ≤ 7`.  \nThe branching factor is at most `2·(N−k)` at depth `k`.\nThe total number of recursive calls is bounded by  \n`\\sum_{k=0}^{N} 2^k·P(N,k) ≤ 2^7·7! ≈ 10⁶`.\n\nAll operations inside a call are `O(C)` in the worst case for placement\nchecks, but practically constant (`≤ 10×10`).  \n\n**Time complexity**: `O(2^N · N!)` – well below one millisecond.  \n**Memory usage**: `O(C)` for the grid + `O(N)` for the used mask.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(10000)\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().strip().split())\n    try:\n        N = int(next(it))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n    H = int(next(it))\n    W = int(next(it))\n    tiles = [(int(next(it)), int(next(it))) for _ in range(N)]\n\n    grid = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    used = 0  # bitmask of used tiles\n\n    # find first empty cell\n    def first_empty() -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n        for r in range(H):\n            for c in range(W):\n                if not grid[r][c]:\n                    return r, c\n        return None\n\n    # try to place tile t at (r,c) with orientation (w,h)\n    def can_place(tidx: int, r: int, c: int, w: int, h: int) -> bool:\n        if r + h > H or c + w > W:\n            return False\n        for dr in range(h):\n            for dc in range(w):\n                if grid[r + dr][c + dc]:\n                    return False\n        return True\n\n    def place(tidx: int, r: int, c: int, w: int, h: int) -> None:\n        for dr in range(h):\n            for dc in range(w):\n                grid[r + dr][c + dc] = True\n\n    def unplace(tidx: int, r: int, c: int, w: int, h: int) -> None:\n        for dr in range(h):\n            for dc in range(w):\n                grid[r + dr][c + dc] = False\n\n    found = False\n\n    def dfs() -> bool:\n        nonlocal found\n        pos = first_empty()\n        if pos is None:\n            return True  # all cells are filled\n        r, c = pos\n        for i in range(N):\n            if (used >> i) & 1:\n                continue\n            a, b = tiles[i]\n            for w, h in ((a, b), (b, a)):\n                if can_place(i, r, c, w, h):\n                    place(i, r, c, w, h)\n                    nonlocal_set_used = used | (1 << i)\n                    # local capture of used for recursion\n                    nonlocal used\n                    used, prev_used = nonlocal_set_used, used\n                    if dfs():\n                        return True\n                    used = prev_used\n                    unplace(i, r, c, w, h)\n        return False\n\n    if dfs():\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\nconforms to the required input–output format.",
