    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_405",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid of H rows and W columns, each cell having a side length of 1, and we have N tiles.\nThe i-th tile (1\\leq i\\leq N) is a rectangle of size A_i\\times B_i.\nDetermine whether it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- Every cell is covered by exactly one tile.\n- It is fine to have unused tiles.\n- The tiles may be rotated or flipped when placed. However, each tile must be aligned with the edges of the cells without extending outside the grid.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN H W\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\ldots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the conditions in the problem statement are satisfied, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 7\n- 1 \\leq H,W \\leq 10\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i\\leq 10\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 5 5\n1 1\n3 3\n4 4\n2 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nPlacing the 2-nd, 4-th, and 5-th tiles as shown below covers every cell of the grid by exactly one tile.\n\nHence, print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n1 1 2\n2 3\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to place the tile without letting it extend outside the grid.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n1 2 2\n1 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to cover all cells with the tile.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n5 3 3\n1 1\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo\n\nNote that each cell must be covered by exactly one tile.",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor the `H × W` board we have to choose a *subset* of the `N ( ≤ 7 )` given rectangles,\nrotate each chosen rectangle by `0°` or `90°`,\nplace it on the board (edges parallel to the board) and\ncover **every** cell exactly once.\nA rectangle can be used at most once, unused rectangles are allowed.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Observations  \n\n* `N` is tiny – at most seven – therefore a complete back‑tracking\n  over the set of rectangles is easily fast enough.\n* The board size is at most `10 × 10 = 100` cells.\n  A board configuration can be stored as a bit mask of length `H·W`\n  (Python integers have unlimited size).\n* While back‑tracking we always know **which cells are already covered**.\n  The first uncovered cell (row‑major order) is forced to be the\n  **top‑left corner** of the next rectangle:\n  all earlier cells are already occupied, otherwise the rectangle would\n  overlap them.  \n  Hence, when we try to place a rectangle we only have to test the\n  placement with its top‑left corner exactly at this first empty cell.\n* Simple pruning:\n    * the total area of the still unused rectangles must be at least the\n      number of still uncovered cells,\n    * if the board is already completely covered we are done.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   State representation  \n\n```\nmask        – H·W bit integer, bit i = 1  ⇔  cell i already covered\nusedMask    – N bit integer, bit j = 1  ⇔  rectangle j already used\n```\n\n`mask == fullMask` (`fullMask = (1 << (H·W)) - 1`)  → success.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Recursive search  \n\n```\ndfs(mask, usedMask):\n    if mask == fullMask:                     # board completely tiled\n         return True\n\n    # ------- pruning by remaining area -------\n    uncovered = totalCells - popcount(mask)\n    remainingArea = sum(area[i] for i not used yet)\n    if remainingArea < uncovered:\n         return False\n\n    # ----- first empty cell (row‑major) -----\n    inv   = (~mask) & fullMask               # bits that are still 0\n    lsb   = inv & -inv                        # least significant 1‑bit\n    idx   = lsb.bit_length() - 1              # its index 0 … totalCells‑1\n    r, c = divmod(idx, W)                     # row, column of that cell\n\n    # ----- try every still unused rectangle -----\n    for each rectangle i not used yet:\n        for (h,w) in its two orientations (h,w) and (w,h):\n            # top‑left corner must be (r,c)\n            if r+h > H or c+w > W: continue   # does not fit into board\n\n            # are all w·h cells still free ?\n            ok = True\n            newMask = mask\n            for dr = 0 … h-1:\n                base = (r+dr) * W + c\n                rowBits = ((1 << w) - 1) << base   # w consecutive bits\n                if (mask & rowBits) != 0:           # already occupied\n                     ok = False; break\n                newMask |= rowBits\n            if not ok: continue\n\n            if dfs(newMask, usedMask | (1<<i)):\n                 return True\n\n    return False\n```\n\nThe recursion depth never exceeds `N ≤ 7`.  \nThe total number of visited states is bounded by the product of\n*choices of a rectangle* (`≤ N`) and *its possible positions* (`≤ H·W`),\nso far below one million – easily fast in Python.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints `\"Yes\"` iff a feasible tiling exists.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nDuring the search, when the first uncovered cell (row‑major) is at position\n`(r,c)`, any rectangle that covers this cell in a valid tiling must have its\ntop‑left corner exactly at `(r,c)`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAll cells with index `< r·W + c` are already covered (definition of the\nfirst uncovered cell).  \nIf a rectangle covering `(r,c)` started at a row `< r` or at the same row\nwith a column `< c`, it would also cover at least one of those earlier cells,\nwhich are already occupied – contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \n`dfs(mask, usedMask)` returns `True` **iff** the board cells represented by\n`mask` can be completely tiled using a subset of the rectangles that are\n*not* marked in `usedMask`.\n\n**Proof.** By induction over the number of uncovered cells.\n\n*Base.*  \nIf `mask == fullMask` the board is already tiled, the function returns `True`.\nConversely, if the board is tiled, `mask` must equal `fullMask`, therefore the\nfunction returns `True`.\n\n*Induction step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for every state with fewer uncovered cells than\n`mask`.  \nThe algorithm chooses the first empty cell `(r,c)`.  \n*If the board can be tiled* from this state, by Lemma&nbsp;1 some unused\nrectangle `i` (with some orientation) must be placed with its top‑left corner\nat `(r,c)` and must fit into free cells.\nThe algorithm tries **all** such rectangles and orientations,\ndetects that the placement fits, and calls `dfs` recursively with the\nnew mask and with rectangle `i` marked as used.\nBy the induction hypothesis the recursive call returns `True`,\ntherefore the current call returns `True`.\n\n*Conversely*, if the algorithm returns `True`, it has found a rectangle `i`\nand an orientation that fits and for which the recursive call returned `True`.\nBy the induction hypothesis the recursive call corresponds to a valid\ntiling of the remaining free cells with the still unused rectangles.\nAdding rectangle `i` back gives a tiling of the current state. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nThe area pruning (`remainingArea < uncovered`) never discards a state that\nbelongs to a feasible tiling.\n\n**Proof.**  \nIn any feasible continuation the uncovered cells must be covered by the still\nunused rectangles, therefore their total area is at least the number of\nuncovered cells. If the algorithm detects a smaller remaining area, no\ncontinuation can possibly cover all cells. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe program prints `\"Yes\"` **iff** the given set of rectangles can tile the\n`H × W` board exactly (using each rectangle at most once).\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*If* the program prints `\"Yes\"`, the top‑level call `dfs(0,0)` returned `True`.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 this is equivalent to the existence of a tiling of the whole\nboard using a subset of the rectangles – exactly the required condition.\n\n*Only if* a tiling exists, consider the execution of `dfs(0,0)`.\nAt each recursion level the algorithm examines the rectangle that the real\ntiling uses to cover the current first empty cell (Lemma&nbsp;1).  \nThat rectangle passes the fitting test, the recursive state coincides with\nthe state after placing it in the real tiling, and the area pruning never\nremoves this state (Lemma&nbsp;3).  \nThus the recursion can follow the real tiling step by step and finally reaches\nthe state `mask == fullMask`, which returns `True`. Consequently the top call\nreturns `True` and the program prints `\"Yes\"`. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n*`C = H·W ≤ 100`* – number of cells.  \n\n*State size* – two integers, negligible.  \n\n*Number of recursive calls* – at most  \n\n```\nΣ_{k=0}^{N}  (C choose k) * k!   ≤  (C+N)! / C!   ≤  (100+7)! / 100!  < 10^7\n```\n\nIn practice far less because of the strong pruning.\nEach call tests at most `N` rectangles, each test touches at most `C` bits,\nso the total running time is well below one second.\n\nMemory consumption: memoisation stores at most the number of visited states,\neach a pair of Python integers ⇒ < a few megabytes.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(10000)\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    H = int(next(it))\n    W = int(next(it))\n\n    tiles = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        a = int(next(it))\n        b = int(next(it))\n        tiles.append((a, b))\n\n    total_cells = H * W\n    full_mask = (1 << total_cells) - 1\n    areas = [a * b for a, b in tiles]\n\n    from functools import lru_cache\n\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def dfs(mask: int, used_mask: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"return True iff the uncovered cells can be tiled\"\"\"\n        if mask == full_mask:\n            return True\n\n        # --------- area pruning ----------\n        covered = mask.bit_count()\n        need = total_cells - covered\n        remaining_area = 0\n        for i in range(N):\n            if not (used_mask >> i) & 1:\n                remaining_area += areas[i]\n        if remaining_area < need:\n            return False\n\n        # ----- first empty cell (row‑major) -----\n        inv = (~mask) & full_mask          # bits that are still 0\n        lsb = inv & -inv                   # least significant 1‑bit\n        idx = lsb.bit_length() - 1\n        r, c = divmod(idx, W)\n\n        # ----- try every unused rectangle -----\n        for i in range(N):\n            if (used_mask >> i) & 1:\n                continue\n            a, b = tiles[i]\n            orientations = [(a, b)] if a == b else [(a, b), (b, a)]\n            for h, w in orientations:\n                # top‑left corner forced to (r,c)\n                if r + h > H or c + w > W:\n                    continue\n                ok = True\n                new_mask = mask\n                for dr in range(h):\n                    base = (r + dr) * W + c\n                    row_bits = ((1 << w) - 1) << base\n                    if (mask & row_bits) != 0:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                    new_mask |= row_bits\n                if not ok:\n                    continue\n                if dfs(new_mask, used_mask | (1 << i)):\n                    return True\n        return False\n\n    answer = dfs(0, 0)\n    print(\"Yes\" if answer else \"No\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input / output format.",
