      "generated_answer": "We are given an array `nums`, and we can traverse from index `i` to index `j` (i ≠ j) **if and only if** `gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1`.\n\nOur goal is to determine whether **all pairs of indices** `(i, j)` with `i < j` are **connected via some sequence** of such traversals.\n\nThat is, we want to know if the entire graph formed by connecting indices whose values have a GCD > 1 is **connected**.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-step Analysis:\n\n#### 1. **Graph Representation**\nWe can model this as a graph:\n- Each index is a node.\n- An edge exists between `i` and `j` if `gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1`.\n\nWe want to check if the graph is **connected**.\n\nHowever, the array can have up to `10^5` elements. Checking all pairs (`O(n^2)`) is too slow.\n\nWe need a **more efficient** way.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Key Insight: Use Prime Factors**\n\nInstead of connecting indices directly, we can use the idea of **prime factorization**.\n\nIdea:\n- Two numbers have `gcd > 1` if they share **at least one prime factor**.\n- So, indices `i` and `j` are connected (directly or indirectly) if their values share a common prime factor, or are connected via other numbers sharing primes.\n\nSo, we can:\n- For each number in `nums`, factorize it into its prime factors.\n- Group indices by the prime factors of their values.\n- All indices that share a common prime factor can be connected (they belong to the same component via that prime).\n\nThis suggests using **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union, DSU)**:\n- Union all indices that share the same prime factor.\n- At the end, check if all indices belong to the same connected component.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Algorithm Plan**\n\n1. **Edge case**: If there is only one element, return `True`.\n\n2. Use **Union-Find** structure to manage connected components of indices.\n\n3. Create a dictionary: `prime_to_index` — maps a prime factor to the first index that had it (so we can union all indices having that prime).\n\n4. For each index `i`, factorize `nums[i]` into its **unique prime factors**.\n\n5. For each prime factor:\n   - If we’ve seen this prime before (in another index), union the current index with the first index that had it.\n   - Otherwise, record this index as the first one with this prime.\n\n6. After processing all numbers, check if **all indices are in the same component**.\n\n7. But wait — what if a number is `1`?\n   - `gcd(1, x) = 1` for any `x`, so any index with `1` **cannot connect to any other index**.\n   - So if there is more than one element and any element is `1`, then:\n     - If **all** elements are `1`, then we have multiple `1`s — but `gcd(1,1) = 1`, so no traversal allowed → unless only one element, it's disconnected.\n     - So: if there is a `1` and the array has more than one element, then any `1` is isolated.\n\n   Therefore:\n   - If there is **any** `1` and `len(nums) > 1`, then unless **all** are `1`, it's impossible to connect.\n   - But even if all are `1`, since `gcd(1,1) = 1`, no edges exist → so graph is disconnected (unless only one node).\n\n   So: if any number is `1`, and `n > 1`, then return `False`.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Optimization: Efficient Prime Factorization**\n\nWe can precompute the smallest prime factor (spf) for numbers up to `10^5` to factorize quickly.\n\nBut since `nums[i] <= 10^5` and `n <= 10^5`, we can either:\n- Precompute smallest prime factors for all numbers up to `100000`, or\n- Factorize on the fly using trial division (acceptable for `10^5` numbers if optimized).\n\nBut worst-case `10^5` numbers, each up to `10^5`, so we want fast factorization.\n\nWe'll precompute the smallest prime factor (spf) array up to `max_num`.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Final Steps**\n\n- Handle `1` early.\n- Precompute `spf` up to `max(nums)`.\n- Use DSU to union indices that share prime factors.\n- At the end, check if all indices are in the same component.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Plan\n\nWe'll write:\n- A function to precompute `spf` (smallest prime factor) up to `N`.\n- A function to get prime factors of a number using `spf`.\n- A DSU (Union-Find) class or just arrays.\n\nBut since we just need union and find, we can implement simple DSU.\n\n---\n\n### Let's Code\n\nNote: We must return **only the function body**, not call it.\n\nWe'll write a function:\n\n```python\ndef canTraverseAllPairs(nums):\n    ...\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-step Implementation:\n\n1. Handle `n == 1`: return `True`.\n2. If any element is `1`: return `False` (since n > 1, and 1 can't connect to anything).\n3. Precompute `spf` up to `max(nums)`.\n4. Initialize DSU for `n` indices.\n5. Map: `prime_to_index` — first index where prime was seen.\n6. For each index, factorize `nums[i]`, get unique primes.\n7. For each prime:\n   - If seen, union current index with the first index having this prime.\n   - Else, record current index as first.\n8. Finally, check if all indices have the same root.\n\nLet’s implement accordingly.\n\nNote: We only need to go up to `max_val = max(nums)` for spf.\n\nBut if `max_val < 2`, handle separately — but nums[i] >= 1, and we already handled 1s.\n\n---\n\nNow, write the function.\n\nWe'll precompute `spf` for `max_num = max(nums)`.\n\nIf `max_num == 1`, then all are 1 → already handled (n > 1 → return False).\n\nSo proceed.\n\n```python\ndef canTraverseAllPairs(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return True\n    \n    # If any element is 1, since n > 1, we cannot traverse\n    if any(x == 1 for x in nums):\n        return False\n\n    max_num = max(nums)\n    \n    # Precompute smallest prime factor (spf) up to max_num\n    spf = list(range(max_num + 1))  # spf[x] = smallest prime factor of x\n    for i in range(2, int(max_num**0.5) + 1):\n        if spf[i] == i:  # i is prime\n            for j in range(i*i, max_num + 1, i):\n                if spf[j] == j:\n                    spf[j] = i\n\n    # DSU (Union-Find) setup\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    rank = [0] * n\n\n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return\n        # Union by rank\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n\n    # Map: prime -> first index that had this prime\n    prime_to_index = {}\n\n    # Process each number\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        # Get unique prime factors of num using spf\n        factors = set()\n        x = num\n        while x > 1:\n            p = spf[x]\n            factors.add(p)\n            while x % p == 0:\n                x //= p\n        \n        # For each prime factor, union index i with previous index having this prime\n        for p in factors:\n            if p in prime_to_index:\n                union(i, prime_to_index[p])\n            else:\n                prime_to_index[p] = i\n\n    # Check if all indices are in the same component\n    root = find(0)\n    return all(find(i) == root for i in range(n))\n```\n\n---\n\n### Final Notes\n\n- We precomputed `spf` for efficient factorization.\n- We used union-find to group indices sharing prime factors.\n- We early-return `False` if any element is `1` (since `n > 1`).\n- We return `True` only if all indices are in the same connected component.\n\nThis should handle all cases efficiently.\n\nTime: O(n log max + max log log max) approximately.\n\nNow, return only the function body as requested.",
