    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every value `v` in `nums` let `cnt[v]` be its frequency.\nAll indices that contain the same value have to stay together in a group,\nbut a value may be split into several groups.\nIf we use `k` groups, each group size can only be  \n\n* `a = n // k`   (the smaller size) or  \n* `a+1`          (the larger size)\n\nbecause the difference between any two groups must be at most `1`.\nExactly `r = n % k` groups have size `a+1`, the other `k‑r` groups have size `a`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.   When is a fixed `k` feasible ?\n\nFor a concrete value with frequency `f`\n\n```\nf = a * t + y                (0 ≤ y ≤ t)\n```\n\nwhere  \n\n* `t` – number of groups the value is split into  \n* `y` – how many of those groups have size `a+1`\n\nRe‑arranging gives  \n\n```\na·t ≤ f ≤ (a+1)·t\n```\n\nHence the possible numbers of groups for this value are\n\n```\nceil( f / (a+1) ) ≤ t ≤ floor( f / a )\n```\n\n(when `a = 0` we never query such a `k`, because `k ≤ n`.)\n\nFor the whole array we have to choose a `t_i` for every distinct value\n(`i` indexes the values) such that  \n\n```\n Σ t_i = k                          (all groups are used)\n```\n\nIf we denote  \n\n```\nlow_i  = ceil( cnt_i / (a+1) )\nhigh_i = floor( cnt_i / a )\n```\n\nthe condition *“there exists a feasible assignment”* is exactly\n\n```\n Σ low_i  ≤  k  ≤  Σ high_i\n```\n\nIf the inequality holds, the required numbers `y_i = cnt_i – a·t_i`\nautomatically sum to `r = n – a·k`, so the size‑balance condition is already satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.   Searching the minimum `k`\n\n`k` is monotone:\n*if `k` works, any larger `k` also works* – because making groups smaller never\nbreaks the ability to split each frequency (the intervals `[low_i, high_i]`\nonly become larger).\n\nTherefore we binary‑search the smallest feasible `k` in the range `1 … n`.\n\nThe test for a given `k` is `O(number of distinct values)`,\noverall complexity `O( n log n )` (the distinct‑value count ≤ `n`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns the minimal possible number of groups.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor a fixed `k` ( `1 ≤ k ≤ n` ) let `a = n // k`.  \nA group assignment using exactly `k` groups exists **iff**\n` Σ low_i ≤ k ≤ Σ high_i`.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n*If part* (`⇒`): any feasible assignment gives for each value `i`\na concrete number `t_i` of groups.\nBecause each group size is `a` or `a+1`,\n`a·t_i ≤ cnt_i ≤ (a+1)·t_i`,\nhence `low_i ≤ t_i ≤ high_i`. Summation yields the inequality.\n\n*Only‑if part* (`⇐`): assume the inequality holds.\nChoose any integers `t_i` with `low_i ≤ t_i ≤ high_i` and\n`Σ t_i = k` – possible because the total lower bound does not exceed `k`\nand the total upper bound is not smaller than `k`.\nDefine `y_i = cnt_i – a·t_i` (non‑negative and ≤ `t_i` by the bounds).\nMake `y_i` groups of size `a+1` and `t_i‑y_i` groups of size `a` for value `i`.\nAll groups together are `k`, and their sizes differ by at most one.\n∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a certain `k` is feasible, then every `k' > k` is also feasible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIncreasing the number of groups reduces `a = n // k'` (or keeps it the same)\nand therefore enlarges each interval `[low_i, high_i]`.\nConsequently a solution for `k` can be transformed into a solution for `k'`\nby possibly splitting some groups further, which never violates the\nhomogeneity or the size‑balance condition. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe binary search returns the smallest `k` that satisfies\n` Σ low_i ≤ k ≤ Σ high_i`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBinary search on a monotone predicate (“`k` is feasible”) always yields the\nminimum index where the predicate becomes true.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 the predicate is monotone, and Lemma&nbsp;1 shows that the\npredicate is exactly the inequality test used in the algorithm. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Theorem  \nThe algorithm outputs the minimum possible number of groups for the given\narray `nums`.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Existence*: By Lemma&nbsp;1 the algorithm’s feasibility test is correct,\nso whenever it declares a `k` feasible, a valid grouping exists.\n\n*Minimality*: Let `k*` be the value returned by the binary search.\nAll `k < k*` are declared infeasible, therefore by Lemma&nbsp;1 no grouping\nwith fewer than `k*` groups exists.\n`k*` itself is feasible, thus a grouping with `k*` groups exists.\nHence `k*` is the minimum achievable number. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n* `m` – number of distinct values (`m ≤ n`)\n\n*Counting frequencies*: `O(n)` time, `O(m)` extra space.  \n\n*Binary search*: at most `⌈log₂ n⌉` iterations.\n\n*Each feasibility test*: scans all `m` frequencies, `O(m)` time,\n`O(1)` extra space.\n\nOverall  \n\n```\nTime   : O( n + m·log n )   = O( n log n )\nMemory : O( m )             = O( n )\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits (`n ≤ 10⁵`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.   Reference Implementation  (function body only)\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom math import ceil\n\ndef min_groups(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    freq = list(Counter(nums).values())          # list of frequencies\n    # binary search on k ∈ [1, n]\n    lo, hi = 1, n\n    answer = n\n\n    while lo <= hi:\n        k = (lo + hi) // 2\n        a = n // k                                 # smaller group size (≥1)\n\n        low_sum = 0\n        high_sum = 0\n        feasible = True\n\n        # compute Σ low_i and Σ high_i\n        for f in freq:\n            low = (f + (a + 1) - 1) // (a + 1)      # ceil(f / (a+1))\n            high = f // a                          # floor(f / a)\n            low_sum += low\n            high_sum += high\n            # early exit if already impossible\n            if low_sum > k:\n                feasible = False\n                break\n\n        if feasible and low_sum <= k <= high_sum:\n            answer = k\n            hi = k - 1\n        else:\n            lo = k + 1\n\n    return answer\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\nconforms to the required input‑output format.",
