    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_390",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given a string S consisting of lowercase English letters. The length of S is between 3 and 100, inclusive.\nAll characters but one of S are the same.\nFind x such that the x-th character of S differs from all other characters.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nS\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- S is a string of length between 3 and 100, inclusive, consisting of two different lowercase English letters.\n- All characters but one of S are the same.\n\nSample Input 1\n\nyay\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\r\n\nThe second character of yay differs from the first and third characters.\n\nSample Input 2\n\negg\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1\n\nSample Input 3\n\nzzzzzwz\n\nSample Output 3\n\n6",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor the given string `S`\n\n* its length is `3 … 100`\n* it contains exactly **two** different letters\n* one of the letters appears **once**, all others are the same  \n\nWe have to output the (1‑based) position of the single different character.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Observation  \n\nIf we count how many times each character occurs, the character whose count is `1` is the one we are looking for. Its index in the string (plus one) is the answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread S\ncnt = frequency map of characters in S\nunique_char = the character with cnt[char] == 1\nposition = index of unique_char in S (0‑based) + 1\nprint position\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm always prints the required position.\n\n*Lemma 1*  \n`unique_char` returned by the algorithm is exactly the character that differs from all others.\n\n*Proof.*  \n`S` contains only two distinct letters, say `a` and `b`.  \nAll characters but one are equal, therefore one of the letters occurs `|S|-1` times and the other occurs `1` time.  \nThe frequency map `cnt` records these two counts. The algorithm selects the character with count `1`. Hence it selects the letter that appears only once, i.e. the differing character. ∎\n\n\n*Lemma 2*  \n`position = S.index(unique_char) + 1` equals the 1‑based index of the differing character in `S`.\n\n*Proof.*  \n`str.index(c)` returns the smallest index `i` (0‑based) such that `S[i] = c`.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 `unique_char` appears **exactly once**, therefore that smallest index is the **only** index where it occurs. Adding `1` converts it to 1‑based numbering, which is exactly what the problem asks for. ∎\n\n\n*Theorem*  \nThe algorithm outputs the correct answer for every allowed input string `S`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the algorithm identifies the unique differing character.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 it computes its 1‑based position.  \nThe algorithm prints this position, which matches the definition of `x` in the problem statement. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Counting frequencies* scans the string once: `O(|S|)` time.  \n*Finding the index* scans at most once more: `O(|S|)` time.  \nOverall time complexity: `O(|S|) ≤ O(100)`.  \n\nThe frequency map stores at most two entries: `O(1)` extra space.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    S = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    # frequency of each character\n    freq = Counter(S)\n\n    # the character that appears only once\n    unique_char = None\n    for ch, cnt in freq.items():\n        if cnt == 1:\n            unique_char = ch\n            break\n\n    # its (1‑based) position\n    pos = S.index(unique_char) + 1\n    print(pos)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms to the required input‑output format.",
