    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_386",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid with H rows and W columns.\nEach cell of the grid is land or sea, which is represented by H strings S_1, S_2, \\ldots, S_H of length W. Let (i, j) denote the cell at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left, and (i, j) is land if the j-th character of S_i is ., and (i, j) is sea if the character is #.\nThe constraints guarantee that all cells on the perimeter of the grid (that is, the cells (i, j) that satisfy at least one of i = 1, i = H, j = 1, j = W) are sea.\nTakahashi's spaceship has crash-landed on a cell in the grid. Afterward, he moved N times on the grid following the instructions represented by a string T of length N consisting of L, R, U, and D. For i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, the i-th character of T describes the i-th move as follows:\n\n- L indicates a move of one cell to the left. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i, j-1) after the move.\n- R indicates a move of one cell to the right. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i, j+1) after the move.\n- U indicates a move of one cell up. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i-1, j) after the move.\n- D indicates a move of one cell down. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i+1, j) after the move.\n\nIt is known that all cells along his path (including the cell where he crash-landed and the cell he is currently on) are not sea. Print the number of cells that could be his current position.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W N\nT\nS_1\nS_2\n\\vdots\nS_H\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- H, W, and N are integers.\n- 3 \\leq H, W \\leq 500\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 500\n- T is a string of length N consisting of L, R, U, and D.\n- S_i is a string of length W consisting of . and #.\n- There is at least one cell that could be Takahashi's current position.\n- All cells on the perimeter of the grid are sea.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 7 5\nLULDR\n#######\n#...#.#\n##...##\n#.#...#\n#...#.#\n#######\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n\nThe following two cases are possible, so there are two cells that could be Takahashi's current position: (3, 4) and (4, 5).\n\n- He crash-landed on cell (3, 5) and moved (3, 5) \\rightarrow (3, 4) \\rightarrow (2, 4) \\rightarrow (2, 3) \\rightarrow (3, 3) \\rightarrow (3, 4).\n- He crash-landed on cell (4, 6) and moved (4, 6) \\rightarrow (4, 5) \\rightarrow (3, 5) \\rightarrow (3, 4) \\rightarrow (4, 4) \\rightarrow (4, 5).\n\nSample Input 2\n\n13 16 9\nULURDLURD\n################\n##..##.#..####.#\n###.#..#.....#.#\n#..##..#####.###\n#...#..#......##\n###.##.#..#....#\n##.#####....##.#\n###.###.#.#.#..#\n######.....##..#\n#...#.#.######.#\n##..###..#..#.##\n#...#.#.#...#..#\n################\n\nSample Output 2\n\n6",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-20b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every cell we keep a flag that says whether it *could be* the current\nposition after some prefix of the moves.\nInitially every land cell is possible, because the crash–landing cell is\nunknown.\n\nFor a move\n\n* `L` – we moved one cell left.  \n  To be able to be at `(i , j)` after the move, we must have been at\n  `(i , j+1)` before the move.\n* `R` – similar, we must have been at `(i , j-1)`.\n* `U` – must have been at `(i+1 , j)`.\n* `D` – must have been at `(i-1 , j)`.\n\nSo we update the set of possible cells by looking at the neighbour that\nwould have been visited **before** the current move.\nThe neighbour must be\n\n1. inside the grid,\n2. a land cell,\n3. marked as possible for the previous prefix of moves.\n\nWe repeat this for all `N` moves.\nAfter the last move the number of `True` marks is the number of cells\nthat could be the final position.\n\nThe grid size is at most 500 × 500 and `N ≤ 500`,\nhence a simple `O(H·W·N)` simulation easily fits in time.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread H, W, N\nread T\nread grid S[0..H-1][0..W-1]           // '.' = land, '#' = sea\n\n# current set: possible cells for the processed prefix\ncur = bool[H][W]\nfor all i,j:\n    cur[i][j] = (S[i][j] == '.')\n\nfor each character c in T:\n    nxt = bool[H][W] all False\n    for every cell (i,j) that is land:\n        # find neighbour that could have been before this move\n        if c == 'L'  : ni = i ; nj = j+1\n        if c == 'R'  : ni = i ; nj = j-1\n        if c == 'U'  : ni = i+1; nj = j\n        if c == 'D'  : ni = i-1; nj = j\n        if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and cur[ni][nj]:\n            nxt[i][j] = True\n    cur = nxt\n\nanswer = count of True in cur\nprint answer\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the number of cells that could be\nTakahashi’s final position.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1**  \nAfter processing the first `k (0 ≤ k ≤ N)` moves, `cur[i][j]` is `True`\niff there exists a starting land cell such that following the first `k`\nmoves keeps the astronaut on land and ends at cell `(i , j)`.\n\n*Proof by induction over `k`.*\n\n*Base `k = 0`*  \nBefore any move, the set is all land cells – exactly the cells that the\nastronaut can start at, which also satisfy the property.\n\n*Induction step*  \nAssume the lemma holds after `k-1` moves.\nDuring the `k`-th move the algorithm sets `nxt[i][j]` to `True`\niff `(i , j)` is land **and** the neighbour `prev` that would have been\nvisited before the move is marked `True` in `cur`.\nBy induction hypothesis `prev` is reachable after `k-1` moves.\nThus, performing the current move from `prev` to `(i , j)` keeps the\nastronaut on land and ends at `(i , j)`.  \nConversely, if a reachable path of length `k` ends at `(i , j)`,\nthe cell before the last move must satisfy the same condition,\nhence `nxt[i][j]` becomes `True`.  \nTherefore the lemma holds for `k`. ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2**  \nAfter all `N` moves, the number of cells with `True` in `cur` equals the\nnumber of cells that can be the final position.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 with `k = N`, a cell is marked `True` exactly when there\nexists a valid path of all `N` moves ending at that cell.\nCounting them gives the desired number. ∎\n\n\n\n**Theorem**  \nThe algorithm outputs the correct answer.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 the algorithm’s output equals the number of cells that\ncould be the astronaut’s current position, which is exactly the problem\nstatement’s requirement. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\nLet `H × W` be the grid size and `N` the number of moves.\n\nFor each of the `N` moves we scan all `H·W` cells once, performing\n`O(1)` work per cell.\n\n```\nTime   :  O(H · W · N)   ≤ 500 · 500 · 500 = 125·10⁶  operations\nMemory :  O(H · W)       two boolean grids of size ≤ 500 · 500\n```\n\nBoth are easily within limits.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().strip().splitlines())\n    H, W, N = map(int, next(it).split())\n    T = next(it).strip()\n    grid = [list(next(it).strip()) for _ in range(H)]\n\n    cur = [[grid[i][j] == '.' for j in range(W)] for i in range(H)]\n\n    for c in T:\n        nxt = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n        for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(W):\n                if grid[i][j] != '.':\n                    continue\n                if c == 'L':\n                    ni, nj = i, j + 1\n                elif c == 'R':\n                    ni, nj = i, j - 1\n                elif c == 'U':\n                    ni, nj = i + 1, j\n                else:  # 'D'\n                    ni, nj = i - 1, j\n                if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and cur[ni][nj]:\n                    nxt[i][j] = True\n        cur = nxt\n\n    ans = sum(cur[i][j] for i in range(H) for j in range(W))\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThis program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above.",
