    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid with H rows and W columns.\nEach cell of the grid is land or sea, which is represented by H strings S_1, S_2, \\ldots, S_H of length W. Let (i, j) denote the cell at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left, and (i, j) is land if the j-th character of S_i is ., and (i, j) is sea if the character is #.\nThe constraints guarantee that all cells on the perimeter of the grid (that is, the cells (i, j) that satisfy at least one of i = 1, i = H, j = 1, j = W) are sea.\nTakahashi's spaceship has crash-landed on a cell in the grid. Afterward, he moved N times on the grid following the instructions represented by a string T of length N consisting of L, R, U, and D. For i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, the i-th character of T describes the i-th move as follows:\n\n- L indicates a move of one cell to the left. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i, j-1) after the move.\n- R indicates a move of one cell to the right. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i, j+1) after the move.\n- U indicates a move of one cell up. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i-1, j) after the move.\n- D indicates a move of one cell down. That is, if he is at (i, j) before the move, he will be at (i+1, j) after the move.\n\nIt is known that all cells along his path (including the cell where he crash-landed and the cell he is currently on) are not sea. Print the number of cells that could be his current position.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W N\nT\nS_1\nS_2\n\\vdots\nS_H\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- H, W, and N are integers.\n- 3 \\leq H, W \\leq 500\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 500\n- T is a string of length N consisting of L, R, U, and D.\n- S_i is a string of length W consisting of . and #.\n- There is at least one cell that could be Takahashi's current position.\n- All cells on the perimeter of the grid are sea.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 7 5\nLULDR\n#######\n#...#.#\n##...##\n#.#...#\n#...#.#\n#######\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n\nThe following two cases are possible, so there are two cells that could be Takahashi's current position: (3, 4) and (4, 5).\n\n- He crash-landed on cell (3, 5) and moved (3, 5) \\rightarrow (3, 4) \\rightarrow (2, 4) \\rightarrow (2, 3) \\rightarrow (3, 3) \\rightarrow (3, 4).\n- He crash-landed on cell (4, 6) and moved (4, 6) \\rightarrow (4, 5) \\rightarrow (3, 5) \\rightarrow (3, 4) \\rightarrow (4, 4) \\rightarrow (4, 5).\n\nSample Input 2\n\n13 16 9\nULURDLURD\n################\n##..##.#..####.#\n###.#..#.....#.#\n#..##..#####.###\n#...#..#......##\n###.##.#..#....#\n##.#####....##.#\n###.###.#.#.#..#\n######.....##..#\n#...#.#.######.#\n##..###..#..#.##\n#...#.#.#...#..#\n################\n\nSample Output 2\n\n6",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every land cell we assume that Takahashi crashed there.\nFrom this cell we follow the given sequence of moves `T`.\nIf we ever step on a sea cell (`#`) the assumption is impossible.\nOtherwise the last cell we reach is a *possible current position*.\nThe answer is the number of different cells that can be reached in this way.\n\nThe grid size is at most `500 × 500 = 250 000` and the number of moves\n`N ≤ 500`.  \nChecking one start cell needs `O(N)` time, therefore the whole\nenumeration needs at most  \n\n```\n250 000 · 500 = 1.25·10⁸   elementary operations\n```\n\nwhich easily fits into the time limit in Python when the code is kept\nsimple.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Algorithm\n```\nread H, W, N\nread string T\nread the H strings of the map\nmake a 2‑dimensional boolean array land[i][j]  (True for '.')\n\nconvert T to a list of moves  (dx, dy)\n\nanswer_set = empty set of positions\n\nfor every cell (i, j) with land[i][j] == True:\n        r, c = i, j                # current position\n        ok = True\n        for (dr, dc) in moves:\n                r += dr; c += dc\n                if not land[r][c]:        # sea -> impossible\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n        if ok:\n                answer_set.add( (r, c) )   # final cell of this start\n\nprint size of answer_set\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs exactly the number of cells that can\nbe Takahashi’s current position.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor a fixed start cell `s` the algorithm declares `s` *valid* (i.e. does\nnot break early) **iff** every cell on the path starting from `s` and\nfollowing `T` is land.\n\n**Proof.**  \nDuring the simulation the algorithm steps through the cells in the\nexact order described by `T`.  \n*If* a sea cell is encountered, `land[r][c]` is `False` and the\nalgorithm sets `ok = False`. Hence the algorithm declares `s` invalid.\n*If* all visited cells are land, the condition `not land[r][c]` never\nholds, the loop finishes and `ok` stays `True`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nIf a start cell `s` is declared valid, the algorithm adds to\n`answer_set` exactly the cell that Takahashi would occupy after\nexecuting all moves from `s`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nWhen the loop over the moves ends (without breaking) the variables\n`r,c` contain the coordinates after the last move, i.e. the final\nposition of the walk that started at `s`.  \nThe algorithm inserts this pair `(r,c)` into the set. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nFor every cell `p` that can be Takahashi’s current position, `p` belongs\nto `answer_set`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume `p` is reachable. Then there exists a start cell `s` such that\nthe whole walk is on land and ends in `p`. By Lemma&nbsp;1 the algorithm\nwill mark `s` as valid, and by Lemma&nbsp;2 will insert the final\nposition `p` into `answer_set`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nEvery cell stored in `answer_set` can be Takahashi’s current position.\n\n**Proof.**  \nTake a cell `q` stored in the set. It was inserted after a start cell\n`s` was processed and declared valid. Lemma&nbsp;1 guarantees that the\nwhole walk from `s` stays on land, and Lemma&nbsp;2 tells us that `q`\nis the final cell of that walk. Therefore `q` is attainable. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe algorithm outputs the exact number of cells that could be Takahashi’s\ncurrent position.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 every attainable cell appears in `answer_set`.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;4 nothing else appears there.  \nThus `answer_set` is precisely the set of attainable cells, and its\nsize, which the algorithm prints, is the required answer. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\n* `H·W` examinations of start cells → `O(H·W)`\n* for each start cell at most `N` moves → `O(N·H·W)`\n* all operations are `O(1)` (array look‑ups, integer additions).\n\nMemory consumption: the grid (`H·W` booleans) and the set of answer\npositions (`≤ H·W` pairs) → `O(H·W)`.\n\nWith the maximal constraints this is  \n`O(250 000·500) = 1.25·10⁸` elementary steps and well below the limit,\nand memory stays well under a few megabytes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    it = iter(sys.stdin.read().splitlines())\n    H, W, N = map(int, next(it).split())\n    T = next(it).strip()\n    grid = [list(next(it).strip()) for _ in range(H)]\n\n    # boolean table: True = land ('.')\n    land = [[ch == '.' for ch in row] for row in grid]\n\n    # turn moves into (dr, dc)\n    move_map = {'L': (0, -1), 'R': (0, 1), 'U': (-1, 0), 'D': (1, 0)}\n    moves = [move_map[ch] for ch in T]\n\n    possible = set()\n\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if not land[i][j]:\n                continue          # cannot start on sea\n            r, c = i, j\n            ok = True\n            for dr, dc in moves:\n                r += dr\n                c += dc\n                if not land[r][c]:\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n            if ok:\n                possible.add((r, c))\n\n    print(len(possible))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\nconforms to the required input‑output format.",
