      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n### Problem Understanding:\nWe are given a positive integer array `nums`. We need to **partition** it into two **non-empty** arrays `nums1` and `nums2` such that:\n- Every element is in either `nums1` or `nums2`.\n- The **value of the partition**, defined as `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)|`, is **minimized**.\n\nWe are to return the **minimum possible value** of this expression.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations:\n\nLet’s denote:\n- `M1 = max(nums1)`\n- `m2 = min(nums2)`\n\nWe want to minimize `|M1 - m2|`.\n\nNote:\n- All elements are used.\n- Both partitions are non-empty.\n\nLet’s suppose we sort the array. Why? Because the value depends on **max of one group** and **min of the other**, so ordering might help us explore adjacent values.\n\nLet’s sort `nums` → let’s say `sorted_nums`.\n\nNow, think about how we can assign elements to `nums1` and `nums2`.\n\nBut note: the two groups can be arbitrary subsets — not necessarily contiguous in the original array. But since only **max of one** and **min of the other** matter, and we want their difference to be small, perhaps the optimal split occurs when the two groups are separated around some **adjacent pair** in the sorted array.\n\nLet’s suppose after sorting, we split the array at some index `i` such that:\n- `nums1` gets the first `i+1` elements (smallest `i+1` values)\n- `nums2` gets the remaining (larger values)\n\nThen:\n- `max(nums1) = sorted_nums[i]`\n- `min(nums2) = sorted_nums[i+1]`\n- So, `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)| = sorted_nums[i+1] - sorted_nums[i]`\n\nThis is **non-negative**, so absolute value is just the difference.\n\nBut wait — is this the only kind of partition we need to consider?\n\nWhat if we don't assign contiguous segments?\n\nLet’s suppose we try to make `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)|` very small. That means we want `max(nums1)` to be close to `min(nums2)`.\n\nBut note:\n- Every element must go to one of the two groups.\n- So all elements ≤ `max(nums1)` go to `nums1` or possibly `nums2`?\nWait: if an element is ≤ `max(nums1)`, it could still go to `nums2`, but then `min(nums2)` might decrease.\n\nBut we are taking `min(nums2)` — so putting a small number in `nums2` will reduce `min(nums2)`.\n\nSimilarly, putting a large number in `nums1` increases `max(nums1)`.\n\nSo to minimize `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)|`, we want:\n- `max(nums1)` to be as small as possible\n- `min(nums2)` to be as large as possible\nBut they have to \"meet\" somewhere.\n\nLet’s suppose we fix a split point in the **sorted array**: assign some elements to `nums1`, the rest to `nums2`.\n\nCan we do better than splitting at a contiguous point?\n\nSuppose we skip a small element and put it in `nums2`. Then `min(nums2)` becomes that small element, which could make `min(nums2)` very small, increasing the difference if `max(nums1)` is large.\n\nSimilarly, if we put a large element in `nums1`, `max(nums1)` increases.\n\nSo, **intuition**: to minimize the gap, we should avoid having a large `max(nums1)` and a small `min(nums2)`.\n\nSo best chance is to **split the sorted array at a single gap** — i.e., choose an index `i` from `0` to `n-2` (so both parts are non-empty), and assign:\n- `nums1 = sorted_nums[0..i]`\n- `nums2 = sorted_nums[i+1..n-1]`\n\nThen:\n- `max(nums1) = sorted_nums[i]`\n- `min(nums2) = sorted_nums[i+1]`\n- Value = `sorted_nums[i+1] - sorted_nums[i]`\n\nThen we minimize this over all valid `i`.\n\nBut is this always optimal?\n\nLet’s test with examples.\n\n---\n\n### Example 1: `nums = [1,3,2,4]`\n\nSorted: `[1,2,3,4]`\n\nTry splits:\n- i=0: nums1=[1], nums2=[2,3,4] → |1 - 2| = 1\n- i=1: nums1=[1,2], nums2=[3,4] → |2 - 3| = 1\n- i=2: nums1=[1,2,3], nums2=[4] → |3 - 4| = 1\n\nMinimum = 1 → matches.\n\nBut wait, is there a better non-contiguous split?\n\nTry: `nums1 = [1,3]`, `nums2 = [2,4]`\n- max(nums1) = 3\n- min(nums2) = 2\n- |3 - 2| = 1 → same\n\nOr `nums1 = [1,4]`, `nums2 = [2,3]` → |4 - 2| = 2 → worse.\n\nSo minimum is still 1.\n\nBut the **minimal difference between adjacent elements** in sorted array is 1 (e.g., 1 and 2, 2 and 3, 3 and 4), and that’s what we’re getting.\n\nNow Example 2: `nums = [100,1,10]`\n\nSorted: `[1,10,100]`\n\nSplits:\n- i=0: nums1=[1], nums2=[10,100] → |1 - 10| = 9\n- i=1: nums1=[1,10], nums2=[100] → |10 - 100| = 90\n\nMinimum = 9 → matches expected output.\n\nCan we do better with non-contiguous?\n\nTry: `nums1 = [10]`, `nums2 = [1,100]`\n- max(nums1)=10, min(nums2)=1 → |10 - 1| = 9\n\nTry: `nums1 = [1,100]`, `nums2 = [10]` → |100 - 10| = 90\n\nTry: `nums1 = [100]`, `nums2 = [1,10]` → |100 - 1| = 99\n\nSo minimum is 9.\n\nAgain, the minimal adjacent difference is `10 - 1 = 9`.\n\nSo in both cases, the answer is the **minimum difference between two consecutive elements in the sorted array**.\n\nWhy is that?\n\nBecause:\n- In any partition, every element is in one of the two groups.\n- Consider the **sorted** array: there must be some position where a `nums1` element and a `nums2` element are adjacent.\n- But more formally: suppose we look at the **largest element in nums1**, call it `x`, and the **smallest element in nums2**, call it `y`.\n- In the sorted array, all elements ≤ `x` are ≤ `x`, and all elements ≥ `y` are ≥ `y`.\n- But if `x < y`, then all elements in `nums1` are ≤ `x < y ≤` all elements in `nums2`, so the array is split between `x` and `y`.\n- So `x` must be some element, `y` is a larger element, and in the sorted array, there is a gap between the two groups.\n- The minimal such `y - x` occurs when `x` and `y` are **consecutive** in the sorted array.\n\nBut what if `x >= y`?\n\nThen `|x - y| = x - y`.\n\nBut that means some element in `nums1` (`x`) is ≥ some element in `nums2` (`y`).\n\nBut then, since `y` is in `nums2` and `y <= x`, and `x` is max of `nums1`, this is allowed.\n\nBut can this lead to a smaller absolute difference?\n\nLet’s suppose `x >= y`. Then `|x - y| = x - y`.\n\nBut now, note that `y` is in `nums2`, and `x` is in `nums1`, and `x >= y`.\n\nBut in the sorted array, `y` comes before `x` or at same value.\n\nBut can we get a smaller value than the minimal adjacent difference?\n\nLet’s suppose we have sorted array: `[a0, a1, ..., a_{n-1}]`\n\nSuppose in some partition, `max(nums1) = a_j`, `min(nums2) = a_i`, with `a_i <= a_j`.\n\nThen `|a_j - a_i| = a_j - a_i`.\n\nWe want to minimize this.\n\nBut note: since `a_i <= a_j`, and they are in sorted order, `i <= j`.\n\nThe difference `a_j - a_i` is at least the gap between some consecutive elements?\n\nBut actually, the **minimum possible** `a_j - a_i` over all `i <= j` such that `a_i` is in `nums2`, `a_j` is in `nums1`, and `a_i <= a_j`, could be zero if there are duplicates.\n\nBut even so, is it possible to get smaller than the minimal adjacent difference?\n\nWait — suppose all adjacent differences are ≥ d. Then any two distinct elements differ by at least d? No — not if non-consecutive.\n\nBut for `a_j - a_i` with `i <= j`, it's minimized when `i` and `j` are close.\n\nBut here's the key: **in any partition, there must be some consecutive pair in the sorted array where the two elements are in different groups**.\n\nBecause the first element is in one group, the last in some group, and as we go from left to right, at some point the group assignment must switch (since both groups are non-empty).\n\nSo let’s formalize:\n\nLet the sorted array be `a[0..n-1]`.\n\nLet’s define an assignment: each `a[i]` is assigned to `nums1` or `nums2`.\n\nSince both groups are non-empty, there exists some index `i` such that:\n- `a[i]` is in one group\n- `a[i+1]` is in the other group\n\nThis is because the assignment vector changes at some point.\n\nSo consider the **first** index `i` where the group changes.\n\nThen `a[i]` and `a[i+1]` are in different groups.\n\nNow, two cases:\n\n1. `a[i]` in `nums1`, `a[i+1]` in `nums2`\n   - Then `max(nums1) >= a[i]`\n   - `min(nums2) <= a[i+1]`\n   - But we need `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)|`\n   - But this could be large.\n\nBut we want to **lower bound** the value.\n\nWait — actually, we are trying to find the **minimum possible** value over all partitions.\n\nBut from the existence of such a split between `a[i]` and `a[i+1]`, can we relate it to the answer?\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nSuppose we consider **all possible consecutive splits** in the sorted array: assign first `i+1` to `nums1`, rest to `nums2`. Then value is `a[i+1] - a[i]`.\n\nWe know that the **minimal such difference** is a candidate.\n\nBut is it the best possible?\n\nSuppose there exists a partition where `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)| < min_i (a[i+1] - a[i])`.\n\nLet `d_min = min_i (a[i+1] - a[i])`.\n\nSuppose `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)| < d_min`.\n\nLet `M1 = max(nums1)`, `m2 = min(nums2)`.\n\nThen `|M1 - m2| < d_min`.\n\nNow, in the sorted array, consider where `M1` and `m2` lie.\n\nCase 1: `M1 < m2`\nThen `m2 - M1 < d_min`\nBut `M1` and `m2` are two elements in the array, with `M1 < m2`, so there must be a sequence of consecutive elements from `M1` to `m2`, and the total gap `m2 - M1` is at least the number of steps times min gap, but more simply: since all consecutive gaps are ≥ `d_min`, and `M1` and `m2` are at least one step apart, `m2 - M1 >= d_min`, contradiction.\n\nCase 2: `M1 >= m2`\nThen `M1 - m2 < d_min`\nBut `M1` and `m2` are elements, `M1 >= m2`, so `M1 - m2 >= 0`.\nBut again, if `M1 > m2`, then since they are distinct elements, and sorted, `M1` comes after `m2` in sorted order, and the gap between them is at least the minimal consecutive gap? Not necessarily — e.g., `[1,3,5]`, `d_min=2`, `5-1=4>2`, but `3-1=2`.\n\nBut if `M1 - m2 < d_min`, and `M1 > m2`, then `M1` and `m2` are not equal, so there is at least one gap between them, but the total difference could be small if they are close.\n\nBut wait — the **minimal consecutive gap** is `d_min`, but two elements can be close even if intermediate gaps are large? No — in a sorted array, if `m2 = a[i]`, `M1 = a[j]`, `j > i`, then `a[j] - a[i] >= (j-i) * min_gap`? No, not unless gaps are uniform.\n\nBut actually: `a[j] - a[i] = sum_{k=i}^{j-1} (a[k+1] - a[k]) >= (j-i) * d_min >= d_min` if `j > i`.\n\nSo if `M1 > m2`, then `M1 - m2 >= d_min`.\n\nSimilarly, if `M1 < m2`, then `m2 - M1 >= d_min`.\n\nSo in **both cases**, if `M1 ≠ m2`, then `|M1 - m2| >= d_min`.\n\nAnd if `M1 = m2`, then `|M1 - m2| = 0`, which is less than `d_min` — but is that possible?\n\nOnly if there is a value that appears in both groups? But `M1` is from `nums1`, `m2` from `nums2`, so if the same value `x` is in both groups, then we can have `max(nums1) = x`, `min(nums2) = x`, so difference 0.\n\nBut is that allowed?\n\nYes! For example, `nums = [2,2]`\n\nThen we can do:\n- `nums1 = [2]`, `nums2 = [2]`\n- `max(nums1)=2`, `min(nums2)=2`, `|2-2|=0`\n\nAnd the consecutive difference is `2-2=0`, so `d_min=0`.\n\nSo in that case, `d_min=0`, and answer is 0.\n\nSo in general, the **minimal difference between consecutive elements** in the sorted array is exactly the minimal possible `|max(nums1) - min(nums2)|`.\n\nBecause:\n- We can always achieve `a[i+1] - a[i]` by splitting at `i`.\n- And we cannot do better than the minimal such gap, because in any partition, either:\n   - `max(nums1) >= min(nums2)` → then `max(nums1) - min(nums2) >= d_min` (if they are different), or\n   - `max(nums1) < min(nums2)` → then `min(nums2) - max(nums1) >= d_min`\n   - Unless they are equal, but that requires a repeated value, and then consecutive difference can be 0.\n\nBut wait — if there are duplicates, consecutive difference can be 0, and we can achieve 0.\n\nSo in all cases, the **minimal value of |max(nums1) - min(nums2)|** is equal to the **minimum difference between two consecutive elements in the sorted array**.\n\nBut wait — is that true?\n\nLet’s test with `nums = [1, 3, 5]`\n- Consecutive differences: 2, 2 → min = 2\n- Can we get less than 2?\n\nTry splits:\n- Split at 0: [1] and [3,5] → |1-3|=2\n- Split at 1: [1,3] and [5] → |3-5|=2\n\nAny other partition