      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumJumps(nums, target):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array nums and an integer target, this function finds the maximum \n    number of jumps to reach the last index from the first index, where a jump\n    from i to j is valid if 0 <= i < j < n and -target <= nums[j] - nums[i] <= target.\n    \n    Args:\n    nums (list[int]): A 0-indexed array of integers.\n    target (int): The maximum allowed difference between nums[j] and nums[i].\n    \n    Returns:\n    int: The maximum number of jumps or -1 if it's impossible to reach the end.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum number of jumps to reach index i from index 0.\n    # Initialize with -1 to signify that the index is currently unreachable.\n    dp = [-1] * n\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    # Iterate through each index i\n    for i in range(n):\n        # If the current index i is unreachable, we cannot jump from it.\n        if dp[i] == -1:\n            continue\n            \n        # Try to jump to every possible subsequent index j\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            # Check the jump condition: -target <= nums[j] - nums[i] <= target\n            if abs(nums[j] - nums[i]) <= target:\n                # Update the maximum number of jumps to reach index j\n                dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[i] + 1)\n                \n    # Return the maximum number of jumps to reach the final index n-1\n    return dp[n - 1]\n```",
