    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Bitmap graphics, also known as raster graphics, represent images as a grid of pixels, allowing for rich color detail (millions of colors), realistic shading, transparency, texture filling, and efficient pixel block operations via hardware. They also support layered editing in software, and polygons or shapes can be filled with colors or textures. However, because bitmap images are resolution-dependent, straight lines may appear jagged (due to pixelation) and cannot always be displayed perfectly straight—especially at low resolutions—unlike vector graphics, which guarantee mathematically straight lines.\n\nThus, the statement that is NOT a property of bitmap graphics is: \"All line segments can be displayed as straight.\"\n\n\\boxed{F}",
