    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the validity of the argument, we can analyze the premises:\n1. $E \\supset F$\n2. $G \\supset \\sim F$\n\nFrom premise 2, using the rule of transposition (contraposition), we get $\\sim (\\sim F) \\supset \\sim G$, which simplifies to $F \\supset \\sim G$. We can now create a chain of implication with premise 1 ($E \\supset F$):\n$E \\supset F$ and $F \\supset \\sim G$ implies $E \\supset \\sim G$ (Hypothetical Syllogism).\nThe conditional $E \\supset \\sim G$ is logically equivalent to $\\sim E \\lor \\sim G$, which is the same as the conclusion $\\sim G \\lor \\sim E$. Since the premises logically necessitate the conclusion, the argument is valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
