      "generated_answer": "Hart's distinction between 'being obliged' and 'having an obligation' is central to his analysis of legal rules in *The Concept of Law*. He makes this distinction to clarify that being coerced (being obliged) is not the same as having a genuine obligation under a rule system, which involves the existence of a rule that confers duties. This distinction primarily serves to illuminate the concept of a rule, particularly how rules create normative reasons for action beyond mere fear of sanction.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
