    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The formal sources of international law are outlined in Article 38 of the Statute of the International Court of Justice (ICJ), which lists treaties, custom, and general principles of law as primary sources, with judicial decisions and scholarly writings as subsidiary means. There is no explicit hierarchy among treaties, custom, and general principles in the statute, and international law generally treats them as complementary rather than hierarchical. While treaties may override prior custom between the parties (per the *lex specialis* or *lex posterior* principles), customary international law can override treaties in cases of *jus cogens* norms, indicating no fixed hierarchy.\n\nThus, the most accurate answer is that there is no rigid hierarchy among the formal sources.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
