    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the sentence step by step for accurate translation:\n\n1. \"After that\" – This refers to a time sequence. In Gujarati, this can be translated as \"પછી\" (pachi).\n\n2. \"you should offer\" – The verb \"to offer\" in the context of a religious offering is often translated as \"ચઢાવવું\" (chadavavuṁ) or \"અર્પણ કરવું\" (arpaṇ karavuṁ). Since this is a suggestion or religious instruction, the modal \"should\" implies duty or recommendation. In Gujarati, this can be expressed using the appropriate verb form. \"તમારે\" (tamāre) means \"you should\", and with the verb, we can use \"ચઢાવવાનાં\" (chadavavānāṁ) in plural/respectful form.\n\n3. \"yellow coloured sweets\" – \"Sweets\" in Gujarati is \"મીઠાઈ\" (mīṭhāī), and \"yellow coloured\" is \"પીળા રંગની\" (pīḷā raṅganī). So, \"પીળા રંગની મીઠાઈ\" (pīḷā raṅganī mīṭhāī).\n\n4. \"to Lord Vishnu\" – \"Lord Vishnu\" is \"ભગવાન વિષ્ણુ\" (bhagavān viṣṇu), and \"to\" in the sense of offering is expressed with the dative case, which in Gujarati uses the postposition \"ને\" (ne). So, \"ભગવાન વિષ્ણુને\" (bhagavān viṣṇune).\n\nNow, putting it all together:\n\n\"પછી તમારે ભગવાન વિષ્ણુને પીળા રંગની મીઠાઈ ચઢાવવાની છે.\"\n\nNote: \"ચઢાવવાની છે\" is a common way to say \"should offer\" in a prescribed or customary sense.\n\nFinal translation:\n\n\\boxed{\\text{પછી તમારે ભગવાન વિષ્ણુને પીળા રંગની મીઠાઈ ચઢાવવાની છે.}}",
