    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To compute the product $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1)$, we denote the roots of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 + x + 1$ as $\\alpha_1, \\alpha_2, \\alpha_3$, such that $f(x) = (x - \\alpha_1)(x - \\alpha_2)(x - \\alpha_3)$. Then, $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 \\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\omega^k - \\alpha_j) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^k - \\alpha_j) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 (1 - \\alpha_j^7)$, where we used the identity $\\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^k - \\alpha) = -(\\alpha^7 - 1)$. Using the relation $\\alpha_j^3 = -\\alpha_j - 1$, we find $\\alpha_j^7 = \\alpha_j(2\\alpha_j^2 - 1) = 2\\alpha_j^3 + \\alpha_j^2 = 2(-\\alpha_j - 1) + \\alpha_j^2 = \\alpha_j^2 - 2\\alpha_j - 2$; substituting this, we get $1 - \\alpha_j^7 = 3 + 2\\alpha_j - \\alpha_j^2$. However, using the property $1 - \\alpha_j^7 = -( \\alpha_j^7 - 1)$, note that for $x^3+x+1=0$, $\\alpha_j^3 = -\\alpha_j-1$, so $\\alpha_j^7 = 2\\alpha_j^2-1$, making $1-\\alpha_j^7 = 2 - 2\\alpha_j^2 = 2(1-\\alpha_j)(1+\\alpha_j)$. The product is then $2^3 \\cdot \\prod_{j=1}^3 (1-\\alpha_j) \\cdot \\prod_{j=1}^3 (1+\\alpha_j) = 8 \\cdot f(1) \\cdot (-f(-1)) = 8 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 = 24$.\n\n\\boxed{24}",
