this product is equal to the resultant of $x^7 - 1$ and $x^3 + x + 1$, but we can use a better trick.\n\nLet us consider the polynomial $h(x) = x^3 + x + 1$. We want $\\prod_{j=0}^6 h(\\omega^j)$.\n\nThere is a known identity: for any polynomial $h(x)$, \n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} h(\\omega^k) = \\exp\\left( \\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \\log h(\\omega^k) \\right),\n$$\nbut that’s not helpful directly.\n\nInstead, use the fact that:\n$$\n\\prod_{\\zeta^n = 1} h(\\zeta) = \\prod_{d \\mid n} \\Phi_d(x) \\text{ evaluated in some way?}\n$$\nBetter: the product $\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} h(\\omega^k)$ is equal to the resultant $\\mathrm{Res}(x^n - 1, h(x))$, which is also equal to the product of $h(\\zeta)$ over all $n$th roots of unity.\n\nThere is a computational method: if $h(x) = a_m x^m + \\cdots + a_0$, then $\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} h(\\omega^k) = \\prod_{j=1}^m \\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} (\\alpha_j - \\omega^k)$, where $\\alpha_j$ are roots of $h$, but that might work.\n\nLet $h(x) = x^3 + x + 1$, and let $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ be its roots. Then\n$$\nh(x) = (x - \\alpha)(x - \\beta)(x - \\gamma),\n$$\nso\n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^6 h(\\omega^k) = \\prod_{k=0}^6 \\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\omega^k - \\alpha_j) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^k - \\alpha_j).\n$$\nNow, $\\prod_{k=0}^6 (x - \\omega^k) = x^7 - 1$, so $\\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^k - \\alpha_j) = \\prod_{k=0}^6 -(\\alpha_j - \\omega^k) = (-1)^7 \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\alpha_j - \\omega^k) = -(\\alpha_j^7 - 1)$, because $\\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\alpha_j - \\omega^k) = \\alpha_j^7 - 1$ (since the $\\omega^k$ are roots of $x^7 - 1 = 0$).\n\nThus,\n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^6 h(\\omega^k) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 \\left( -(\\alpha_j^7 - 1) \\right) = (-1)^3 \\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\alpha_j^7 - 1) = - \\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\alpha_j^7 - 1).\n$$\n\nNow, $\\alpha_j$ are roots of $x^3 + x + 1 = 0$. We need to compute $\\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\alpha_j^7 - 1)$.\n\nThis seems hard, but note that $\\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\alpha_j^7 - 1) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 \\alpha_j^7 - \\text{some symmetric functions}$, but better to write:\n$$\n\\prod_{j=1}^3 (\\alpha_j^7 - 1) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 \\alpha_j^7 \\cdot \\prod_{j=1}^3 (1 - \\alpha_j^{-7}) = \\left( \\prod \\alpha_j \\right)^7 \\cdot \\prod (1 - \\alpha_j^{-7}).\n$$\n\nFrom $h(x) = x^3 + 0x^2 + x + 1$, so sum $\\alpha_j = 0$, sum $\\alpha_i\\alpha_j = 1$, product $\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_3 = -1$ (since constant term is $-(-1)^3 \\times \\text{product} = 1 \\Rightarrow \\text{product} = -1$).\n\nSo $\\prod \\alpha_j = -1$, so $(\\prod \\alpha_j)^7 = (-1)^7 = -1$.\n\nThus,\n$$\n\\prod (\\alpha_j^7 - 1) = -1 \\cdot \\prod (1 - \\alpha_j^{-7}).\n$$\n\nBut $\\alpha_j^{-1}$ are roots of the reciprocal polynomial: since $h(x) = x^3 + x + 1$, the reciprocal is $x^3 h(1/x) = x^3 (x^{-3} + x^{-1} + 1) = 1 + x^2 + x^3$, so $x^3 + x^2 + 1$, whose roots are $\\alpha_j^{-1}$.\n\nSo $\\prod (1 - \\alpha_j^{-7}) = \\prod_{j} (1 - \\beta_j^7)$ where $\\beta_j = \\alpha_j^{-1}$ are roots of $x^3 + x^2 + 1 = 0$.\n\nBut this seems messy.\n\nAlternative idea: compute numerically?\n\nLet $\\omega = e^{2\\pi i /7}$. Try $k=0$: $\\omega^0 = 1$, so term is $1^3 + 1 + 1 = 3$.\n\nFor $k=1$: $\\omega^3 + \\omega + 1$\n\n$k=2$: $\\omega^6 + \\omega^2 + 1$\n\n$k=3$: $\\omega^{9} + \\omega^3 + 1 = \\omega^2 + \\omega^3 + 1$ (since $\\omega^7=1$, $\\omega^9 = \\omega^2$)\n\n$k=4$: $\\omega^{12} + \\omega^4 + 1 = \\omega^5 + \\omega^4 + 1$\n\n$k=5$: $\\omega^{15} + \\omega^5 + 1 = \\omega^{15 \\mod 7=1} + \\omega^5 + 1 = \\omega + \\omega^5 + 1$\n\n$k=6$: $\\omega^{18} + \\omega^6 + 1 = \\omega^{4} + \\omega^6 + 1$ (since 18 mod 7 = 4)\n\nSo the terms are:\n- k=0: $1 + 1 + 1 = 3$\n- k=1: $\\omega^3 + \\omega + 1$\n- k=2: $\\omega^6 + \\omega^2 + 1$\n- k=3: $\\omega^2 + \\omega^3 + 1$\n- k=4: $\\omega^5 + \\omega^4 + 1$\n- k=5: $\\omega + \\omega^5 + 1$\n- k=6: $\\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + 1$\n\nNote that the terms come in pairs:\n- k=1 and k=3: $\\omega^3 + \\omega + 1$ and $\\omega^3 + \\omega^2 + 1$ — not the same\nBut observe that the set of exponents is symmetric