2 = \\alpha + 1 - \\alpha^2$\n- $\\alpha^6 = \\alpha \\cdot \\alpha^5 = \\alpha(\\alpha + 1 - \\alpha^2) = \\alpha^2 + \\alpha - \\alpha^3 = \\alpha^2 + \\alpha - (- \\alpha - 1) = \\alpha^2 + \\alpha + \\alpha + 1 = \\alpha^2 + 2\\alpha + 1$\n- $\\alpha^7 = \\alpha \\cdot \\alpha^6 = \\alpha(\\alpha^2 + 2\\alpha + 1) = \\alpha^3 + 2\\alpha^2 + \\alpha = (- \\alpha - 1) + 2\\alpha^2 + \\alpha = 2\\alpha^2 - 1$\n\nSo $\\alpha^7 = 2\\alpha^2 - 1$\n\nTherefore, $1 - \\alpha^7 = 1 - (2\\alpha^2 - 1) = 2 - 2\\alpha^2 = 2(1 - \\alpha^2)$\n\nSimilarly for other roots.\n\nSo for each root $\\alpha_j$, $1 - \\alpha_j^7 = 2(1 - \\alpha_j^2)$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\prod_{j=1}^3 (1 - \\alpha_j^7) = \\prod_{j=1}^3 2(1 - \\alpha_j^2) = 8 \\prod_{j=1}^3 (1 - \\alpha_j^2)\n$$\n\nNow, $\\prod_{j=1}^3 (1 - \\alpha_j^2) = \\prod_{j} (1 - \\alpha_j^2)$\n\nLet $u = \\alpha_j^2$, but better to consider the polynomial whose value is this product.\n\nNote that $\\prod_{j} (x - \\alpha_j^2)$ is the polynomial whose roots are $\\alpha_j^2$, which can be found by composition.\n\nSince the $\\alpha_j$ are roots of $p(x) = x^3 + x + 1$, then the polynomial with roots $\\alpha_j^2$ is the resultant of $p(y)$ and $x - y^2$ with respect to $y$.\n\nThat is, $\\mathrm{Res}_y (y^3 + y + 1, x - y^2) = \\mathrm{Res}_y (y^3 + y + 1, y^2 - x)$\n\nCompute this resultant.\n\nLet $f(y) = y^3 + 0y^2 + y + 1$, $g(y) = y^2 - x$\n\nResultant of cubic and quadratic.\n\nWe can use the Sylvester matrix.\n\nFor $\\deg f=3$, $\\deg g=2$, Sylvester matrix is 5x5? No, for res(f,g), with deg f=m, deg g=n, it's (m+n) x (m+n), but here m=3, n=2, so 5x5, but we can use polynomial division.\n\nDivide $f(y) = y^3 + y + 1$ by $g(y) = y^2 - x$:\n\n$y^3 + 0y^2 + y + 1 = y(y^2 - x) + x y + y + 1 = y(y^2 - x) + y(x + 1) + 1$\n\nBetter:\n$y^3 + y + 1 = y \\cdot (y^2 - x) + x y + y + 1 = y(y^2 - x) + y(x + 1) + 1$\n\nNow, divide $y(x+1) + 1$ by $y^2 - x$: since degree lower, remainder is $ (x+1)y + 1 $, and the quotient is y.\n\nSo 