ows that the **only** failures occur when we can replace **two 25s (50¢)** with **five 10s (50¢)** — same value, but 5 vs 2 coins? No, that’s worse. Wait: two 25s = 2 coins; five 10s = 5 coins — worse. So that doesn't help.\n\nWait — perhaps the reverse: use more 10s to avoid many 1s.\n\nThe real issue is when the remainder after using 25s is small but requires many 1s. For example, $ N = 30 $: greedy: 25 + 5×1 = 6 coins; but 3×10 = 3 coins — better. So greedy fails at $ N = 30 $.\n\nSimilarly, $ N = 40 $: greedy: 25 + 10 + 5×1 = 7 coins; but 4×10 = 4 coins — better.\n\nSo pattern: when $ N \\mod 25 = r $, and $ r $ is between 10 and 24, greedy uses one 10 and some 1s, but maybe multiple 10s would be better.\n\nWait: for $ N = 30 $: 30 ≥ 25 → take 25, remainder 5 → five 1s → total 6 coins. But 3×10 = 3 coins → better.\n\nSo failure occurs when $ N $ is divisible by 10, but $ N \\mod 25 \\in \\{5,6,\\dots,9,10,\\dots,24\\} $? Not quite.\n\nLet’s generalize.\n\nLet $ g(N) $ be the number of coins used by greedy, and $ m(N) $ the minimal.\n\nGreedy: use as many 25s as possible: $ q = \\lfloor N/25 \\rfloor $, remainder $ r = N \\mod 25 $. Then use $ \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor $ 10s, then 1s. So:\n\n$ g(N) = q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) $\n\nBut optimal might use fewer 25s and more 10s.\n\nSuppose we use $ k $ fewer 25s (i.e., $ q - k $ 25s), then we have $ N - 25(q - k) = 25k + r $ cents to make with 10s and 1s.\n\nMinimal coins for amount $ A $ with 10s and 1s is $ \\lfloor A/10 \\rfloor + (A \\mod 10) $.\n\nSo total coins for this alternative: $ (q - k) + \\lfloor (25k + r)/10 \\rfloor + ((25k + r) \\mod 10) $\n\nWe want to know if for some $ k \\geq 1 $, this is less than $ g(N) = q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) $\n\nSo define:\n\n$ \\Delta_k = g(N) - \\left[ (q - k) + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25k + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor + (25k + r \\mod 10) \\right] $\n\nWe want to know if $ \\Delta_k > 0 $ for some $ k $, meaning greedy is not optimal.\n\nSo:\n\n$ \\Delta_k = q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) - (q - k) - \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25k + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor - ((25k + r) \\mod 10) $\n\nSimplify:\n\n$ \\Delta_k = k + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) - \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25k + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor - ((25k + r) \\mod 10) $\n\nBut $ \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) = r - 9 \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor $? No.\n\nActually, $ \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) $ is not $ r $, it's $ \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + r - 10\\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor = r - 9\\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor $ — not helpful.\n\nLet $ a = \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor $, $ b = r \\mod 10 $, so $ r = 10a + b $, $ a \\in \\{0,1,2\\} $, $ b \\in \\{0,\\dots,9\\} $\n\nThen $ g(N) = q + a + b $\n\nNow $ 25k + r = 25k + 10a + b $\n\n$ \\lfloor (25k + 10a + b)/10 \\rfloor = \\lfloor 2.5k + a + b/10 \\rfloor = 2k + a + \\lfloor (5k + b)/10 \\rfloor $? Wait:\n\n$ 25k /10 = 2.5k $, so $ \\lfloor 25k + 10a + b \\over 10 \\rfloor = \\lfloor 2.5k \\rfloor + a + \\lfloor (5k + b)/10 \\rfloor $? No, not linear.\n\nBetter: $ 25k + 10a + b = 10(2k + a) + 5k + b $, so:\n\n$ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25k + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor = 2k + a + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{5k + b}{10} \\right\\rfloor $\n\nAnd $ (25k + r) \\mod 10 = (5k + b) \\mod 10 $\n\nSo total alternative coins: $ q - k + 2k + a + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{5k + b}{10} \\right\\rfloor + ((5k + b) \\mod 10) = q + k + a + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{5k + b}{10} \\right\\rfloor + ((5k + b) \\mod 10) $\n\nBut $ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{5k + b}{10} \\right\\rfloor + ((5k + b) \\mod 10) $ is just the number of coins to make $ 5k + b $ cents with 10s and 1s, which is $ \\lfloor (5k + b)/10 \\rfloor + (5k + b \\mod 10) $\n\nLet $ c = 5k + b $. Then that sum is $ \\lfloor c/10 \\rfloor + (c \\mod 10) $\n\nSo total alternative coins: $ q + k + a + \\lfloor c/10 \\rfloor + (c \\mod 10) $\n\nBut $ g(N) = q + a + b $\n\nSo difference:\n\n$ g(N) - \\text{alt} = (q + a + b) - (q + k + a + \\lfloor c/10 \\rfloor + (c \\mod 10)) = b - k - \\left