      "generated_answer": "To find the positive integers $n$ such that $n + 2$ divides $3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9)$, we note that $n+3 = (n+2) + 1$ and $n^2+9 = (n^2-4) + 13 = (n-2)(n+2) + 13$. Substituting these into the expression, we get $3[(n+2) + 1][(n-2)(n+2) + 13] = 3[(n+2)^2(n-2) + 13(n+2) + (n-2)(n+2) + 13]$, which simplifies to $3(n+2)(\\dots) + 39$. Thus, $n+2$ must be a divisor of $39$, which are $\\{1, 3, 13, 39\\}$. Since $n$ is a positive integer, $n+2 \\ge 3$, yielding the possible values $n+2 \\in \\{3, 13, 39\\}$, corresponding to $n \\in \\{1, 11, 37\\}$. The sum of these values is $1 + 11 + 37 = 49$.\n\n\\boxed{49}",
