      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ n + 2 $ divides $ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) $, and we are to find the sum of all positive integers $ n $ satisfying this condition.\n\nLet $ d = n + 2 $, so $ n = d - 2 $. Substitute into the expression:\n$$\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n$$\nWe want $ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $, $ d $ must divide $ 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. But $ d \\mid d^2 $ and $ d \\mid 4d $, so $ d \\mid (d^2 - 4d + 13) $ only if $ d \\mid 13 $. Thus $ d \\mid 3 \\cdot (d^2 - 4d + 13) $ implies $ d \\mid 3 \\cdot 13 = 39 $, because $ d^2 - 4d + 13 \\equiv 13 \\pmod{d} $. So $ d \\mid 39 $.\n\nThe positive divisors of 39 are $ 1, 3, 13, 39 $. Since $ n = d - 2 $ and $ n > 0 $, we require $ d > 2 $. So valid $ d $ are $ 3, 13, 39 $, giving $ n = 1, 11, 37 $.\n\nCheck each:\n- $ n = 1 $: $ n+2 = 3 $, product = $ 3(4)(1 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 10 = 120 $, $ 120 \\div 3 = 40 $: OK.\n- $ n = 11 $: $ n+2 = 13 $, product = $ 3(14)(121 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 130 = 5460 $, $ 5460 \\div 13 = 420 $: OK.\n- $ n = 37 $: $ n+2 = 39 $, product = $ 3(40)(1369 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 40 \\cdot 1378 = 165360 $, $ 165360 \\div 39 = 4240 $: OK.\n\nAll valid. Sum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $.\n\n\\boxed{{49}}",
