    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The ellipse can be written as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1,\\qquad \na^{2}= \\frac{1}{k},\\; b^{2}=1\\;(k>1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b>a\\), the major axis is vertical and the foci are  \n\\[\nF_{1}=(0,c),\\;F_{2}=(0,-c),\\qquad \nc^{2}=b^{2}-a^{2}=1-\\frac{1}{k}= \\frac{k-1}{k}.\n\\]\n\nA circle that passes through both foci must be symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis; its centre must be at the origin, giving radius \\(R=c\\).  \nFor the circle to be tangent to the ellipse at the two points where the ellipse meets the \\(x\\)-axis, those points \\((\\pm a,0)\\) must lie on the circle, i.e. \\(a=R\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\na^{2}=c^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\frac{1}{k}= \\frac{k-1}{k}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k=2.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
