      "generated_answer": "The key to understanding why electron microscopes achieve much higher magnifications than light microscopes lies in resolution, which is the ability to distinguish two close points as separate. Resolution is fundamentally limited by the wavelength of the radiation used: electrons have much shorter wavelengths than visible light, allowing electron microscopes to resolve much finer details. This enables electron microscopes to achieve much higher effective magnifications without the image becoming blurry, unlike light microscopes which are limited by the longer wavelength of light.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}",
