      "generated_answer": "The observation that mouse and human cell-surface proteins mix in the plasma membrane of a fused cell indicates that the experiment involved the physical merging of two cells, allowing their membranes to combine. This process is achieved through cell fusion, which results in a hybrid cell (also known as a heterokaryon if nuclei remain separate, or a synkaryon if they fuse). The technique used to produce such a cell is most accurately described as cell fusion.\n\nWhile option D (\"Producing a heterokaryon\") is related, option G (\"Using a method called cell fusion to combine the mouse and human cells\") most directly describes the technique employed. The classic experiment referenced (such as the Frye-Edidin experiment) used cell fusion to demonstrate membrane protein mobility.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
