    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The expansion of $(m+n)^6$ is given by $\\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$. Since $m$ and $n$ are odd, $m^{6-k} n^k$ is always odd, meaning the parity of each term depends entirely on whether the binomial coefficient $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd. According to Lucas's Theorem, $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd if and only if the binary representation of $k$ is a submask of the binary representation of 6 ($6 = 110_2$), which occurs for $k \\in \\{0, 2, 4, 6\\}$. There are exactly 4 such values, so there are 4 odd terms.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
