    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The volume of a cube with edge length $s$ is $V = s^3$. To construct a cube with volume $X$ times the volume of a given cube (with edge length $s_0$), we must construct an edge length $s = s_0 \\sqrt[3]{X}$. According to the Wantzel's theorem, $\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is constructible via compass and straightedge if and only if $\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is a rational number, which occurs if and only if $X$ is a perfect cube. Evaluating the given set $\\{3, 17, 8, 27, 343, 1331\\}$ for perfect cubes, we find that 8 ($2^3$), 27 ($3^3$), 343 ($7^3$), and 1331 ($11^3$) are perfect cubes, while 3 and 17 are not. Thus, the construction is possible for $\\{8, 27, 343, 1331\\}$ (marked as 1) and impossible for $\\{3, 17\\}$ (marked as 0), resulting in the list $[0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
