    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given a linear homogeneous recurrence relation:  \n$ y[n] - 3y[n-1] + 2y[n-2] = 0 $ for $ n \\geq 2 $, with initial conditions $ y[0] = 2 $, $ y[1] = 3 $.  \n\nWe compute the next few terms using the recurrence:  \n- For $ n = 2 $: $ y[2] = 3y[1] - 2y[0] = 3(3) - 2(2) = 9 - 4 = 5 $  \n- For $ n = 3 $: $ y[3] = 3y[2] - 2y[1] = 3(5) - 2(3) = 15 - 6 = 9 $  \n- For $ n = 4 $: $ y[4] = 3y[3] - 2y[2] = 3(9) - 2(5) = 27 - 10 = 17 $  \n\nSo the sequence for $ n \\geq 2 $ is $ y[2] = 5, y[3] = 9, y[4] = 17, \\dots $, which matches option B.  \n\n\\boxed{B}",
