      "generated_answer": "The energy stored in an inductor is $ W = \\int_0^1 i(t) \\, d\\phi $, and since $ \\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3(t) $, then $ d\\phi = i^2(t) \\, di $. Substituting $ i(t) = 2\\sin t $, we compute $ W = \\int_0^1 i^3 \\, di = \\int_0^{2\\sin 1} i^3 \\, di $, but more directly, using $ W = \\int_0^1 \\phi(i) \\frac{di}{dt} dt $, we find $ W = \\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{3}(2\\sin t)^3 \\cdot 2\\cos t \\, dt $, which evaluates to approximately 1.00 J.  \n$\\boxed{\\text{A}}$",
