    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Blood components, particularly red blood cells, must be kept within a specific temperature range (typically 1–6°C) to prevent bacterial growth and maintain viability. If a blood unit has been out of refrigeration beyond the acceptable time limit (usually 30 minutes to 1 hour, depending on local protocols), it is considered unsafe for transfusion and must not be used. The proper procedure is to return it to the blood bank so they can document the issue and ensure appropriate disposal or quarantine.\n\nTherefore, the correct action is to return the unit and inform the blood bank of the temperature excursion.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
