    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which option matches the following scale?\n\nOptions: \nA. Phrygian mode with D as the II degree\nB. Phrygian mode with ♯F as the VI degree\nC. Dorian mode with Si as the leading tone\nD. Locrian mode with Sol as the dominant tone\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each option step by step.\n\nOption A: Phrygian mode with D as the II degree. In Phrygian mode, the second degree is a minor second above the tonic. So if D is the II, the tonic must be C. The Phrygian mode on C would be: C, Db, Eb, F, G, A, Bb, C. This is valid.\n\nOption B: Phrygian mode with ♯F as the VI degree. The sixth degree in Phrygian is a minor sixth above the tonic. So if ♯F is the sixth, the tonic is B (since B to ♯F is a minor sixth). B Phrygian is: B, C, D, E, F#, G, A, B — but the sixth degree is G, not ♯F. So this is incorrect.\n\nOption C: Dorian mode with Si (B) as the leading tone. The leading tone is the major seventh above the tonic. Dorian has a major sixth and minor seventh, so the seventh is a whole step below the tonic — not a leading tone (which is a half step). So B cannot be the leading tone in Dorian. Invalid.\n\nOption D: Locrian mode with Sol (G) as the dominant (V degree). In Locrian, the fifth degree is diminished, so the dominant is not a perfect fifth. If G is the fifth, the tonic is C. C Locrian: C, Db, Eb, F, Gb, Ab, Bb, C — but here the fifth is Gb, not G. So G cannot be the dominant. Invalid.\n\nOnly Option A is consistent.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
