      "generated_answer": "Jean Dauberval is recognized for elevating **turned-in positions** (or \"turned-in\" legs), which were traditionally considered \"false\" or \"grotesque\" in contrast to the \"noble\" *en dehors* (turned-out) style of ballet. By incorporating these inward-rotating movements into his choreography, most notably in *La Fille mal gardée*, he challenged the rigid aesthetic hierarchy that reserved such techniques solely for comedic or low-class characters. This shift toward naturalism helped bridge the gap between courtly ballet and character-driven performance.\n\n\\boxed{turned-in positions}",
